      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Dr. Williams' 1 'ink Pills.</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' 1 'ink Pills.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>A Great Charity Fate.—The Queen's Fate at the. Mansion House in aid of the Lord Mayor's Cripples' Fund, will rank ae one of the most fashionable Society bazaars and sales of recent years in London. Such functions are a feature of every season, but on this occasion the Queen, the Royal Princesses, and the leaders of Society have done all, that was possible to make the fête a great success. The principal rooms at the Mansion House were tastefully got up for the occa- sion, two features of interest being the model of Temple Bar, and the exhibition of old gold and silver plate. Nothing is omitted in the decorations and entertain- ments to please and amuse the visitor, and enlist his or her practical sompathies on behalf of thefund. Princess Christian, Princess Henry of Battenberg,the Duchess of Albany, and Princess Alexander of Tech, all offer valuable tervices in presiding over the Princess's stalls and a great show is made by the United States Embassy stall, practically all the leading American women in London Society assisting in its organisation. Raffles at Charity Bazaars.—Prize drawings are a very ropular feature at church and charity bazaars, but unfortunately the proposed " tombola " or raffle at the Queen's Fate had to be abandoned as being illegal. For this event, a thousand balf-guinea tickets were sold,the prizes ranging from a leg of mutton to a ton of coals, and a grand piano. It was supposed that there being all prizes and no blanks, nothing could be said against the tombola " but evidently the law on such matters is not understood, even at the official residence of the Chief Magistrate, of the City of London. Prize draw- ings at church and charity bazaars, it would seem, are only permissible .when the tickets are sold at the time of the drawing. If sold biforetaand, they-are illegal, but in the Mansion House case, the Cripples' Fund did not suffer much, as nearly all who had bought tickets were content to forego the prizes. The Fate of Crosby Hall.—Few of the old buildings in the City of London are so rich in historic associa- tions as Crosby Hall, which is of interest to visitors to to the metropolis on that account. Built by Sir John Crosby, grocer and woolstapler, about the year 1470, it was for the most part the home of merchant princes of the city, many of whom were Lord Mayors of London, and had their official residence there. In 1594 it belonged to Sir John Spencer, Lord Mayor of that year, and it is to the marriage of his daughter and sole heiress, to the Lord Compton of that time, that the Marquis of Northampton owes much much of his immense wealth, derived from London property. Sir John Spencer was the richest citizen of his day, and as he objected to the marriage of his daughter' Lord Compton, so the story runs, carried her off from Crosby Hall in a baker's basket. But Crosby Hall, which escaped the great fire, has been all things in its time, from a Royal palace, to a warehouse and prison ; Presbyterian chapel and an eating house. For many years past it has served the purpose of a City restaurant, but is shortly to be pulled down to make room for modern banking premises. Efforts are being made to save the Hall from destruction, but it is scarcely likely that they will succeed, and in that event the City of London will lose one of its most interesting and historic buildings. New Society Diversions.—A fashion for ballooning and caravanning is the Society woman's latest craze. Amongst the devotees to the -latter pursuit is Lady Arthur Grosvenor, who is married to an uncle of the present Duke of Westminster, and is taking a two months holiday touring with a caravan. Lady Arthur is a member of the Gipsy Lore Society, but gipsying has a cettainlascination which appeals to most people in summer time, althouels one would think that fine weather was an essential condi ion to its success. It is at any rate, not very difficult to indulge in, and many fashion- able people, tired of motoring, are taking advantage of the easy facilities now offered for the hiring of touring caravans. Ballooning as yet is somewhat more exclusive but Mrs. Assheton Harbord, and Mrs. Iltid Nichol!, who are both experienced neronants resent the accusa- tion of foolhardiness, which women balloonists generally have to put up with. Mrs. Nicholl is a daughter of Mr. F. H. Butler, and both she and Mrs. Harbord con- tribute aricles to a hook " Five thousand miles in a balloon," which Mr. Butler has published. Both ladies have made numerous aerial journeys and speak enthpsi- astically of the little-known joys of ballooning. At the Milliners.—Copenhagen blue,—undoubtedly in honour of the visit of the King rind Queen of Denmark to our Court—is greatly to the fore at all fashionable milliners, and cornflowers in this particular hue are in considerable request. Possibly before long, we shall weary of the masses of flowers, and great cluster of feathers that smother our bats at the moment and in many instances make for the groteaque. but so far there are no signs of such a happy state of affairs. The up-to-day woman, with a huge, end very notch over- trimmed bell hat, worn well bask and apparently falling off her head behind, is not so charming and artistic in appearance as she should f e, and we °en only hope that it will not be long now before she realises the fact. All the best followers are somewhat truer to Nature, as regards colour, than they have been for several seasons; past, and are scented like those of Natives own grow- ing. Hats for Wear with "Lingerie" Toilettes.—To wear with the dressy "lingerie" gown, the best:description of hat is the fine white chip crinoline, or other fine make of straw. Quantities of feathers are the most sought-after form of trimming for these hats, while next in favour come whole flewer beds of blooms, and last, but by no means least, swathings of white tulle upon which flowers are embroidered or painted. Hats of broderie Anglaise</p>
        <p>• which once seemed correct with washing dresses are no • longer worn, except by children, which is a move in the ' right direction, as even quite young girls seldom look • their best in this form of headgear, while the appearance of those women past their first youth who have sallied forth in times gone by crowned in this baby fashion, is past description. Gigantic Hat Pins.—lt was the opinion of the majority of us in the spring, that hat pins had increased in size to the greatest possible extent, hint we were wrong, and the latest specimens are positiv‘ly gigantic • —indeed three or four of them skilfully grouped, would make a noval hat, and one of no inconsiderable dimen- sions. These new hat pins shew heads of straw,cleverly manipulated and they should either match the straw of the hat through which the business part of them is thrust, or prove a distinct contract. These hugh straw- headed pins are also being utilised to hold the veil in place, and usually with happy effect. About Gloves.—For several seasons past it has been : the white glove only that, morning, afternoon and evening, has contented the fashionable woman. Now we have all rushed off to the opposite extreme, and are even wearing pale pink and lettuce-green gloves, with white toilettes touched with these colours. Needless to ; say, such a procedure is a huge mistake. Even with .coloured gowns such hues would prove most unbecoming and most enlarging. With all-white gowns, white gloves should be worn, despite any decree on the part of Dame . Fashion, and for wear with every other dressy toilette, pale biscuit, pearl, and tan shades should be chosen. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHRONIC ASTHMA with Haamorrh2ge of Lungs. DECLARED HOPELESS. Extraordinary Results from VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>CHRONIC ASTHMA with Haamorrh2ge of Lungs. DECLARED HOPELESS. Extraordinary Results from VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Beard, of 40, Hucknall-road, Carrington, Nottingham, writes :—" I suffered for five years from chronic asthma and shortness of breath, with periodical and dangerous attacks of bleeding of the lungs. Doctors declared me incurable, and I gave up all hope of ever being better. "One day, as a last resource, I began to take Veno's Lightning Cough cure, and it at once checked the bleeding and eased the spasms, when doctors' medicine and all else had failed. I am quite sure that your Wonderful remedy has prolonged my life. "I was very bad when I began taking Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, but now I am able to fol)ow my employ- ment. lam never without a bottle 'of your valuable</p>
        <p>specific." Mr. Herbert C. Lees, Chemist, of Market Place, Carrington, Nottingham, verifies the above as follows: —" I can personally vouch for the value of Veno's Lightning Cough Cure in the case of Mr. Beard being indisputably and substantially correct." (Signed) H. C. LEES.</p>
        <p>Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is free from all dangerous drugs, and is the safest, purest, and most effective remedy, extant for asthma, coughs, colds, bronchitis, catarrh, hoarseness, sore throat, whooping cough, and all chest and lung troubles. Price 9Ad., is. id, and 2s. 9d. of all chemists and stores everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Wife Desertion.</head>
        <p>Wife Desertion.</p>
        <p>TZicliard Haddock, a labourer, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Monday with leaving his wife and children chargeable to the funds of the union. It was stated by Mr. Shearer that prisoner had deserted his wife periodically for the last ten years. In April last his wife and children were admitted into the workhouse, and remained there until the end of May. Altogether the family had cost the guardians not leis than £7O. When his wife and children were nicely settled in the s ork- house prisoner kept away from the town, and was quite unconcerned about them. The magistrates sent prisoner to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Rowland Stuart, manager of the Theatre Royal, Bournemouth, was found drowned in the sea at Bourne- mouth on Tuesday. He had been missing several days. The Dean of Carlisle, at a" trade" banquet in Loudon on Tuesday night, said a time-limit for licences was confiscation, but be hoped for some diminution of licences, shorter hours on Sundays, and inspection of clubs.</p>
        <p>After waiting 03 years James Chamberlain, who went through the Crimean War, was fighting at Sebastopol and the Alma, and later in the Indian Mutiny, recently received an intimation that the Chelsea Hospital Com- missioners have granted him a shilling a day pension. Chamberlain, who lives at Heswall, Cheshire, is 71 and his wife 63.</p>
      </div>
