      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 15, 1907</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 15, 1907</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME,</p>
        <p>SUMMER CLOTHES.</p>
        <p>Summer clothes are portraying a more than ex- ceptional amount of charm, especially those that assume a certain air of simplicity, writes Mrs. Jack May in the Bystander. The washing frock, as was prophesied, is in extensive evi- dence, and it will take long to exhaust the range of kimonos, fichus, and pinafore corsages which play so conspicuous a ride in its success. We have assuredly had nothing prettier ,or more be- coming for years than these frame-like bodices, supplemented by dainty lingerie sleeves and chemisettes. In lingerie alone there is a wealth of choice, from the plainest tucked lawn to a be- wilderment of elaboration effected in several qualities of lace. As a finish to the waist, I per- sonally prefer the sterner type of belt, built of suede, embroidered linen, or kid; while the very last thought in this regard is of siraw, hand-plaited and finished with a straw buckle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SCHOOLGIRL'S SHIRT BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLGIRL'S SHIRT BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>; Pictured in our sketch is a smart and useful little shirt blouse intended for a schoolgirl's !•wear. The material in which the original was carried out was a fine white French cambric, patterned with a red spot. -Down the centre of the front runs a plain wide box-pleat, bordered :on either side with a narrow kilted frill, and further adorned with four buttons covered with ' red linen. On either shoulders comes a small sloping yoke, firmly machined at the edges. On</p>
        <p>to this yoke the blouSe is set in gitt Irs, the fur. nees being drawn down to the waist, where it is allowed to pouch slightly. From the neck turns back a plain "Puritan" collar of white lawn, machined at the edges, and finished with a nar- row pleated frill. The sleeves, of three-quarter length, are rather full, and are finished with turn-back lawn cuffs frilled and stitched to match the collj,r. The same idea might be carried out with efual success in any pretty cotton or linen washing fabric.</p>
        <p>I.P4l449\NTh'-' ,-.(7\cAloili vk.s._. ill'. ~' -....:N,;-' • %).. t . ...• !'.l-; A -6 ...A LIO "--'4,: .1 4city, ,9,e ‘-,--,,t ''' i 1 , r • 1,111, t I ,il‘ ;hip</p>
