      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WOMAN AND THE BUSINESS SENSE</head>
        <p>WOMAN AND THE BUSINESS SENSE</p>
        <p>Miss Constance Smedley, writing on this sub- ject in the World's Work, says the days when it was held •" unladylike" to earn one's living are well over; hundreds of thousands of women are now warring on the economic battlefield, with "Independence!" as their battle-cry. The busi- ness girl and woman are common enough types. They flood the City. But in what capacity? Almost entirely as clerks and secretaries! So few, indeed, are the prosperous business women, and so very large the proportion' of women in the army of hopeless and ill-paid subordinates, that it is a state of things requiring careful examina- tion. There are plenty of departments in which women might make big successes if they would only venture a little more. Laundries may be run at a big profit; a scrod cook who will study her trade on its economic a, well as its artistio basis may embark on a restaurant- .7. hotel iceQp- ing. Dairy, poultry-rearing, fruit-preserving, and market gardening have all proved lucrative. There should be a big opening for women archi- tects who would make a special study of domestic architecture; house decorating and furnishing give particular scope for woman's taste and in- vention. But in all these professions the desire for money-making must exist. It stands to rea- son that the person who is learning a trade with the object of embarking for herself will learn it far more thoroughly and with far greater zest than the person who merely wishes to obtain a berth and give satisfaction." To the first, no detail is too small to be interesting; every detail must be watched in the endeavour to im- prove it. The whole of a woman's attention must be centred on her career. DAINTY CAMISOLE.</p>
        <p>The transparent or "pneumonia" blouse, as it is aptly °ailed, is no longer the fashionable affair of a few months ago, and with the return of the opaque corsage has come a welcome reversion to more simple, and more truly dainty, underwear. Quito the most modish thing in camisoles just now is the simple well-fitting bodice of fine nein- sook, furnished with a neat little hip basque for</p>
        <p>the proper protection of the corset beneath. The pretty little model sketched in our illustration is quite of the best and newest style. This camisole, you will observe, has for sole trimming a button- holed scallop round shoulders and arms, an em- broidered spot, and a series of buttonholed slits, through which is threaded a pretty pink ribbon, which is tied in a bow in front. Extremely simple, but very smart and in the best of taste. ' t To KEEP FLOWERS FRESH.</p>
        <p>Of the various suggestions for keeping flowers in the house, there is nothing better than clean vases or jars and fresh, cool water, changed at least once .a day. The vase must be deep, in order to hold plenty of water, especially if the stems of the flowers are long. In selecting flower-jars one should choose plain forms and subdued colours, with simple decorations. Simple ones may be used to advantage with a greater variety of flowers. A plain dark green bowl is excellent for yellow roses, and there are some very artistic effects in Japanese holders. When glass is desired, white is said to be much better 'for flowers than coloured. Carnations, if perfectly fresh when they come from the florist, are more economical and keep longer than roses. It is always a great advantage to buy flowers just as the buds are opening, for then they will continue to blossom for a considerable time.</p>
        <p>( 1 if 4-1 .f,. 1 'il 1 ii) \ , . -c \ ki,-; ~ ' •i f. F //,/ ~/,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>ASPARAGUS: VIENNESE Way.—A very good way to serve asparagus is to brown about a tea- cupful of very fine breadcrumbs in butter, serve poured over the heads of the asparagus only; adding some clarified butter if required. A v.,ry nice sauce to serve with asparagus is butter sauce. A tablespoonful of cornflour, 107.. of blittor. and one nint of cold stock: stir wAll a good fire. When the flour and butter are well mixed and begin to thicken, add a little more butter, pepper and salt and a little lemon juice or nutmeg according to ta.ste, and serve at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE APPRECIATEp IN FRANCE. A popular vote that had an unusually in- teresting result was taken in France some time since. A Paris newspaper proposed the question, "Who were the ten greatest Frenchmen of the Nineteenth Century?" Fifteen million votes were recorded, and Pasteur stood at the head of the poll with 1,300,000 votes, Victor Hugo came second with 1,200,000, and Napoleon was only fourth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WHY RUBBER TYRES GET HOT.</head>
        <p>WHY RUBBER TYRES GET HOT.</p>
        <p>When an automobile is running at high speed tne rubber tyres are rapidly, warmed, and the heat sometimes becomes very great, with resultant injury to the rubber. The cause of this accumulation of heat in the tyre is ascribed to the kneading of the rubber, which generates heat faster than it can be radiated away. For this reason manu- facturers have found it to be an advantage to have metal parts in the tread, such as the ends of rivets, in contact with the tyre, be- cause the metal, being a good radiator, helps to carry off the heat to the outer air.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>AN EYE EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>AN EYE EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>In the museum at Stockholm there is a very interesting collection of eyes taken from human beings at different ages. They are cut across in such a way as to exhibit plainly the internal and the external eye. In looking at these specimens it is easy to observe that the eye of the young child is as transparent as water; that of the youth a little less so; in the •man of thirty the eye becins to be slightly opaque; in the man of fifty or sixty it Is decidedly opaque, and in the man of seventy or eig i hty it s dull and lustreless. This gradual development of opacity is due to the increase of fibrous tis- sue and the deposit of waste matter in the eye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DENATURED ALCOHOL.</head>
        <p>DENATURED ALCOHOL.</p>
        <p>In order that alcohol may be considered to be denatured, something must be added to it which destroys the possibility of using it as a beverage. For instance, a mixture which contains 100 gallons of grain alcohol, ten gailons of wood alcohol, and half a gallon of benzine would be unsuitable for drinking. As a matter of fact, if the above mixture had water added to it, the benzine would be thrown down. For alcohol which is used for the manufacturo •of celluloid, camphor is mixed. For shellac varnish, only a mixture of grain and wood alcohol is used. It costs more to produce the raw denatured alcohol than the pure grain alcohol, but, of course, the tax on the latter makes the denatured alcohol comparatively cheap.</p>
        <p>A MICROPHONE FIREDAMP DETECTIVE. In France Monsieur Hardy has invented an application of the microphone to detect firedamp in mines. This is its principle: If sound-waves from two pipes of equal pitch impinge on microphones connected in series with a telephone, a clear note is hei*d ; but if one of the pipes emits a slightly different note, beats will be heard in the telephone. Here is the application: One pipe is placed in the mine, the other above ground, and they are blown simultaneously. If the air in the mine is charged with firedamp, it Will produce a different note from that produced by clear air, owing to the difference of den- sity, and, in consequence, a series of beats in the telephone gives warning of the pre- sence of firedamp. The same apparatus is very sensitive to the presence of coal-gas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WALL – PAPER AND TEMPERAMENT.</head>
        <p>WALL - PAPER AND TEMPERAMENT.</p>
        <p>It has long been recognised by medical men that the colour of the wall-paper has an appreciable effect on the occupants of a room, says the Prader. Scientists have made the subject a distinct branch of study. They call it chroinopathy. And some of the more important of their results may be sum- marised as follows Red, as a predominant colour on the walls, tends to rouse, in- vigorate, and cheer • blue has a sedative, calming influence, but with some people it is depressing, and leads to melancholy, al- though nervous excitement is frequently allayed by it; yellow is a capital cerebral stimulant, and, combined with red, thus forming orange. it proves a powerful specific in those of a- sluggish nature; green has a very resting effect upon the eyes, and their being in such close sympathy with the brain, the soothing influence of this colour proves helpful in case, of brain-fag.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TO HELP THE VOICE.</head>
        <p>TO HELP THE VOICE.</p>
        <p>Experiments by M. Marage shew that it is practicable to gauge the acoustic proper- ties of any public hall, and to direct each speaker how to use the voice to the best ad- vantage. It was found that in a hall where a regular continued sound ia produced, there may be heard the primary wave from the source; many diffuse waves returned from the walls, producing resonance; and waves regularly reflected, giving distinct echoes. The duration of the resonance sound deter- mines whether the acoustic properties are good or bad, the satisfactory condition being where the resonance is sufficiently short to reinforce the sound that produces it without interferins with the following one. His "vowel siren" has been used by Marage in stutlyinq the resonance sound, and the natural voice has been thus replaced by a mechanical vibration whose pitch, intensity and quality, can be determined exactly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CONCRETE AND STEEL</head>
        <p>CONCRETE AND STEEL</p>
        <p>The engineering experiment station in con- nection with the University of Illinois is con- stantly publishing the result of some useful work. Amongst other things it has presented to the world the result of ea series of tests, which extended over a period of two years. Peculiarly enough, although reinforced con- crete is used greatly in building work, yet we have very little data concerning its real pro- perties. We now know something of the shearing strength of concrete, as well as the strength of the bond between the concrete and steel. With regard to the test on the concrete, it was decided to subject the mate- rial to a "punching test," which simply means that a hole was punched in a concrete plate or block. These punching tests were made on blocks 13in. square. The specimen was placed on a bed-plate: lin. thick, with an opening 6in. in diameter in the centre. A spherical bearing block was used to apply the load, and a die, which was 7iin. in diameter, was used as the punching tool. The inve.stigators say ,that "it is difficult to devise a form of test specimen and a method or testing which will satisfactorily deter- mine the resistance of concrete to shear." The trouble is that it is very difficult indeed to secure an even distribution of the shear. Complications are formed by the compres- sive, tensile, and bursting stresses developed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MYSTERY OF SLEEP.</head>
        <p>THE MYSTERY OF SLEEP.</p>
        <p>Although the phenomenon has been ob- served since man has existed, the true nature of sleep and what causes it are prac- tically mysteries to-day. All recognise the necessity of Sleep and know that it is the sovereign remedy for fatigue "tired nature's sweet restorer." This fact has led to the theory that sleep is induced by the poisons which are known to be formed and passed into the blood stream during weary- ing exercise of mind or body. The theory is that these poisons benumb the nerve-centre controlling the circulation in the brain, with a consequent reduction in the blood supply; this " anemia " of the brain produces un- consciousness, or sleep, during which the activities of body and mind are repressed, the forniation of " iatigue poisons" is ar- rested, and those already formed and circu- lating in the blood are eliminated. When this has taken place the purified blood again nourishes and stimulates the circulation centre, an increased volume of blood flows t..) the brain, and the subject awakes, re- newed in spirit and flesh, and ready for the labours of the day. This explanation is only a theoretical one, rqmarks an American magazine, but it is plausible. Whatever may produce it, and whether it is a cause or an accompaniment, we know that a lessenpd blond simply in the brain is associated With healthy sleep, and that cerebral excitement and congestion must be overcome before normal sleep can take place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>11LL RIIITTB RZEIERVID.I THE EXPLOITS OF THE ONE-EYED MAN.</head>
        <p>11LL RIIITTB RZEIERVID.I THE EXPLOITS OF THE ONE-EYED MAN.</p>
        <p>BY EDWARD TOWLER.</p>
        <p>VIII.-LE DEBACLE.</p>
        <p>The adventure of the One-eyed Man here related proved his undoing. To use a well- known aphorism the pitcher went to the well once too often, and the series of tremendous crimes that had shaken England were brought to a sudden and unexpected end.</p>
        <p>It was on a wet and dreary day in Novem- ber that the One-eyed Man and the Greaser, in a dingy room in Soho, met, by appoint- ment, two Germans. The elder of the two, a grey-moustached man, spoke, and with a wave of his hand said:</p>
        <p>"I am Breitenschein; my friend is Bern- stein, monsieur. We are those you expect." "You are from?" queried the One-eyed Man.</p>
        <p>" Leonski, of Brussels," replied Breiben- schein.</p>
        <p>" Good !" said the One-eyed Man. "Now to business. It appears that even if I did fail in the matter of the Treaty, your Government is still anxious for my services. What is the nature of the work?"</p>
        <p>"Monsieur." said Breitenschein, "we re- quire a signal-book of the British Navy." The Greaser burst into a laugh. Friend Breitenschein, your Government will want. Why, they are so precious they are bound in lead, so that if they fall overboard they sink at once. As well ask for the Dome of St. Paul's for a newspaper kiosk in your Unter den Linden ' "</p>
        <p>"Not so fast, Greaser. I think, with the resources at our command, we may be able to accomplish that which now appears to be im- possible," said the One-eyed Man. "Don't tackle it, Boss," urged the Greaser. "Governments are not individuals. Let well alone. Let us quit the game now and retire upon _our. laurels and.. our gains.."._.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>"Monsieur is timid," siieered Bernstein.</head>
        <p>"Monsieur is timid," siieered Bernstein.</p>
        <p>"Take that back, you half-bred skunk ! " said the Greaser in a sudden white-hot rage; and Bernstein, looking down the muzzle of the Greaser's, pistol, apologised. The One-eyed Man poured oil on troubled waters, " and the conversation was resumed. Finally. the One-eyed Man undertook the commission for the sum of twenty thousand pounds, in spite of the Greaser's objections, and after a few preliminaries had been settled the meeting broke up.</p>
        <p>It was the evening of November 14th, and Plymouth was wrapped in a thick fog. The Submarine was off Prawle Point, awaiting the arrival of the One-eyed Man, the Greaser, Bresci, Simini, and four of the crew. They were coming to join the Submarine in a strange craft, for the One-eyed Man was going to essay for the first time in history the cutting out of one of his Britannic Majesty's torpedo-boats. That was how the signal- books were to be obtained.</p>
        <p>A big dance was to take place in Plymouth that evening, and most of the officers had ac- cepted invitations. The X. Y. Z. lay at her moorings under banked fires. She was due to sail at daybreak, and the crew had liberty leave until midnight. She was left in charge of a sub-lieutenant and Rix A.B.'s.</p>
        <p>The " sub " smoked furiously, and cursed his luck at not being able to join in the revels with a fluency only acquired by those to whom the Seven Seas are as their own street for knowledge. " Bloomin' dark, Bill," rumbled an A.B. to his mate, who stood smoking under the lee of the engine-room. a dog's throat's the break o' day to it! This 'ere's a night for liftin' goods from them sheds," said his mate, jerking a stubby forefinger over in the direction of the tailway goods sheds. Had they looked round then they might have saved the situation, for out of the black- ness of the fog eight rubber-shod figures crept silently towards the fo'castle. Two stunning raps over the heads of the A.B.'s and they were stretched senseless upon the deck, then bound hand and foot.</p>
        <p>• " Quick ! " jerked out the One-eyed Man. " Guiseppe, take the wheel! Now follow me , below. We'll take the crew first. There's only four, and they'll be 'turned in' and nalehAn." This was done, and almost in less time than it takes to write, the surprise was over and the men securely bound, with two of the Sub- marine's crew keeping guard. There Wc only the lieutenant left. He was dozing in the mud rnom aft, and so silently had the work been done that, strange as it may appear, he heard no noise. . . . .</p>
        <p>Bie-sci and Simini you be off down to the engine-room—the Greaser and I can manage the lieutenant. You "—to the remaining man —" up you go on deck, and cut the cables; then stay with Guiseppe until we come," said . the One-eyed Man as he and the Greaser • moved towards the ward-room. Turning the handle they flung themselves upon the aston- ished lieutenant, who was taken unawares. For all that he fought like a fiend. Never had either of his assailants such a struggle as they had with him; but he was at a disad- vantage, and, with the Greaser on his chest ,he was tied up securely. The fight had been severe. The One-eyed Man had a cut over the temple, and the Greaser lacked two teeth as the result of what the " sub " described afterwards as "a very decent little scrap." Breathing out oaths of vengeance the lieutenant looked at his foes, then, as recol- lections forced themselves upon him, he ; cried:</p>
        <p>"By Heavens! It's you scoundrels of the ' Submarine! What's your game this time?" The One-eyed Man spoke suavely: "As you so ably remark, Lieutenant—' the scoundrels of the Submarine'. We have business with you.,, "Money, I suppose?" said the lieutenant. "You are sadly out, Lieutenant." The face of the lieutenant went pale as a low pulsation ran through the boat—a pulsa- tion he knew only too well. "We are moving!" he cried. "We are under way! What in thunder is the meaning of this? " He writhed in his ropes until they cut into his very flesh. The One-eyed Man looked at him steadily. "We have called for your signal-books," he said. "We happen to know they are on board."</p>
        <p>"My signal-books? I'll see you in perdition before you have them ! " roared the liputenant. "It matters not where you will see us, my friend," said the One-eyed Man, "the fact remains that, choosing our time, we have cut out —I think you would term it—your torpedo-boat, the X. Y.Z., for the sole pur- pose of getting the code-books of your Navy; and get them we shall. At the same time, I grant you, it's hard luck you should have been so ignominiously captured by us in our Sub- marine a few weeks past, and now you allow yourself to be carried off, boat, crew, every- thing. I fear—if you survive—your promotion will be slow."</p>
        <p>The helpless sub-lieutenant was nearly de- lirious with rage, but he spoke no word in answer. The One-eyed Man continued: "I think it fair to tell you our programme so that you may be prepared for the end. "The Submarine will pick us illa off Prawle Point. We shall open your se-cocks before leaving you, and stand by until the finish of the drama, viz., until the waters have closed over your boat for ever. Au revoir, Lieu- tenant ! " With that they left the cabin. The lieutenant, a prey to the bitterest anguish, cudgelled his brains for a way of escape. If he could only save his signal-books; he would not mind. It was the honour of his beloved profession. His! life he did not value a rap in the stakes at issue. He looked round the ward-room for some means of escape, but none _presented itself to him. _ .</p>
        <p>Halt-an-hour later the Greaser entered. "Lieutenant," he said, we shall rid you of our presence shortly. The Submarine is in sight, and as we have your signal-books delay is needless. I regret that circumstances compel us to leave you to your fate." The lieutenant did not deign to answer 1-om : hA wag thinkine. There was still time. The Greaser, turning on his heel, left the cabin.</p>
        <p>The lieutenant waited until the footsteps</p>
        <p>Aka away, and then, with a great joy in hit !see, rolled over the floor towards a bright object that attracted his attention, shining like a diamond, but to him far more pre- cious. It was a diagonal piece of glass from the broken tumbler that had held his whisky, smashed in the fracas and jammed into the locker. Would it hold? He held his bound wrists tightly againet it, and, to his unspeak- able delight, it held! It was no more than a splinter some two inches long, but it had a razor-like edge, and he laboured on until the ropes fell from his hands and he was free. Cramming a heavy Navy revolver into his pocket and ripping off the cover of a box of cartridges, he felt himself a man again. Thinking they might give him another look before they left he proceeded to tie himself up, " trick " fashion, so that in the event of a visit he would be safe, for it was not his plan to stop them. He had a deeper game to play. They eventually paid him another visit, but seeing him, to all intents and purposes, safe, they left him. As they stepped out the Greaser said to the One-eyed Man: "Boss, this is your outfit, but leaving 'em to drown like rats is too thick, Boss, and I quit after this affair, see?" The One-eyed Man was surprised. Humanity was not, and never had been, a. strong trait in the Greaser's character.</p>
        <p>"Touch of the sun, I should say if it wasn't winter, Greaser. Are you turning soft, or what? But, come, see to that later. Now hurry up, boys, and let's get off. The sea-cocks are open. The boat from the Submarine put off, and in less than ben minutes the two men, with Bresci, Simini, and the crew of the Sub- marine who were with, them and had helped in the capture of the X. Y. Z., were again on board their craft, and the One-eyed Man had the signal-books buttoned tightly at his breast.</p>
        <p>The moment they were gone the lieutenant, who had heard their departure, rushed below, and, tearing through the water that wtts al- ready washing round the engine-room floor, turned off the cocks. Then, creeping warily along the deck to the fo'castle, he found his men, who were about to raise a cheer as he cut them loose.</p>
        <p>"Silence—on _ your life! Follow me!"</p>
        <p>The keen nigh-t air revived them, and when they knew there was a prospect of repaying with interest the outrage upon them, they were wickedly joyful. Barely a quarter of a mile away they could dimly make out the low "whale back" of the Submarine, waiting for the final act of the tragedy—the last plunge of the torpedo-boat. The " sub " flitted round, a great joy on his face, seeing to things with a glee he could not suppress, while the A.B.'s rumbled in the darkness with a choking laugh. The lieutenant knew he could not get the books back by ordinary means. That being so, he must use extraordinary, for the means most certainly justified the end. Nearer and nearer he edged his boat to the Submarine, and so ably that she appeared but to drift in the eddies of the current.</p>
        <p>Then suspicion deepened with the One-eqed Man. "Curse it, she's under command ! " he yelled. "Below, all of you, for your lives!" For they were standing on the whale back.</p>
        <p>It wa-s the last utte-rance of the man who had terrorised England with his crimes, for even as he spoke the Whitehead torpedo, that had been fitted in the for'ard tube, leaped with a whizz into the darkness of the night towards the Submarine.</p>
        <p>; A great cry, mingled with shrieks of com- mand, issued from the doomed boat, but all of no avail. Nemesis was at last upon them. There was a terrific explosion, and the Sub- Alarine heaved up and flung her shattered ribs high into the sky. A few cries, then all was silence. The end had come swiftly and unexpectedly, but, as is often the case, from a quarter undreamt of. The lieutenant lowered a boat and cruised round to look for possible survivors. He picked up the body • of Simini, who had the signal-books upon him intact and damaged only by seawater. Evidently the One-eyed Man had passed them to him, for some reason or other. Any way, they were safely back in the keeping of the lieutenant, with their secrets inviolate.</p>
        <p>The coastguard at the Start Point reported hearing a dull explosion; but no vestage of the Submarine's wreckage Wag ever washed ashore. The sub-lieutenant, " sub " no longer. but full lieutenant, wears a special decora- tion on his breast. The X. Y.Z. still proudly flies the red ensign, and the signal code is still England's secret.</p>
        <p>When the facts were known that the lieutenant had blown up the mysterious Sub- marine, together with her gang and her booty, there was great rejoicing, and the Press loudly acclaimed the gallant officer as the hero of the hour. It must be added, however, that there were sundry heart-burnings in Scotland Yard that theirs were not the hands to lay tho gang by the heels. Inspector Bullen, for instance, would have given much to add the capture of the One-ey.ed Man and his gang to the laurels he had so deservedly wow" _ _</p>
        <p>[THE END.I</p>
        <p>eligible for re-election as managers and a proposition , to this effect was placed before the meeting. Mr. BOUCHIER, however, said that owing to the I pressure of engagements he must retire, and Mr. Harry i Hope was elected in his place. The other managers named were re-elected.</p>
        <p>IMessrs. Willial</p>
        <p>OTHER ELECTIONS. Bodt id &amp; -</p>
        <p>-m Bodloam ,obert Lee were re-elected i surveyors and Messrs. Messrs. A. W. Howard and G. Armstrong auditors.</p>
        <p>MANAGERS' REM tiNERATION. i It was decided that the managers be allowed ls. each for every meeting they had attended. MR. BOETHIER'S RETIREMENT. ! Mr. E. WALLINGTON moved a vote of thanks to Mr. , Bonchier for his services, extending over somethinglike a quarter of a century. He had been able to give them some very valuable assistance, owing to his practical acquaintance with finance. At one period in the history of the Society the hblp he gave them was of an exception- ally valuable character, because when the finances of that and all kindred societies had to be remodelled according to a basis set forth by the Registrar. General it involved a considerable amount of work and nobody : but a person familiar with accounts could have done it. Mr.Bouchier was one of two or three who put the finances on a sound fopting and did a great deal to make the other membereunderstand them. Mr. HOPE seconded, and said Mr. Bouchier was an ideal manager. • The CHAIRMAN supported, and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>Mr. BOUCHIER briefly returned thanks, and said it was not without considerable regret that he felt com- pelled to ?diminish the duties.</p>
        <p>TRUSTEE THANKED. I On the motion of the CHAIRMAN, seconded by Mr. HARDING, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. John Hatton, trustee and one of the founders of the Society. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded ther meeting.</p>
        <p>; PRESENTATION TO EX-SERGEANT LAING, AT HAYDOCK. IMr. Laing, who has just retired from the police force after 28 years' service, the latter part of which time has been spent in charge of the Haydock police station,has been presented with a handsome and service- able gold watch. The presentation was made at the i District Council Office, n the presence of several local residents, by Mr. John Robinson, Chairman of the Council, on behalf of the subscribers. Mr. Robinson said Sergeant Laing had been in the force twenty-eight ; years; after three years' service he obtained the order of merit ; after six years he received a sum of money; and after ten years he was highly commended. He had been at Haydock for three years and now that he was retiring altogether they were so pleased with his set.. vices that the local people had subscribed for this watch, which he handed to him with every good wish that he might long en;oy his retirement. (Applause.)--- Dr. Dowling, the -Medical Officer, said he endorsed the chairman's good wishes and added that ever since he knew Sergeant Laing be found him to be most consis- tent and efficient in the performance of his dutieS• Inspector Palmer (County Police Force), said that the sergeant joined the force about 28 years ago, and be had known him all that time; he had found in hira everything that was required in a good officer. Sergeant Laing, in response, said he thanked them for their good wishes, and was very glad to find his efforts had been appreciated. (Hear, hear.)</p>
