      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PROSPEROUS BUILDING SOCIETY</head>
        <p>A PROSPEROUS BUILDING SOCIETY</p>
        <p>Anncal Meeting of the Workingmen's Society.</p>
        <p>A successful year's working was vnder review at the thirty-seventh annual meeting of members of the War- rington Workingmen's Permanent Benefit Building Society, which was held in the Golborne-street school- room on Monday evening. Mr. Robert Isherwood presided over a good attendance, which included Messrs. James Harding (chairman of managers), G. Wallington, J. W. Bouchier, J. Hawthorn, H. Hope, J. Longland (solicitor) and Peter Dalton (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>In the course of their annual report the managers stated :—During the year the sum of £1,676 2s. 2d. has been received as subscriptions ; £2,401 12s. Od. for repayments; and £5 Bs. entrance fees, fines, Scc. ; banker's interest, £22 17s. 2d., making the total receipts £4,195 19s. 4d.; the working expenses being £69 17s. 2d. The withdrawals amount to £968 13s. 2d. The sum of £3,125 has been advanced to members. There are 223 subscribers, holding 296 shares; and 49 borrowers, holding 2271 shares ; making 272 members, holding 523 i shares. The bonuses the year amount to £546 17s. 6d. on the amount lent, of which sum £lOO is divided as profit, and £446 17s. 6d. carried to the prospective interest account. rhe total amount remaining owing upon mortgages is £11,486 95., of which only £10,102 14s. 3d. can be regarded as capitol, the balance of £1,383 14s. 9d. representing the propor- tionate amount of interest thereon, calculated for the number of years the several mortgages have still to run. The profits for the year, including £l7 15s. 11d, from last account, after deducting working expenses, amount to £430 10s. Bd., out of which the managers recommend a dividend of 4 per cent., which will take £387 7s. sd. leaving a balance of £4335. 3d., £25 of which they direct to be placed to creeit of the contingent fund, and the balance, £lB 3s. 3d., carried forward to next year's account. The total amount owing to the society on mortgages is £11.486,95. The valuation of the property upon which the advatices have been made is £22,900. The whole of the managers retire. Messrs. Harding, E. Wallington, Ishervood, Bouchier, Baxter, and W. H. Wallington are eligible, and offer themselves for re-elec- tion. S'ix additional managers will be taken from the Register in rotation. The CHAIRMAN said the report proved that the Society was not at a standstill but that it was progressing. They were not getting very rich but they were keeping their heads above water and making an improvement. The working expenses amounted to £69 17s. 2d. as compared with £73 last year. The Society was worked on the most economical principles and it did not cost one per cent to carry the work through. There were now 223 members and 49 borrowers as against 209 mem- bers and 40 borrowers last year. They had a great deal to encourage them in their work. He had read many reports of Building Societies, but he had not seen nay society in a more flourishing condition than their's. Some people said they could not understand a building society having no property. It was true they had no property on hand. The forty-nine borrowers were the landlords of the property and it was in their own hands; conseauently the Society ran no risk in that respect. Mr:GREALEY in moving the adoption of the 'Aare° sheet said it was the best h 3 could remember having been presented to the Society. They had had a very successful year. The balance sheet was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DIVIDEND.</head>
        <p>THE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>It was decided to pay a dividend of 4 per cent. Mr. GREALEY said it 'masa satisfactory percentage but they ought not to stop at that. He considered it unnecessari to increase the contingency fund much further and to keep building it up was to deprive the members of the full fruits of their investments.</p>
