      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Old Age Pensions Discussed. Th„ Labr,„rtionthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and ues-dmr Council was held in Bold-street school on atte7,a37 evening, Mr. T. Plumb presiding over a fair uance.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. 5. PLUMB. CUA/RMAN referred to the loss the Council had tained by the death of Mr. Samuel Plumb, one of railway delegates, who was an ardent and respected trade unionist. H e moved a vote of condolence with the widow and family. Rr.HICKINBOTTOM seconded and the vote was passed. THE PLACE OF MEETING. tholl the proposition of Mr. GREALEY it was decided thltthe officials should take steps to obtain the use of Independent Labour Party's room,Friars' Green.for Meetings of the Council, commencing on the second Uesday in October.</p>
        <p>OLD-AGE PENSIONS. DEMONSTRATION TO BE HiLD. H- l.letter was read asking if the Council would be to co-operate with the Independent Labour tturtY in holding a joint demonstraticn to protest • gainst the action of the Government in not providing 1131? this year's Budget a decent scheme of old-age pen- The Independent Labour Party, it was stated, 41 also seeking the co-operation of the Labour Repre- entation Committee, as the question of old-age tensions affected all the workers. If the Council decided :wen-operate, it was suggested that they should appoint 1,2 T.res to confer with representatives of the lnde- -11 eat Labour Party. th't was proposed by Mr. GREALEY that the Council ?uld co-operate in the demonstration. 11r• WAINWRIGHT said he did not think many trade factrists wanted pensions, and he did not believe in rt.jog for men who had never paid a halfpenny to a %elety. 4a,,tlr. SMITH said hundreds of men in Warrington were gLitled to pensions and had been paying to societies • theiri lives. He could not understand anyone trying r"? Put a damper on old age pensions. He had as much :ght to receive a pension from the State as from his rl society. He did not believe in pensions just for i °so who never paid to unions. Why not have pensions 11,atead of workhouses? The workhouse was not the 145ce for the British workmen. „ Mr. RICHARDSON said ordinary workers were as much 'Otitled to pensions as Government servants. One !teat drain on trade unions at present was the super- oation fund. They wanted old age pensions to .;:18ve that fund and enable them to build up a fighting -'serve. Those who had had the benefit—the capitalists c• oght to be made to pay towards keeping the work- in their old age. esnir• WILLIAMSON said the money paid to keep people Workhouses would he better spent on pensions. The wiliY People who benefited by the workhouse system tte those who neglected to provide for old age. arr. MASSEY said the great difficulty was to draw the ,Itle between the deserving and the undeserving. He ;Geld not care to support the demonstration if it a ?old commit the Council to expense and necessitate 'evY• He would like to see workhouses more like gardens of paradise. t,Alr. HALEY said capitalists were fond of preaching re gospel of thrift to workingmen, but he had yet to tD that there were many industriee in which a work- 'ngman had the chance of exercising that thrift which 17.0u1d bring him a competency in his old age. The hlnae had come when the captains of industry who reached thrift should show how it could be done. The tate should provide through the captains of industry for 14 rank and tile who could not piovide for themselves. neouncillor SToTT said that if they could persuade the iovernment to introduce a scheme of old age pensions Would he one of the finest things for organised labour 4"▪ ,at could happen. At present very few societies gave nPerannuation to their old members and there was no encouragement for men to be thrifty. Workingmen could Z§ not save sufficient to bring in an income at the e of sixty that would be sufficient to keep them. If 10. Government, however, promised them from 6s. to a week it would be an encouragement for them to ire to keep themselves out of the Worhhouse.Any man aveciVing less than 30s. a week and who bad a family and tn'e,mpted to be thrifty deserved to be flogged. He "-11.'d not save without lotting the family suffer. 1107 r. PoDstoßE said that if organised labour did make a fieht for old age pensions they nuld not get them. He would like the question</p>
        <p>Makes ironing easier and beAter.</p>
        <p>• ~ •,i i.,41vii,i11,KTV41.1t.:01.4r, ''Zl:'%'„•;figfi,ir43‘kri:':kl!';4t;lZ•A.***4 V%:l4.'";A:i:liiti.:2:4Zl}4;•-; P'.l7."4.;;A:4lFRAlntVirrAPlVifitttl;Vll4g.t.,;:"'aft, :Vigett •,L ,i.t.;..r,Atpickr ottso.:C! o.4%*4,,,Ok.tiVertlV.rit),!.M. k 4 ap,V.,1":.74.1, ~.4.3,„; ~,,,,,,,, , -,r,,,,, izn,,,..t....----,1, it ?-sti' 6 • I :0. 461 41 g...' i•ii.: $ 11 , 2,7•4-.. 34... i. t,,,,,,,,*,;,,,,,„-.,,,,,,,- ,i :, • -,k'v,i&gt;e.k.,_v4-AA,,,t4*.:,..53-:v,? ~ ' „y...,,,,,4,7,,,, ...,,,...v. , ,il-,07,4- ,y, , , ~,,r ... VA, .. .... ,k '&lt;l _.,,,,,-- .1,7 14,- :ft,A.- •( 1 ;:. :34 4 14 'm '.i." '''' , , ---.i7: -- A,,,,,,r, , . . .. ~,,,,jsr ,</p>
