      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington resumed. operations on Saturday after several week's inactivity and judging by the trouncing which they gave Newton they have not suffered by absence from the field. The victory has placed them in a higher position in the League table and at present everything points to a keen struggle between St.Flelens, Iferlestown and Warrington for premier honours. St. Elelens at present hold the lead and are undefeated, whilst Warrington and Earlestown have each lost one match. In the second division Earlestown are making he running through here as in the senior section the uccess of any team cannot be foretold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>league tables to date are :- -_</head>
        <p>The league tables to date are :- -_</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION. Pl'd. W. L. D. Pts. St. Helens 4 4 0 0 8 Earlestown Warrington Prescot St. Helens Recs. Stanley Newton SECOND DIVISION. Earleatown Prescot Warrington St. Helens Recs. 5 2 3 0 4 Newton NA St. Helens Stanley</p>
        <p>So far this season Newton have done rather badly and there was no turn in the tide of fortune when they visited Warrington on Saturday. Batting first they opened poorly, two wickets being down for three runs. A brief stand was then made but Cresswell and Callon bowled so well that runs were exceedingly hard to get. After some stonewalling, Harrison was tempted to hit and 'Wright brought of! a fine catch. Further success attended the Warrington efforts and half the Newton team were out for 27. The dismissal of Hindley prac- tically closed the innings and Warrington required 36 to win.</p>
        <p>With feelings of confidence J. Callon and Richardson proceeded to the wickets and fifteen runs were obtained before Callon fell a victim to good fielding. Wright joined Richardson and at once proceeded to make merry and when he was joined by Bibby the spectators were treated to a delightful exhibition. Both men are hitters and both forced the pace, banging the bowling all over the field. The changes were rung in the Newton attack but without success and the score was carried to 136 when Wright played forward and was promptly stumped. His 65 was secured by good cricket and included seven fours. Podmore and Cresswell gave little trouble and presently Bibby's bright innings was brought to a close Howarth upsetting his stumps when his score was 52. The remaining men offered but poor resistance and the innings closed for 160.</p>
        <p>The chief feature of the afternoon was of course the hitting of Bibby and Wright and the return to form of the latter was particularly pleasing. Previously Wright had performed indifferently and in three matches had only secured 19 runs. Bibby on the other hand has scored heavily in both first team games and placed 49 to his credit when he assisted the second eleven against St. Helens.</p>
        <p>Cresswell showed a falling off but, largely owing to 63 scored by him in the opening match, his average is 191. It is as a bowler that he has profited the War- rington team so far and his 16 wickets have cost only 5.2 runs each. As an upsetter of the calculations of batsmen Callon runs the professional close with 15 wickets for 97 runs. May both continue in the good form which they have so far shown.</p>
        <p>None of the other batsmen on the first eleven have.as yet, done anything brilliant, and it is about time some of the second teamers were given a chance. The old professional, Tnewlis is on top form and with an aver- age of 28 for five matches he ought to catch the eye of the Selection Committee. Hawtin too has lost none of his deftness and after three weeks probation with the second, during which he has bagged 99 runs, he is surely worth consideration.</p>
        <p>OZ. Saturday these two materially assisted the second team to victory on the Newton ground, whilst H. Dolan also gave promise of future success, as, hitting with great freedom he scored 2S. The Warrington total reached 177, and in response Newton could only score T 6, out of which H. Lyons claimed 39.</p>
        <p>The position of the Clubs in the Warrington and Dis- trict League continues pretty much as it has been for some weeks, and there is great sorrowing in St. James' thereat. Last week Orford Barracks held the League with St. James' second, and as the two teams met at Arpley on Saturday, St. James' confidently expected to occupy the proud position of leaders, pinning their faith on their own abilities, and upon the disadvantage which the Soldiers would labour under on a grlss wicket &amp;filer being used to operating on cocoanut matting.</p>
        <p>Batting first St. James failed before the deliveries of Gleason, Price and Knight and were all tumbled out for 47. When the soldiers took their place at the wickets Johnson proceeded to knock up the runs but as no one could be found to stay with him St. James pros- pects were rather rosy, but Wilson and Knight came to the rescue and before the last wicket fell the soldiers had won by 10 runs. For their victory they had to thank Johnson who scored 30 out of a total of 57, a performance which is the more meritorious as no less than seven soldiers" got the bullet."</p>
        <p>Orford Barracks now have a clear lead of four points and will apparently take a good deal of shifting from their position. The League table is : P. W. L. D. Pts Orford Barracks St. James' 4 3 ... 1 ... 1 ... 7 Mackies Bewsey Wesleyans Castle Rubber Cheshire Lines 6 ... 2 ... 4 ... 0 ... 4 Wilderspool Brewery 5 ... 0 ... 5 ... 0 ... 0</p>
        <p>Bewsey Wesleyans suffered defeat on their own ground at the hands of Castle Rubber by five runs. Batting first the rubber men ran up 57 out of which W. Wright claimed 16 and Shand 10. The Wesleyans opened well and a first wicket stand raised their hopes. Then a rot set in and after a promising start they lost.</p>
        <p>A miss may be as good as a mile but Wilderspool Brewery do not think so. For one thing they have yet to win their first match and on Saturday they almost accomplished that object. Playing at Rattersbviane they dismissed the C.L.C. engineers for 40, Brown with 16 being top scorer. Wilderspool were not alarmed at the task which had been set them and when Hallows and Stone made a stand their hopes ran high. Both batsmen, however, fell and as the next three men bagged " ducks " things looked black. Then Briggs came to the rescue, knocked up five and was clean bowled by McKay when two runs were wanted to win. Hallows showed the best form of the afternoon his 20 being got by good cricket when all his colleagues failed.</p>
        <p>Widnes White Rose have at last succeeded in bagging a scalp at Warrington, the victims being Penketh, and they are -looking forward to tasting more of the sweets of victory when the return matches with the other clubs are played. Batting first Penketh scored 41, Dumbell with 15 being the only man to meet with any success. Widnes lost two or three men for a few runs and then Sadler and Robinson proceeded to wipe off the rest of the arrears and the Penketh total was passed before they were parted. Sadler was top scorer of the match with 18.</p>
        <p>Time after time have Moore and Daresbury had to thank the brothers Taylor for victory and on Saturday they were again indebted to the two for their success. Playing on Crosfield's recreation ground H. Taylor took six wickets for 13 runs, whilst the three soaprnen who fell before S. Taylor only scored seven between them. Moore had previously run up NO, Dutton (36) being the principal scorer.</p>
        <p>H. Maybury, a Lovely-laue batsman, on Saturday made merry at the expense of the Holy Trinity bowlers and was 58 before he was dismissed, out of a total of 94. Holy Trinity in reply could only knock 36, Stanley (12) and Harding (11) being top scorers.</p>
        <p>Not a single Fairfield player on Saturday reached double figures and as Ashcroft with 11 for St. James' was the highest scorer of the day it can he judged that the totals ware not big. They were : Fairfield 21, St. James' juniors 34.</p>
        <p>All the Latchford men scored in their match with Monks Hall, and as Cox secured 20 and Hayes 12, the total was carried to 80. Monks Hall opened very badly and six wickets were down for ten runs, when Highley attempted to stop the rot. Flitting out vigorously be scored 20, but as the rest of the batsmen failed his side were defeated by 42.</p>
        <p>The works competition match mn Monday between the Nowall Engineering Co., and Modks Hall (No. 1) produced some good cricket. The engineeis secured 30 for S wickets whilst in the 50 minutes allowed Monks Hall ran up over 100. Out of this H. Hawthorn a Sankey Wire Mills player. contributed 50 not out and is a strong candidate for the gold medal presented by Mr. J. W. Bibhy for the highest individual score during the competition. Fifty six in 50 minutes takes a lot of knocking up and enyono who can beat that score will deserve the medal but the probability is that it will stand as the record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>, --7:7-77- . _</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
