        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The fast round of the Warrington Works Competition was completed on Saturday and for a few weeks there will be a break in the tournament for the teams, to enable the preliminary rounds of the individual main to be played off. This has always been a prominent feature in the programme of the Norks Association and the present season will not witness any falling off of interest. On the contrary the number of entries will probably be greater than ever. The draw for the first round, which opens on June 23th,takes place on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>Orford Tannery on Saturday secured a narrow victory over Longford in the first round of the team competi- tion. Opening indifferently they proceeded to forge ahead and with the match half over held a handsome lead, and though Longford made a determined effort to pull the match out of the are, Orford proved too strong and qualified for the succeeding stages though with a somewhat reduced margin. Richmonds have never run a very strong team and Whitecross (No. 1), experienced no difficulty in disposing of them. The men from Milner-street always held the upper hand and though they suffered several reverses their progress was never seriously checked and they won by 59 points.</p>
        <p>The only match in the second round was bet‘vaen Pearson and Knowles and Greenings and the issue of this also was never in doubt. The first two games were evenly contested but from this stage P. and K. carried all before them and ran out winners by 43.</p>
        <p>After a season's inactivity, the Warrington z. nd Dis- trict Association resumed operations on Saturday. This year it. had been decided to conduct the competition on the knock-out principle and the first round was °petted last week-end. In two of the matches the result was somewhat of a foregone conclusion, as Bull's Head and Cemetery possess teams of more than average strength and the probability is that both will figure in the final, unless they meet earlier. Bull's Head had the hardest task of all the teams in facing Sankey Chapel House, but they surrived, 23 points separating the teams. Cemetery were called upon to oppose the Green Dragon, Heatley, and were easy victors. Two new teams—new to membership of the Association that is—ttied con- clusions and ass result Rylands' representatives reported progress to the second round at the expense of Earles- town Institute. The match was the most exciting of the two, as up to half way only a few points separated the teams and these were claimed by Earlestown. Rylands's however,commenced to assert themselves and a minority of 19 was before the close turned into a majority of 16.</p>
        <p>The Junior Conserratives had a busy time on Satur- day as in addition to a bowling main the competition for the Challenge Cup presented by Lady Greenall was also concluded. In this the prize went to R. Tilling who bowled exceedingly well. In the main the victory rested with W. Skelhorn who in the final defeated W. Worsley 13-10. after an exciting game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Swimming.</head>
        <p>Swimming.</p>
        <p>The Warrington v. Everton match was played on Wednesday at Everton and resulted in a win for the homesters by 2 goals to none. Ihe Warrington team were very unfortunate in losing as they had as much of the play as Everton. In the first half R. Allen scored for Warrington but the point was disallowed.ln the second half Skinner scored twice for Everton and when the whistle blew the result was Everton 2, Warrington nil</p>
        <p>The teams meet again on July 17th iu the 2nd round of the Northern Counties Competition and the lo:al team hope to rise to the occasion and avenge their defeat.</p>
        <p>There should be a good attendance on Tuesday-even- ing next at the Baths whoa Warrington and Bacup meet in the Ist round of the Lancashire County Cup. The teams have never met I)sfore but the visitors have a good reputation and should provide the spectators with a very interesting game.</p>
        <p>The reserve team are also engaged the same evening a match having been arranged with Wavertree. This will be played before the Ist team match.</p>
        <p>• The following entries have been received for the Works Squadron Competition: Pearson and Knowles, Rylands, Monks Hall, Fletcher Russell and Co., Post Office, Corporation, P. Walker and Sons, Sankoy Wire Mills, Richmonds, Co-operative Stores and Crosfields. The first round will be drawn for on Tuesday evening next and due notice given when the engagements have to be fulfilled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The Chaplain's Salary.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. W. Houghton presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Wilkinson, Mrs. Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. M. Ryan, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, J. Billinge, W. Gill, J. Evans, W. Flannagan, M. W. Seton, W. C. Brooks, W. Furness, R. Bolton, S. Davies, A. Brooks, J. Backhouse, W. Stanier, G. Mather, T. Ritchie, E. L. Bent, P. Longtone. S. Sutton (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master). and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools). The ( lerk (Mr. J. C. Sutton) and Dr. Murray were attending the meetings of the Rural District Councils Association in London.</p>
        <p>TRADESMEN AND PROVISION TICKETS. ARE ORDERS FAIRIX DISTRIBOTEb ?</p>
        <p>Mrs. SIMPSON called attention to the system of deal- ing with tickets' given to people to enable them to get provipions. She asked why the people who got the tickets could not negotiate with tradesmen in their own districts who often had to supply them with food without payment. The ASSISTANT CLIIRK said the matter was in the hands of the relieving officers. He understood the orders generally went to the nearest shops. Mr. AUKLAND (relieving officer) said the tickets were given for negotiation with the shopkeepers who were nearest to the residences of applicants. Mrs. SIMPSON : Shopkeepers have complained to me that people often run up a big bill with them and then when they get a ticket they must go to some other shop in town. Mr. AUKLAND thought that was an argument against Mrs. Simpson's suggestion, as ten to one the tickets would go towards paying off the accounts and the people would not get relief. The CHAIRMAN : Have you ever known anything to go wrong in that way? Mr. AUKIAND : NO, only some people will ask for things that are not specified on the tickets. Mr. EVANS maintained that Mr. Aukland's statement reflected considerably on the tradespeople. To suggest that there were tradespeople in the town who would receive the tickets and refuse to supply the goods,taking the money towards the reduction of a debt, was a very serious statement to make. Mr. AUKLAND : I Said they might do. Mr. Evan : I see no necessity for such a statement to be made. Mr. GARNATT (relieving officer) said that as a rule he had found a difficulty in getting tradespeople to take the trouble to deal with the tickets. He had had tickets sent back to him for that reason. When they found tradesmen who were willing to take the trouble and keep things square, they gave them the preference—if there was any. If those tradesmen's shops happened to be a long distance from the applicant's house, they gave an order on the person suggested by the applicant. In answer to Mr. LONGTON, Mr. thieserr said people cOuld have tickets for any shops they liked, if they made a special request. The CHAIRMAN: Is there any improvement in the system that you can suggest? Mr. GARNETT : I don't know that there is. his. SIMPSON said her desire was that those people who cared, to supply the goods should have a share in . the orders for their own districts. Mr. GARNETT : If they make a request to deal with some particular person it will be granted. In the absence of that request the order goes to the general dealer. The CHAIRMAN said the question must be raised again if anything else tranepired. WALKING DAY. It was decided to give the children at Padgate schools the usual treat on Walking Day. HOUSE COMMITTEE. CHAPLAIN'S SALARY INCREASED. At a meeting of the House Committee the applica- tion of the Chaplain (Rev. W. Bracecamp) for an increase of his stipend was again considered, and event- ually it was carried to recommend to the Board that the same be advanced from £5O to .£75 per annum, in recognition of his valuable services. The night engineer's decease having created a vacancy ; it was decided to continue the services of Joseph Baxter in that capacity at a weekly wage of 28s. The resignations of Charge Nurse Sugden and Probationer Nurse Nadin were accepted. The lady Guardians and matron were appointed to deal with future vacancies. The examina- tion of three years nurses was fixed for June 13 tb, Dr. Hodgkinson to be the examiner and to receive a fee of three guineas. It was recommended that Engineer Brobbins' wages be advanced from 38s. to 405., in con- sideration of his 18 years' services. Mrs. Crowe's services as night nurse during the epidemic were retained for an additional four weeks.— A letter was read from the Secretary *of the Poor ' Law Association and it was recommended that the Clerk should reply that the Guardians did not think it expedient at present to make application to the Central Midwives Board for recognition as a train- ing school for midwives of the Warrington Union Infirmary, but that the Guardians cordially support any combined effort for securing from the Central Mid- wives Board more general recognition of efficiently organised large Union Infirmaries as training schools for midwives. Mr. BILLINGE in moving the confirmation of the minutes said they had received the practically unani- mous vote of the House Committee. The question of the increase of salary to their esteemed chaplain had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>been carefully considered. Those who were in a position to speak definitely upon the subject thought ' Mr. Bracecamp was entitled to an advance of EU. ' Some thought he was certainly entitled to £3O, whilst others suggested £2O. The committee, after reviewing the work of the chaplain for the last seven or eight ' years and going fully into the matter of the spiritual needs of the inmates and also of the aptitude with which he had discharged the duties, came before the Board with the modest proposal of £25. It was not, however, intended that the £75 should be the salary at 'which his successor would commence. iSome members perhaps had in mind the fact that in t certain moderately large Unions the chaplains gave their services, but on the other hand those Unions generally contributed something to the chaplain's Church in the shape of pew rents. The Warrington Union made no such contribution. ; Mr. MaTttea seconded. 1 Mr. Frtatzess moved that the proposed increase of salary to the chaplain be referred back. He did so, he said, in no antagonistic spirit to the chaplain or any particular denomination. He had a great admiration for the chaplain as a worker in that institution and as a general worker in the town, and he ventured to think that admiration was shared by every member of the Foard. No tangible reason or evidence had, however, been given to the Committee as to why a 50 per , cent increase should be granted. The increase, he contended, was out of all proportion. He asked the• Guardians as businessmen whether they would raise the • The annual general meeting of the Northern Rugby salary of another official 50 per cent without very Union was held in Manchester on Tuesday evening. substantial reasons. Another reason why he objected, The report and balance sheet showed a balance in hand was because this sort of thing grew by what it fed of £1,534. upon. They had no guarantee, if Mr. Bracecamp left On the motion of Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes) it was agreed tha