yer knocks on or procedure was in accordance with the Standing Orders, throws the ball forward, any player of the opposite side Mr. EVANS : That is evidence that some Standing may play the ball; but should he or any player of his Orders need amending. own side play the ball it shall at once be brought back Mr. W. C. BROOKS said that when a similar proposi- to where such knock on or throw foiward took place, tion was made some time ago a vote was taken for and against. Several members were not inclined to vote, and there put down and scrummaged. If the ball or any player running with the ball touches the referee, it and the Chairman asked them to leave the room and shall there be put down and scrummaged. A player another vote was afterwards taken, who accidentally knocks the ball on shall not be The ASSISTANT CLERK said it would need a special penalised if he obtains possession before it falls to the resolution, apart from the passing of the minutes, to ground." confirm the advance. The proposal was adopeed unanimously. The CHAIRMAN said that those who did not intend to THE GENERAL CGMMITTEE. vote should leave their seats. A meeting was also held of the General Committee. Mr. EVANS submitted that the amendment was not lost, as the procedure was not in accordance with the Mr. J. B. Cooke presided. The sum of £B3 19s. was S voted to the Lancashire County Football Union on Standing Orders. Those who did not wish to vote behalf of junior and schools foofball. The following should have left their seats, were appointed as a special sub-committee to carry out The ASSISTANT CLERK said the Standing Order was all the arrangements for the visit of the New as follows :—" In taking the vote upon the question, Zealanders :—Messrs. H. Ashton (Warrington), J. W. those members of the Board shall be entitled to vote who are in their seats in the Board room when the Wood (Leeds), J. B. Cooke (Wakefield) J. Platt , (Oldham), J. H. Smith (Widnes), and W D. Lyon (Hull) question is put fro