        <p>lord of the Red Lion Inn, for permission to make certain structural alterations to the premises, which belong to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Company. It was found, said Mr. Browne, that the kitchen was too small and the owners were prepared to enlarge it for domestic purposes only and make the back of the premises consistent with it. The kitchen was quite , private.—Superintendent Ennion, on behalf of the Chief : Constable of the County, objected,as there was a cottage adjoining, and anyone living in it could enter the licensed premises without going on the highway.—Mr. Browne : The cottage is occupied by a man and his wife. There are no children.—After some discussion it , was decided to adjourn the application for a month to see if the difficulty could be overcome by making other arrangements.—The Chairman said the objection stated by the police was a grave one.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I have declared the minutes carried and will take the responsibility, . -</p>
        <p>• Mr. BILLING E then formally moved that the advance be granted. Mr. MATHF.R seconded, and said he believed that with the exception of the Clerk, Mr. Bracecamp was the only official of the institution who had not had an advance during the six years that he had been connected with it Men of exceptional ability ought to be paid accord- ingly. Mr. Braeeoamp was undoubtedly a specialist in his profession, and the Board ought not to be close fisted in dealing with the increase. The resolution was put to the meeting, and the CHAIR- MAN declared that the Ayes" had It.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ftrwass asked for a show of hands but the chairman declined to revert to the matter, as he said it had been thoroughly thrashed out by the House Com- mittee and there was no doubt as to the vote of the Board being in favour of the increase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>VACCINATION.</head>
        <p>VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>The ASsISTANT CLERIC reported the receipt of a circular letter and general Order amending the Vaccination Order, 1898. As the order came into O practical force on ctober Ist, an adjonrnment of dis- cussion thereon was desirable. Mr. JOLLEY said that unless the Guardians came to an arrangement for a revised scale of fees by October Ist the opportunity would be lost and the power would pass into the bands of the Local Government Board. He suggested the appointment of a committee to con- fer with the public, vaccinators so that definite action might be taken. Mr. EveNs seconded and it was decided to appoint the following committee :—The Chairman (representing the Rural District), Mr. Jolley (Warrington), Mr. Billinge (Haydock), and Dr. Patterson (Newton).</p>
        <p>THE COMPENSATION ACT. The ASSISTANT CLERK reported that a complete list of the officers and servants employed by the Guardians, with particulars of salary and remuneration, bad been sent to each insurance company and agent applying for same. He now produced the tenders. The tenders were referred to a committee consisting of Messrs. Jolley, Furness and Evans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>STOCKT9N HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKT9N HEATH.</p>
        <p>A CONSOINNTIOUS OBJECTOR.-At the Daresbnry Petty Sessions on Tuesday Arthur Spencer, Stockton Heath, applied for a certificate exempting his child from vaccination.—Mr. H. Greenell (magistrates' clerk): What is your objection ?—Applicant: It is a hard thing to define,but I have had other children vaccinated with ill effects. It is a sure fact that vaccination is detrimental to a child's health.—Mr. Greenall : Did a doctor tell you vaccination had done your child harm? —Applicant: No, sir; I saw it without.—The Bench granted the application.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS' TRIP . —The school teachers at St. Thomas's Suuday School had their annual trip on Saturday and a most enjoyable outing it proved. About 42 teachers and friends met at thc Square at 7.20 a.m. and proceeded by 'bus to the Central Station. The party then journeyed on the 7.50 train to Bakewell, and on arrival lunch was partaken of at the Wheat Sheaf Hotel. They afterwards had a 20 miles drive and great pleasure was derive from inspecting Chatsworth House and Haddon Hall. Matlock Bath was also visited, and tea was served at the Albion Hotel. The party returned home by rail from Matlock Bath, after having spent a very enjoyable day. Ideal weather was experienced. THE RED LION INN.—Mr. Browne made application at the Petty Sessions on Tuesday, on behalf of the land-</p>
        <p>STRIKING POSTERS For Sunday School Anniversaries, Concerts, Balls, SoiAes, Meetings, Sales by Auction, &amp;0., &amp;c., in one or more colours, on white or coloured paper, in the most striking type, size 60" x 40" in one sheet, JOHN WALKER AND Co. EXAMINER OFFICE WARRINGTON.</p>
