      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>THE, NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE, NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING-PLAYING RULES REVISED</p>
        <p>Widnes F.G. Meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Smith and the New Zealander's</p>
        <p>Visit</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Widnes Football Club was held on Monday evening at the Grosvenor Hotel, when the report and balinoo choot warA rpqrl and adopted. The income for the season was £670 17s. 5d., and the expenditure £S9S Gs. 2d., including £l5O adverse balance [ from the previous season. On the general statement [of assets and liabilities there was a deficiency of £3l 2s. 3d.</p>
        <p>The President and. vice-presidents were re-elected. Mr. F. W. Smith was unanimously re-elected secretary. Mr. F. W. Wright was unanimously chosen again as hon. treasurer, and Mr. J. C. Evans was re-appointed finan- cial secretary. It was docided to leave the appoint- ment of a captain to the committee. It was decided to re-appoint nine of the retiring committee and to co-opt three other members, bring- ing the number of the committee up to 12. The com- mittee consists of Messrs. J. Barber, S. Laws, J. Littler, F. Hampson, J. Hardman, C. Mather, J. H. Smith, R. Jones, T. W. Wright, E. Willsams, B. Baker; S. G. Owen, J. H. Malam and the two secretaries ex-officio. INTERESTING SPEECHES. A BRIGHTER OUTLOOK.—NEW ZEALANDERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>COMING.</head>
        <p>COMING.</p>
        <p>In returning thanks for his re-appointment as hon. treasurer, the chairman (Mr. Wright) said that if he had been asked three months ago regarding the future of the Widnes club he would have been very pessimistic. Things bad happened, however, which augured a much brighter future for Northern Union football in Widnes. (Hear, hear.) The club, in the first place, was practic- ally assured of a first class list of fixtures, and a match for every Saturday of the season. Those who had stood by the club had been much gratified by the way Wigan, Warrington and Runcorn had helped to get them out or the mire. The committee were hoping to get junior players sufficiently enthusiastic to form an "A ' team. A New Zealand team was to visit this country, and through the generosity of the Athletic Club who had paid their rent in advance, and the generosity also of a lady and gentleman, Widnes had been able to provide the necessary guarantee for a match with the New Zealanders. (Applause). Mr. J. Smith said there was no doubt that the Club had passed through a dark time. He bad never looked upon professional Rugby football in Widnes as hopeless for he saw clearly that for the decline in interest last Iseason there was a reason to be found in the lack of fixtures. Up till the time-of blank dates the club gate receipts last season were better than the previous season They were now absolutely assured of a list of fixtures, and the reconstruction of the League which had been decided upon ensured the team meeting every club of standing in Lancashire and four Yorkshire Clubs also. Some people seemed very sceptical regarding the team which was coming from New Zealand. At the beginning of the negotiations the Northern Union were asked by the New Zealanders not to publish the names of those coming,for naturally these players desireo to be allowed to take part in the international games. The Northern Union wanted to see that the team they were to offer a guarantee to was a strong one, and the names were given to the president and secretary of the Union in confidence. Those gentlemen had assured the Union that the team would be a very strong one indeed. The question of the Welsh clubs might have great develop- ments. Two Welsh clubs had already applied, and he believed that half-a-dozen or a dozen were prepared to come over any time. The Union would have to be assured that any club they took in was a good club. He believed that there was a bright future before Northern Union football, and in Widnes he believed there were hundreds who still preferred Rugby football to Association. He believed that at that meeting twelve months hence they would have a far different story to tell than this year. (Applause.) In reply to a question, Mr. Smith said he under- stood 25 men were coming from New Zealand. Of these 21 names had been sent—and every one was the name of an International. The team would be over about the middle of September, and would play the weaker teams first, in order to acclimatise themselves, and get into the new methods easily. He did not think any attempts- which were being made to prevent the men coming were likely to be successful. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>OLDHAM'S RECORD YEAR.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM'S RECORD YEAR.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union club at Oldham have had a recerd season, their gate receipts amounting to £3,858, which is the highest since the Northern Union was formed, the nearest being £3,670 in 1900-1. The total receipts were £4,423 155., and the handsome profit of £605 4s. id. was made on the season. Such a flowishing state of affairs has never previously been known in the hiStory of the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>PROFIT AT LEIGH.</head>
        <p>A PROFIT AT LEIGH.</p>
        <p>The Leigh Northern Union club last season made a profit of over £7O.</p>
      </div>
