        <p>BRIDLINGTON.—Aingirth BOARDING MOUS); ; sea view. Special terms, May and June.—Miss MORGAN.</p>
        <p>It appears to us that far too THE NEW pessimistic a view has been SITUATION. taken of the new manifesto of the Irist party. In point of fact, it is only such a declaration as might have been expected under existing circumstances. It is as well understood by Irish members as by the other parties in Parliament that there can be no new Home Rule Bill during the existence of the present House of Commons. The General Election was conducted on the basis of that understanding, and the Government will not,of course, break faith with" the predominant partner," any more than it would have broken faith witl4 Ireland if it had promised a Home Rule Bill. But it is unreasonable to expect the Nationalist Party to acquiesce in the situation. That party stands for Home Rule, and nothing short of Home Rule. It expects for Ireland what we have given with so generous a hand to the Transvaal and to the Orange Free State. That it should continue to declare this to be the case is merely natural, and nothing else was to be expected. Nothing else that is to sr.y, except a desire to make the best of existing conditions during a period throughout which it is impossible to approach the only possible, legislative solution of Irish grievances.</p>
        <p>The rejection of the Irish Council Bill did not come upon us as a surprise. But, though it was not a surprise, it was a disappointment. The Government and the country had been led by Mr. JOHN REDMOND to hope that Mr. BIRRELfs pro- posals would be accepted. The Irish Council Bill was not, in any sense whatever, put forward as a settlement. Nor, on the other hand, was it offered as a sop to Cerberus. It was an interim, measure, intended to ease in some degree an admittedly difficult situation. It. offered to Irishmen a much larger share in the control of their own affairs ; but it was never for a moment looked upon by the Government as a possible substitute for Home Rule. It was a step towards Home Rule, indeed, not a political device, intended to stave off the Irish demand. Mr. REDMOND knew this quite well, and there is every reason to suppose that he would have accepted Mr. BIRRELVS proposals, with some slight modifications, perhaps, if there had been Any likelihood that they would be accepted by the Irish Convention.</p>
        <p>However, a new condition of things has arisen in Ireland, which may eventually crush even so able and zealous a leader as Mr. Redmond. The new movement would put an end to Parliamentary action altogether. It would withdraw the repre- sentatives of Ireland from Westminster. It would settle pending controvetsies according to some curious rule of thumb. It is a nrwernent that Mr. RHDMOND may or may ri,;t. a,,:rPe with, hat it is certainly a iTlOVBinaut that he caittiot ignore. If one of its results should be the adoption of such a policy as seems now to be contemplated in con-</p>
