      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>nection with Jarrow, there will be no regret on Our own part if the Irish forces are drawo oft' in English elections. We shall be in a position to settle our differences among ourselves. That will be all the good under existing conditions. The Irish psrty, in and out of Parliament,as we cannot Conceal from our ourselves, is to a large extent the Occasion of the present most irritating phase of the education question.</p>
        <p>Without anticipating the THE PREMIER important pronouncement AND THE LORDS. which he is to make iti Parliament on the 24th inst the Premier spoke very plainly and explicitly at 'Plymouth last Friday night on the burning 'question of the Lords. He made no hesitation in avowing the firm determination of himself and his Party to settle the issue with the House of L .rds in such a fashion as cviU "define the limits of the Power which the Constitution has conferred on the House of Lords." The situation, he said, was a. climax to the serious attacks on the rights and liberties of the Commons, and even if the immedi- ate cause of the deadlock were withdrawn, the Oevernment were bound to go forward, Liberals could not blame themselves for lack of forbearance but they could not go on kissing the rod. The time had gone by for comprornisings. The Hemse of Lords must understand that whilst the C.irn- teens were perfectly ready to legislate with due deliberation, and to give every weight to their representations, the British people must be master in its own house. The Government meant to hand down their heritage enlarged and secure to the citizens of the future. In a letter dated June 12, the Borough Member, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, alludes to the rrime Minister's "able and very important speech " at the Liberal Federatimn meetings at I)lYmouth. He adds: "I have heard Sir Henry I:sake a number of speeches at different times, but Teither in point of matter, or spirited delivery, 'lave I ever listened to weightier, more effective rin every way more admirable addresses than those he delivered in the Holborn Restaurant in Ate latter part of April, and more recently still in nchester. Month after month the respect and ;deed affection felt for Mill in the Party grows i'rouger and stronger, and with it there is a i feel- 3g,ef deep satisfaction amongst Liberal and aaeur Members who represent all the best Pr. ssiVe forces in the country, that the leadership cur great historic party should be in the hand; ut sci courageous, honest, capable and strenuous a statesman.,</p>
        <p>Referring to a newly-issued MESSRS. CROSFIELD book on the smoke AND SONS nuisance—" The Destrnc- AND THE tion of Daylight," by Mr. SMOKE NUISANCE. John W. Graham, M.A., principal of Dalton Hall, _trniversity of Manchester —the" Liverpool Post and alercury " says ;—The practical mannfacturer, Itaxions to mitigate the evil, will turn with interest to the chapter on " Some Experiences," in which three firms detail their experience of, the special Ineasuros taken to make their works free from .11111°k% The tree firms are Cadbury Brothers, at ournville ; Rowntree and Co.' at York; and Joseph Crosfield and Sons,at "Warrington. All three firms have succeeded in making their Chimneys perfectly smokeless, and the most I,:,,lnarkable feature in connection with Messrs. 'rosfield's example is that practical smokelessness lalnd economy have been achieved together. They eft," a row of twenty-three boilers fitted with 1:4(111g stokers, and they achieve smokelessness eearlY all the time, and save a thousand tons of t2til per week, or £25,000 a year, compared with ir unregenerate days. This sum includes f:A:inomies on labour and in prolonging the lives of Manufacturers will not embark upon ' tkPelasive schemes of smoke prevention unless I "B'9 can see a more tangible return than a clearer' atm() ez sPhere ; but in face of Messrs. Crosfield's , tillriments, it would seem that clean chimneys sotto be attended by larger dividends—surely a 'eient incentive t,o any sound business man.</p>
        <p>The prospects for the %Inas ITNIoN Northern Union game have PooTsALL. brightened steadily during the last twelve months, the number of clubs in allegiance to the 'lton does not increase. Still there are good grolltltis for optimism. Last season the game Itself found more favour in the eyes of the public, Vith the result that gates were better and Club tevenues healthier. The greatest drawback was the constitution of the League, which has been 7 atlended so that all clubs will now be assured a lull fixture list, while the competition for the leadership will be a more real and genuine one lan heretofore. Each club will play all the other Clubs in its own county, and at least four from ~r 01313 the border. This alone should. keep up ~`ae interest, but there are bright signs much f.t.!.ther afield. A professional movement in South viales is gaining ground,and the sham amateurism Stands a fair chance of being exposed. Aberdare have already signified their intention to come over rld have secured promises of fixtures from all the eliding Northern Union clubs. Their example feature have far-reaching effects. Another gratifying already being boomed for the 1907-8 aeason is a visit of the "All Blacks "—perhaps not le identical team which slaughtered Rugby Union so unmercifully, but a strong side of New Zealand footballers who are willing to enter the Professional fold. At the meeting of the Widnes lub this week Mr. J. H. Smith, one of the best Ilcm'n men in the Northern Union, expressed e?tfidence in the tour being carried out. He said that 25 players were coming over, and satisfactory 41hrbefl had been given to the Union as a guarantee t the quality of the visiting team. Should both a ee Welsh and New Zealand invasions materialise iqerefore, Northern Union football will be more Interesting than ever and ought to obtain a still rner grip on the public of Lancashire and -1- orkshire. We are most anxious to see the Rugby at holding its own in this part of the country *lu least, and much depends on the way it is played 114c1 governed.</p>
        <p>Of the minor benefits o LORD RoSIBERY AND fame surely none is greater CECIL RHODES. at the present day than that of having deserved a post- 1111Mous eulogy by Lord Rosebery. English httOry_a decaying force in present-day life—is Perserved, some think exclusively, in that elusive litatesman. It is not merely that Lord Rosebery ,t3llllnands supreme literary graces, or that is style 2 Perfect. Be has the large mind which appre- ;qttes greatness in others, even greatness that 1:1, blurred by many faults and obscured by ;all memory of a great mistake. Cecil Rhodes has gone a little out of British 181nory. South Africa is pacified, Kruger is tll3, and the old unpleasant things, not so old ther when time alone is the test, make no demand "Pc% the daily attention of the public. But in the 153 Laces where the great intellects of the race are ~,ortured Rhodes is not forgotten. Oxford and I;4lllPire were his two great affections. For one he Ocrificed everything, to the other he gave all that ~.18 years of labour had accumulated; but they vet- • ta inseparable in his mind. Lord Rosebery, his speech on unveiling the Rhodes tablet the Examination Schools of the University, 127 bow Rhodes designed to do his work of b,.ll.lting the Empire in one great federation, and aging the separated sections of the Anglo-Saxon hlca together through the great University which Played so large a part in English history. The oeaa,l—large and imaginative beyond the grasp, 4irlaaPs, of ordinary men—was ever present in ot,ades's thoughts. It was his companion while ers played or talked the commonplaces of exist.. Cdca; For it he laboured—not, as Lord Rosebery "rafted, always with too great scruple, but .4.„9.; With impersonal and whole-hearted devotion. inT-1 When he came near the end of his short life di? Which had been crowded so great events, he iau what be could to ensure that his cherished iutleal should not be lost, but should be ever present cotbe great English school of learning to inspire r,7,ere to follow his lead, and to work for the 19Ziaation of a dream before the magnitude of tllle_,ll even the Napoleonic ambition seems puny. hi:ues was one of the great men of Empire. fAn„ Tient will stand bide that of Clive in the 110418 of the race. Both had their faults, but 01 atoned for them nobly.</p>
        <p>Lord Halifax and a number ANGLICAN PASSIVE of other Anglicans, mostly RESISTANCE. of the English Chur.;h Union type, addressed a letter to the Prime Minister in which thy threatened to resort to Passive Resistance if Mr. McKenna's Bill was passed. They are a god many years too late in raising a conscientious objection to the payment of rates for simple Biblical teaching. They have been paying such rates without a protest for more than thirty years, and the very teaching they no , denounce has been praised by three Archbishops ' of Canterbury in succession. The case of Nonconformist Passive Resisters is entirely differ- ent. By the Act of 1902, which was passed by a ' Tory Parliament at the bidding of the bishops and convocations, for the first time in our history sectarian teaching was thrown on the rates. An Archbishop declared that simple Biblical teaching was in great part the teaching of the Church. Certainly there is nothing hostile to the Anglican Church in such teaching. But the religious teach- ing in Anglican and Catholic day schools is openly and exclusive sectarian and hostile to other forms of faith. It is worthy of note that the High Churchmen who made this protest grudgingly admit that Non- conformists have a grievance under the Act of 1902. They alcogether ignored this grievance at the time, and openly derided those who, like Dr. Fairbairn, said : "We will never submit." Then their only answer was : "We will make you." It is for Churchmen generally, and especially for the Bishops, to say whether these gentlemen really represent the mind of the Anglican Church when they set themselves against the use of the Bible in the schools. They would kick it out if they could, to use the elegant phrase of Tory platforms. Should politicians, weary of ecclesiasticit strife, fall back upon the secular solution, Free Church- men will not be alarmed, but what says the Arch- bishop of Canterbury ?</p>
        <p>W. HODGKINSOS LTD.</p>
        <p>LADIES' TAILORING.</p>
        <p>EXPERT WORE. COSTUMES FROM 3 GUINEAS. BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>There is one law for the rich and one for the poor. .liessrs. Christie have just sold an Auk's egg, 200 years old for ;£370, whilst Bridget Jennings has been fined 20s. for selling a hen's egg that was laid three weeks ago. o _ _ _</p>
        <p>We hope every reader will send a donation, however small, to the officials of the Poor Children's Summer Camp fund, whose letter we publish in another column. The cause of the little ones is ever dear, and no one can begrudge the coin that will give some poor slum-dwelling child a holiday at the seaside.</p>
        <p>The smoke nuisance is not only a menace to health, but threatens also to destroy our buildings. Even if scientific skill and energy are able to suppress the visibility or opacity of smoke it still remains to eliminate the products of combustion of the sulphur which is contained in more or less quantity in all ordinary coal.—" Lancet." e§</p>
        <p>It seems a pity that sAne of the Cheshire police have nothing batter to do than take up stations on the Chester main road, st,ip-watches in hand, in order to trap unwary motorists who may be exceeding the legal speed limit on some tempting stretch. If we were a burglar we should take the opportunity of looting a few dwellings in order to convince the men in blue that they have more essential duties to perform in the intcrests of the public. o o _ _</p>
        <p>The Rose Queen festival at Padgate Cottage Homes was a delightful function and its success must have been gratifying to the staff who had devoted so much time to its preparation. The youngsters entered into the affair with zest and looked the pictures of happiness. The day must indeed have been a red letter one to them, and we hope the event will only be the precursor of many more of these old English festivals which are only too rare in these utilitarian days.</p>
        <p>We hear - that the new organ at Bold-street Wesleyan Church will bs opened early in July and the occasion is being looked forward to with great interest. The organ will be unique locally, as it is to be replete with the most modern improvements. Arrangements have been made for recitals by Dr. Kendrick Pyne, the Manchester city organist, Mr. David Clegg, another famous performer who is at present on tour in America, and Mr. W. H. Payton, Mils. Bac., our well-known local organist. * * * . _</p>
        <p>"if it be true," says a correspondent, " that the stamp of genius can only be set upon a batsman when he invents a new stroke, TvWesley's claim must be considered indisputable. One of the Manchester evening papers, in describing his recent big innings, says : Then Tyldesley, feeling greater freedom and less responsibility, hit a brace of lovely fours by the the o shrdl cmf vb,g otg shrdl—cmfy.—There is something very "comfy "about the final spasm.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield, M.P., in the course of an important letter which we print on page 8, combats the assertion of some critics that the Government is "doing nothing." He writes interestingly on what has been already accomplished and indicates what he expects to see carried through during the current legislative year. The other day we were speaking to a Socialist, and we asked him why he had so much criticism of the Liberals to offer. His reply was 'Because they are the only party which ig doing anything." Here we have the opposite view, though it was not expressed with the idea of con- veying a compliment. * * * The many friends of the Rev. Dr. Mercer, formerly ' of St. Mary's, Warrington, will be interested to hear of him in the role of a " navvy-rector.,' At Lostock Hall, rear Preston, the congregation at St. Paulinus Church, of which this genial priest has charge, are working every evening upon the house they are erect- ing in connection with the Father Turner Memorial Hall. Dr. Mercer, who superintends operations, is attired in old clothes and a pair of clogs. He takes his stand alongside other workers, and wields pick and shovel as if he were a brawny labourer by occupa- tion. * *_</p>
        <p>Why do clergymen dress in black? is a simple question which the majority of people would find it difficult to answer readily. It is related that in the year 1524 Luther laid aside the monk's costume, and henceforth dressed according to the fashion of the day. He chose black clothes, and, consequently, that colour has become the fashion of the clergy. His reason for choosing this colour was that the Elector of Saxony took an interest in him, and now and then sent him a piece of black cloth,being at that time the Court fashion ; and, because Luther preferred it, his scholars thought it incumbent upon them to imitate the dress of their master. From that time black has been constantly worn by the clergy. * * *</p>
        <p>Energy is of more value than cleverness.—AnNoin. It is safer to hear and take counsel than to give it.— Tuomas A KEMPIS.</p>
        <p>Woman possesses naturally the same rights that men do. If she does not eujoy them, someone is a robber.—Du. FOOTE.</p>
        <p>The test of a man or woman's breeding is how they behave in a quarrel. Anybody can behave well when things are going smoothly. —GEORGE BERNARD Slimy. If the gods have no sense of humour they must weep a great deal—JOHN OLIVER HOBBES. Each man should learn what is within him, that he may strive to mend; he must be taught what is with- out him, that he may be kind to others.—ROßEßT LOUIS STEVENSON.</p>
        <p>* There will doubtless be some scheme devised soon that, instead of gentlemen having to give up playing cricket after a year or so in order to settle down to some business or profession, they may be able to devote years, instead of holiiays only, to the game ; for the game played merely by professionals would I soon result in its total extinction. There is no more + incongruity in a gentleman being remunerated for devoting his time and gifts to the game than there t is in gentlemen writing about the game and receiving ; remuneration for it There is the bare fact that there are only two or three men who have money who are worth their place in in a county eleven; and moderate players are now no good in representative cricket any i more than they are n representative football—V. le. S. Crawford in" London Opinion."</p>
        <p>• -</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone has told some of his musical friends at Leeds (says the London correspondent of the " Liverpool Post and Mercury " ) that there is every prospect that the ban on The Mikado " will be removed. There is now no doubt that it was mainly imposed in view of the visit of Prince Fushimi' though it is said that the Prince himself was highly diverted with the fuss which had been made over the matter. Mr. Gladstone suggested that the name should be altered and one or two excisions made. If that were done, he thought that there would be no difficulty in getting the ban removed. With regard to the title, Mr. Gilbert would probably make no objection, but he has hitherto declined to listen to the idea of cutting anything out of the piece. It is satisfactory to know that there is every prospect that it will soon be possible again to play a very slightly altered "Mikado."</p>
        <p>Pageants are in the air just now. " With the object of assisting in the development of this praiseworthy local patriotism, anl kindling the pride of race," Sub Rosa," in the" Morning Leader " who is one of the candidates for Jarrow) has jotted down " a few historical notes which may come in useful for the organisers of pageants in the more backward com- munities." At Widnes, he says,the tableaux should be peculiarly pungent. And these suggestions take us back to the very essence of things : I. —Otto of Roses first manufactured at Widnes. 11. —Baron Randall of Old Drurie sings " It ain't all lavender." 111. —Hugo de Widnes discovers chloride of lime. IV.—Great joust between Sir Watteau de Nyffe, the Lancashire champion, and Lord Eau de Cologne. Sir Watteau, the victor is granted the right to bear the three smelts, niffy, on his shield. V.—Processional chant of perfumers, dustmen, and essence peddlers. Air : " What though the spicy breezes."</p>
        <p>The officials of the Manchester Order of Operative Bricklayers are anxious that their Union should be more widely patronized,and held a propaganda meeting in Manchester this week. In their propaganda the society point out that "the pen, the sword and the trowel are three grett factors in the nation's life, but the greatest of all fs the trowel." The point of view is obviously largely dependent on which of the three implements one wields. While we have much respect for the sturdy bricklayer and the substantial and last- ing quality of his achievements, yet we are obliged to maintain that he is somewhat egotistical in claiming that the nation's life has been principally moulded by the trowel. * * *</p>
        <p>There is a certain amount of humour —conscious or unconscious —in the letter from the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton on the House of Lords. The rev, gentleman, whom we have good reason to presume is an admirer of this fine old crusted institution, points out that in 1648 the Commons abolished ihe Lords and assumed complete control in order that they might bring about the execution of King Charles. The methods were about as summary as the executfon, and we are inclined to agree with Mr. Carleton when he concludes by saying : "It is well to recur to this, the only prece- dent which our history furnishes for our guidance in the matter." It would be sad indeed if we had to curtail the Lords by cutting off their heads! * * *</p>
        <p>The Conservatives of Ne-wton and St. Helens have attacked and slandered Mr. J. A. Seddon M.P., at every opportunity since he won the Newton Division.. It is just possible therefore that be took a little mali- cious pleasure in" spoiling their gime" last Saturday by preventing the attendance of the Lancashire Hussars at a Primrose League fete. A word to the Secretary for War seems to have sufficed to prevent this misuse of his Majesty's forces. Mr. Bottomley, the Conservative agent for Newton Division, was par- ticularly bitter in his comments on Mr. Seddon's " tit for tat." He forgets that he has spilled a lot of ink in his efforts to belittle Mr. Seddon in the eyes, of the electors. Even a Labour M.P., is not a lamb!</p>
        <p>* * * • There have been many evasions of the three-mile limit as applied to the sale of intoxicants on Sunday, but none more barefaced than the one which occupied the attention of the magistrates at Daresbury Sessions 'on Tuesday. We have heard of men elsewhere taking the train to some suburb and then posing as bona fiat travellers because they had come three miles. That was a sufficiently impudent fraud, but here the men had not even travelled three miles according to law. They entered the "Cat and Lion," Stretton, on Sunday May 12th, and were supplied with liquor, two of them representing that they had come from Liverpool and producing a return ticket to that city, while the third gave Warrington as the place of stoppage the previous night. All three belonged to Stockton Heath, and it was their merited misfortune to be caught in the act by a police officer who knew them. The result was a prosecution, supported by the Licensed ,Vietualler's Association on behalf of the publican, and a fine of 10s. and costs each. It was a good "try on," but the drink was hardly worth the cost.</p>
        <p>Success to the Infirmary bazaar, and fete! We hope the inhabitants of Warrington and district will flock in their thousands to Hill Cliffe on Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, and Saturday next for this great event. The cause is a noble one, and as the extension rapidly progressess the managers of the institution become all the more anxious that it shall be opened free of debt. Over g,12,000 has already been raised and it would be a grand thing if the bazaar could be made the means of raising the greater part of the balance. £15,000 is the sum required and already many Warringtonians have responded most liberally, We are sure, however, that their generosity is not yet exhausted, and those who have not subscribed will thus have an opportunity of aiding this excellent project. There will be a host of up-to-date attractions and, given fine weather, we have no doubt that the bazaar will realize the most sanguine anticipations. * * *</p>
        <p>The Viresleyans of Warrin-gton will regret very much the impending departure of one of the most popular local ministers —thp Rev. J. Manning—who leaves at the end of August to take up work at Preston. The cordial appreciation of his efforts here was fittingly voiced at the quarterly meeting at. Penketh on Thurs- day, many speakers bearing testimony to Mr. Manning's endeavours to further the cause of his church. The rev, gentleman has striven particularly hard to bring the masses into closer touch with Christian agencies and influences and we believe to some extent he has succeeded. The popular penny concerts which he organized proved a signal success and showed that the public will avail themselves of wholesome and refining recreation when it is placed within their reach for the modest penny or twopence. It is to be hoped that this good work will be continued when Mr. Manning has left the circuit, for the experiment has been abundantly justified. The wishes for Mr. Manning's success so heartily expressed at the quarterly meeting will be re-echoed by all local Wesleyans and by many members of other denominations who have come into contact with him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 15, 1907</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 15, 1907</p>
        <p>EXPLOSION AT CROSFIELD'S.</p>
        <p>Exciting Scenes.</p>
        <p>-4</p>
        <p>An exploiion, fortunately— more alarming than serious, occurred at Messrs. Crosfield's Works, Bank Quay, yesterday afternoon. Shortly before four o'clock a silicate dissolver, worked under great internal pressure, burst with a tremendous report. A large piece of iron and the contents of the yowl were hurled into the air amid a cloud of steam and in a few moments there was a shower of debris in the immediate vicinity. A large amount of the wreckage fell upon and wrecked the roof of the carbosil departinent,whilst a portion of the wall was also carried away. A number of nieces of silicate, some of considerable size, crashed through the glass roof of the milling room,a number of the girls being cut by the falling glass. There was at once a panic and several of the workers fainted, but once the momentary alarm was over order was quickly restored. The works fire " buzzer" was immediately sounded and in a little while there was a large corps of salvage men at work. Steps were at once taken to ascertain the extent of the damage and this was found to be very slight'in proportion to the sensation created. A number of employees in addition to the girls were also slightly cut and bruised, but no serious injuries are to be recorded. All wen attended to by members of the works ambulance corps, who worked admirably. The comparative immunity from accident to the workpeople was a piece of good luck. Ordinarily there are a number of men in the immediate neigh- ourhood of the dissllver, but these at that time had been called to another part of the works.</p>
        <p>"LEA-FRANCIS" "PREMIER," and " WHITwORTH " CYCLES. Price from £4 108. Cash or easy payments.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BANK QUAY STATION SCENE</head>
        <p>BANK QUAY STATION SCENE</p>
        <p>Summonses Against Grammar School Boy</p>
        <p>Dismissed.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court, yesterday, before the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), and other magis- trates, William Watkins. 158, Haydock-street, Earles- town, was summoned for having attempted to enter a carriage while in motion at Bank Quay Station on May 7, and also for assaulting a ticket examiner named George Martin.—Mr. Smythe, of Euston, prosecuted, and Mr. H. J. Longton defended.—At 5.15 p.m., 'on the date in question the defendant, who is a Warrington Grammar School boy, was in a carriage at Bank Quay Station intending to proceed to Earlestown. When Martin came to examine tickets and contracts the defendant told him he had left his contract at home. Martin told him he could not go on the train unless he got a ticket and defendant said he would get off the train before he would pay. When the train was in motion defendant got hold of the handle of the carriage, and when Martin pulled him off, defendant hit him in the face.—For the defence witnesses were called who stated that Martin got hold of the defendant by , the wrist and also by the throat part of his collar. He also seized him by the back of the neck, and defendant then struck him with his flat hand on the face. The ' defendant offered to give his name and address, brit Martin refused to take it,and when a young lady offered to my his fare he told her to shut up.—The cases were discussed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Coming Events. A series of public meetings and services in connec- tion with the Independent Methodist Churches will be held in Warrington this week end, commencing this afternoon. To-night a fellowship meeting will be conducted in the Friars Green Church, by Mr. James Hudson. At the services in the same church on Sunday the speakers will be, in the morning Mr. Hinry Holt, and in the evening Messrs. J. Dodd,Liverpool, and J. Parkinson, Salford. Young people's services will be held at Friars Green and Birch-street in the after noon. Business meetings will be held at Friars' Green on Monday in the morning and afternoon, whilst in the evening a public meeting will be held. The fifth annual sports in connection with Messrs. Crosfield's Recreation Club, will be held on the War- rington Football Ground on the 22nd inst. Valuable prizes will be competed for, and the works prize band will be in attendance A novel event in the shape of a ladies' cycle racefar'which there are 20 entries, is included in the programme. Mr. J. R. Clynes, M.P., will be the speaker this evening at a Socialist demonstration at Bridge Foot. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST 4 BOOTS IN LANCASHIRB.--AdVt. Personal.</p>
        <p>At the International horse show at Olympia,London Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., has been one of the judges of the horses in harness. Mr. E. H. Pearce, 35, Robson-street, this week exhibits a number of British ferns at the Museum. Mr. Pearce has a remarkable collection of about half the total known British species which he cultivates, in natural surroundings, in the yard at the back of his house: The exhibition, which is in the wild flower room, will be continued till next week-end.</p>
        <p>Warrington Grammar School. H. Dutton the head of the Grammar School and grandson of Mr. Henry Thornton, J.P., has been successful in winning in open competition an Entrance Exhibition of £3O per annum at St. John's College Cambridge.</p>
        <p>The Volunteer Band in Manchester The Warrington Volunteer Band, conducted by Mr. J. T. Richardson, on tiunday completed the first of four engagements to play in Manchester Parks' when they performed in Whityorth Park, Manchester, before a large and appreciatiCre crowd.</p>
        <p>• ' Wear BANK'S Ladies and Gent's Gloves.—Advt. Presentation at Padgate. At the close of the Rose Queen festivities at the Padgate Cottage Homes on Tuesday, the Matron (Mrs. Wright), on behalf the officers presented Miss Lilian Ivey, the infant mistress, who is leaving to be married, with a handsome silver cake basket. The children also subscribed their mite and gave her a pretty silver cake knife, which was handed to her by Lily Losh.</p>
        <p>• BLOUSES, a grand selection from is. 11/d.— BANKS, Outfitter, Horsemarket st.—Advt. Music In the Park.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Band will play the following programme in the Park on Sunday at 3.15 p.m. :—Hymn, selected; overture, "Bohemian Girl,' Balfe • recit and chorus, "Comfort ye "And the Glory," Handel; selection, " Saffo," Facial. (test piece at Hereford Contest, June 19th at which the band will compete) ; selection, " William Tell " Rossini ; march, 20th Century," J. 0. Hume • "God save the King " ; conductor, Mr. C. F. Becliorth: PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TRE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdVt.</p>
        <p>Street Betting. On Thursday Samuel Webster, of Houghton-street, Earlestown, described as a bookvi.oker, was before the Newton-le-Willows Bench on thrcle charges of-street betting, on the sth, 6th, and 7th inst., in Houghton- street. The evidence of two constables was to the effect that they say defendant receive slips and money, and on the third occasion they al-3tirehended him. At the police office he admitted having carried bets for other people. - The Bench mulcted him in £5 and 0°64, to cover the three cases He had not been up for a similar offence before. CELLULAR S rE,Ts from2/6—BANKs,Ontfltter.-Advt. Ancient Order of Foresters, The Past Officers Society had their annual picnic on Saturday, when. a party of 33 drove to the Bull Hotel, G-ateacre, where a substantial tea was provided. Afterwards the party had the use of the fine bowling green there for about two hours, and then had a stroll to Childwall. Altogether the outing was thoroughly enjoyed. A few remarks were made by P.D.C.R's Gorge Maybury, and L. Davies, congratu- lating the officers upon the excellent arraugements,and P.D.O.R. Peter Peacock, president, and P.D.C.R. H. James, secretary, suitably replied. TAILOR-M ADE CYCLE BREECHES from 6s. 11d.— BARKS, Outfitter, Horsemarket st.—Advt. Smart Fine for Wilful Damage. - --•</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on - Tuesday, Nellie Greenwood, widow, Hanle-street, was charged on a warrant with' wilfully committing damage to the dwelling-house of John Geraghty, Hume-street. Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted, stated that recently there had been a number of quarrels between defendant and several of the neighbours, but that complainant had nothing to do with the disputes. On Friday last defendant threw a brick through the window,smashing p picture. The magistrates imposed a fine of £2 4s. including damages and half of the advocate's fee. Education Committee. A meeting of the Education Committee will be held in the Town Hall on Monday next at 6 o'clock.—Mr. W. Davies has been appointed caretaker of St. James' school at a salary of £7l 19s. per annum.—Alderman Bennett has again offered the Trafalgar Essay Shield and prizes for competition, and the offer has been accepted.—Miss Mabel Houson has been appointed assistant cookery mistress at a salary of £7O per year; and Mr. H. B. H, Sander has been ,appointed manual instructor (conditionally) at £BO per year.—The Mayor hasibeen asked to lay the raemori al stone at the Bolton School, Latchford.—Mr. T. P. Bradshaw will move that the wages of the school-keepers be paid on Friday afternoon instead of Monday as at present. LADIES' Busy Gracz-BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.— IdtV.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Robert Garnett and Sons v. William Sprowson and Co.</head>
        <p>Robert Garnett and Sons v. William Sprowson and Co.</p>
        <p>This was a remitted action from the High Court to the Liverpool County Court and was heard before His Honour Judge Thomas. The plaintiffs claimed the sum of 2..54 14s. for work done for the defendants in connection with lifts supplied by them to Messrs. Henry Bannerman and Sons Ltd. Manchester and Cloncaird Castle, Ayrshire, and the defendants who are lift manufacturers carrying on business at Ascendant Works, Newton Heath, counter-claimed the sum of F.,12 15s. and ri'3s 9s. 4d. The plaintiffs were represented by Mr. F. Cuthbert Smith, barrister-at-law (instructed by Messrs. Davies and Forshaw) and the defendants were represented by - Dr. Atkinson (instructed by Messrs. Farrar and Co., solicitors, Manchester, and Messrs. Whitley and Co., solicitors, Liverpool). After a lengthy hearing his Honour gave judgment for the plaintiffs for .£46 16s. 6d. on claim with costs and disallowed the defendants' counter- claim (with the exception of £2 10s.) with costs on lhe higher scale for the plaintiffs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
        <p>CRIN' AND CREAMY</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>Ship Canal Litigation. Mr. Justice Kekewich, in the Chancery Division yesterday, allowed a motion in the case of the Man- chester Bhip Canal Company versus the Corporation of Warrington, for an injunction to restrain the pour- ing of salt water in the River Mersey to stand over. His Lordship said that when the parties were ready he would listen to an application to expediate the hearing. Defrauding the Railway Company. Minnie Monks, a young married woman, who stands at various markets with drapery, was, at Newton-le- Willows,on Thursday, fined 10s. and costs for defraud- ing the railway company by travelling from Ghorley to Warrington on the night of the 7th May and tendering only 6d —the fare from Earlestown. Bowling Picnic. A number of Warringtonians spent an enjoyable day on Saturday when they held a picnic to Bollington. Arriving there about 3.30. between 30 and 40 of the members took part in a bowling main held on the green adjoining the Stamford Arms hotel, the games being remarkably keen. Before the conclusion,of the main the players were entertained to tea. The prize winners were Messrs. W. Pemberton, J. Atherton, J. Griffiths, H. Savory, J. Hollinsworth, A. Atherton, J. Bradshaw, and P. Boardman. Great credit is due to the hon. secretary, Mr. J. Hollinsworth, and Mr. J. T, Bate for the excellent arrangements. "LEA-FRANCIS," P RIMIER," and " BUDGE- WHITWORTH " CYCLES. Price from £4 10s. Cash or easy payments.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Beyond . Reform. At the Borough Police Court on Thursday Ellen James of Dale-street, Sankey Bridges, was charged with neglecting her five children. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, who prosecuted on behalf of the society, detailed the usual sordid story of a drunken mother, and, consequently, child neglect. Since March, 1906, the case had been under ,Ahe observation of the society's inspector, but, despite numerous warnings, there had been no effort to reform by the woman. She received good wages from her husband, and also kept a lodger, but her fondness for drink resulted in the home and children being neglected. The woman said it waa all through keeping bad company. The magistrates sent her to goal for two months. County Court Judge's Return. His Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., took his seat at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, after an illness extending over six months. The solicitors present were Mr. H. J. Loni;ton and Mr. Thomas S. Steel. Mr. Longton said that before com- mencingthe business of the Court he should like to be i allowed n a very few sincere words, on behalf of the solititors practising in that Court, to express to his Honour their sincere congratulations on his restora- tion to health, and his return to the Court. They hoped that the health which had been restored to him might be continued for a long time, and they were very pleased to see him back again. His. Honour thanked Mr. Longton most heartily and sincerely for having so kindly expressed himself. He was very glad indeed to come back amongst them, and he hoped they would all be spared to continue in the admistra- tion of justice in that Court. Above all Wear B.4.NICS'S HATS and CAPs.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Photographic Society. This society is in a flourishing condition and it is anticipated that the president's competition for 1907, will be a great success. Three prints of any subject, size or process may be submitted by each competitor, the same not having been previously exhibited or used in any competition in connection 1 with the society. The entries must be solely the work of the competitor except framing which is optional, and must be deposited in the reading room at headquarters not later than the 24th September, 1907. The president (Hr. F. G. Taylor) offers a silver plaque for the best picture. Second and third awarAs of a bronze medal and photographic spoon will be made if, in the opinion of the judge, (who will be nominated by the president) the number of entrants warrant them. The whole of the pictures submitted will be on view during the exhibition of members' work to be held at headquarters in the autumn. Other pictures for this exhibition must be handed in at headquarters not later than the last day of September. The Lancashire and Cheshire Photographic Union excursion to Barrow-in-Furness takes place to-day (Saturday). SUITS made to measnre.—BANK s, Outfitter.—Advt. The Painter and the Workhouse. 1 James Clarke, a painter, last from the Workhouse, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, yester- ' day with neglecting to maintain himself, whereby he income chargeable to the Union. He pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Basil Shearer prosecuted, and said the defendant was admitted to the Workhouse on May 16th, and had remained there since. He had been working for a plumber and painter in Runcorn, earning 71d. an hour. He, however, left his work an 4 went into the workhouse. The defendant had been chargeable to the guardians off and on for the last ten years, although he was well able to work, and could earn good wages.l Defendant stated that he was destitute when he went into the workhouse, and he had not cost the guardians ' a penny since he had been there. He had practically " done" the house through two or three times over, and he stopped working on account of bad weather. In reply to Alderman Tinnion, Mr. Shearer said that probably defendant made £1 a week more than he cost the guardians, but he wished to point out that if- men who were able to work were allowed to go into the workhouse they would require ten ',workhouses.— The case was dismissed. "LEA-FRANCIS," " PREMIER," and " R LTD GE- WHITWORTH " CICLES. Price from £4 10s. Cash or easy payments.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt, Warrington Chamber of Commerce. At the lsst meoting of the Council, the President (Mr. Holman Kingdon, M.A.) in the chair, the follow- ing new members were elected: —Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., Mr. J. Rymer Young, F.C.S.' and Messrs. Bellian and Davies, the Bifurcated and Tubular Rivet Co„ Ltd., the Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank, Ltd., the Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Company, Ltd., the National Provincial Bank of England, Ltd., and the Union Bank of Manchester, Ltd. The Secretary (Alderman Bennett) reported that he and Mr. Kingdon had attended the forty- seventh annual meeting of the Associated Chambers in London, and gAve a brief report of the proceedings. He also stated that he attended the Secretaries' annual dinner, which, as usual, gave rise to a fruitful dis- cussion en matters relating to the work of Chamber. of Commerce. A letter was read from the Manchester ; Ship Canal Company in reply to an enquiry from the Chamber as to which line of steamers was most likely to stop 'lat Warrington for local goods to the effect that the arrangements for handling cargo at the lay-by were still under consideration, and that some matters required adjusting before the Company would be in Et position to suitably deal with it there•without delay- ing vessels timed to call at fixed hours. The Secretary was instructed to communicate with them further on the subject. Mr. Louis Henshall reported that his firm (Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd.) were doing a Urge trade in timber at Stag Bridge and that the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., had received a considerable quantity of iron there. A communica- tion was read from Mr. Edward J. Burrow of Cheltenham, offering to issue, under the auspices of the Chamber a Commercial Guide to Warrington, pointing out, amongst other things, its great advan- tages as a manufacturing centre, and the Secretary was instructed to obtain further particulars and bring the matter before the Council again. NORPOLK SUITS fromlB/6—BANKS,OUtfitter.—Adyt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PERILOUS FALL AND LUCKY ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>George Thomas, of King-street, Runcorn, had a marvellous escape from death on Saturday. He is a labourer on the transporter bridge. When a transporter car was out of its berth, he fell into the liersey. For- tunately the tide was about half-ebb, and he fell into about five feet of water. At low tide he would have dropped on the rocks. He was rescued in an unconscious state. His injuries are believed not to be serious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman S. Owens) presided over the meeting of Widnes Council, held on Tuesday at the Town Hall. Before commencing the usual business,the Mayor made touching reference to the death of the late Councillor William Norris. and also to the late ex- Councillor C. E. Barlow. In the Health Committee's minutes the birth-rate showed 48 per 1,000 better than last year, whilst the death-rate showed a decrease.— The report of the Gas and Water Committee was also on the side of progresi, and showed that during the year there had been on increase of 8•3 per cent. in the making of gas. The profits from this undertaking alone were £5,255, which was £551 in excess of the profit last year. although in September the price of gas was redvc,ed ld. per 1,000 cubic feet to large consumers, and a reduction also to slot meter consumers.—The much- talked-of-topic re deputation expenses, which has been an eyesore to not a few, was also thrashed out, and it was decided to allow deputations their travelling ex- penses and 13 guineas per day and night if spent in London, one guinea per day and night if spent else- where, and 15s. per day if not absent at night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>I The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when I there were present: Messrs. G. Pilling (presiding), T. Baxter, C. Roylance, E. Britch. P. Mounileld, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. Whitlow, P. Booth, C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies, T. Guest, S. Woodward, W. Harrison, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, C. F. Bell, R. Nixon, J. Spruce, J. Renshaw, J. Hallows. J. Mynekvme, Admiral Jones Parry, A. Raining, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyor), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical officer). The Local Government Board wrote asking that the Medical Officer should supply them with particulars ,as' to the deaths which had occurred in the Parisi; of Worley, from diphtheria, and also upon the recent Pre- valence of the disease in the district.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams said he was preparing the report. Mr. Woodward said that since the last meeting of the Council two other cases of diphtheria had been reported from Norley. The Highways Committee at a meeting had under consideration the offer of the County Council, to place a dust laying preparation on the roads provided the District Council would bear two-thirds of the cost. They recommended that in view of the excessive cost the offer be not entertained.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker pointed out that during the past year the timated cost for maintenance as highways was greater than the actual expenditure by 436. The high- way rate since 1903 had gone down from Ntl. to 70. and their expenditure was gradually decreasing. He thought they might congratulate themselves as their roads were in as good, if not a better condition than ever they were. Mr. Pilling thought the report was highly satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, when there were present :—Messrs. T. Baxter (presiding), C. Roylance, E. Britch, P. Mounfield, G. Egerton-Warbur- ton, J. Whitlow, P. Booth, C. E. Linaker. J. H. Davies, T. Guest., S. Woodward, W. Harrison, G. Pilling, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton. C. F. Bell, Ti. Nixon, J. Spruce, S. Woodward, J. Dauglas,J. W. Temperley, A. R. Norman, J. R.enshaw, J. Hallows, J. Mynekvme, Admiral Jones- Parry, A. Raining. G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), T. Ciaok, W. Ti. de Prez and F. Pearson (relieving officers), and 3 Cunnah (master). It was decided that the Guardians hereby consent :- 1. To the making of alterations and additions to the workhouse of the Union in accbrdance with plans to which the Local Government Board have sifted their seal, subject nevertheless to such modifications thereof as that Board may hereafter direct; 2. To the expendi- ture for the purposes of a sum not exceeding two thousand three hundred and ninety-five pounds; 3. To the borrowing of the said sum of two thousand three hundred acid ninety-five pounds or such part thereof as may be necessary, and that the sum of £2,395 be borrowed for the purposes aforesaid; that the sum so borrowed be repaid within a period not exceeding 27 years. Mr. Norman asked if the Rural District Council paid anything to the Runcorn Union for the use of the Council Chamber. They in Runcorn had to pay for premises for the meeting of the Urban District Council. Mr. Linaker said it was to the advantage of Runcorn that they should be joined to the Rural District for poor law purposes. Runcorn's poor if the Urban Authority bed to keep them would cost 10d. in the £ Whilst the poor inlindworth could he kept for 11. in the twhereas both districts paid the same rates, to the benefit of Runcorn. They in the district did not grumble but they would have a grievance if the Council had to pay for the use of the room. Mr. Wood said there was another point of view. People went from the country into the towns and then came on to the rates. Mr. Woodward: No they work themselves out in the town. _ .</p>
        <p>_ Mrs. Blair, Moore, wrote offering to entertain the inmates and children in the Workhouse, provided the Guardians would undertake the expense of conveying them to Moore. It was decided to accept Mrs. Blair's offer and at the next meeting to invite the members of the Board to subscribe to the cost of conveyance. The Finance Committee reported the payment for out-relief of £65 to Mr. Cook, £29 to Mr. de Prez and .£25 to Mr. Pearson. Mr. Spruce reported that the number of inmates was 152 against 135 for the corresponding period of last year. The vagrants relieved during the fortnight were 172 against 137. The Board of Guardians of St. Marylebone, forwarded a resolution viewing with alum the creation of new offices necessary for the administration of Lord Cecil's Bill for the notification of births, to the medical officer for the district and asking that births shall be, as at present, notified to the superintendent registrar. The matter was referred to the Parliamentary Committee.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS PRIMROSE LEAGUERS. HUSSARS THAT COULD NOT COME. The demonstration of the St. Helens and Newton habitations of the Primrose League, which was the subject of a question in Parliament on Thursday last,was held on Saturday afternoon at Rainford Hall. The programme consisted of sports. A military display by- the Lancashire Hussars should also have formed part of the programme. but this had to be abandoned conse- quent upon the intervention of the War Office. There was a large gathering, conveyed by special trains from all parts of the Newton Division. Colonel R. Pilkington, C. 8., presided, and speaking on general politics, said the Government was rude and unfriendly to the Colonial Premiers, who came to ask the mother country to grant them Preference. The action of the Government in giving the Boers the government of the Transvaal would have an extraordin- ary effect in the future. It would be detrimental to the interests of the settlers and white population in South Africa, who would simply be at the mercy of the Boers. The Government bad very speedily given away ail that had been won by the lives of sl many of their sons and by so many millions of money. It almost looked as if some members of the Government were ashamed of the Union Jack. What Englishmen wanted was more Imperial patriotism. Unless the English people could carry the burden of Empire with enthusiasm and patriotism, they were not worthy of the responsibility which had been placed upon them. Mr. J. Ratcliffe Cousins spoke at length on Taziff Reform, and Mr. J. H. Bottomley, the Conservative agent, made some sarcastic references to the action of Mr. J. A. Seddon, the M,P. for the Newton Division, in spoiling the</p>
        <p>ST. HELEN'S RECREATION SPORTS The twelfth annual sports of the St. Helen's Recrea tion Cycle Club were carried out most successfully 012 Saturday. There was a good attendance, and the pro- gramme was greatly extended, including no fewer than seventy-four events. Colonel W. NV. Pilkington, head, of the firm of Messrs. Pilkington Bros., was present, and presented the prizes. Messrs. E. Pugh (N.C.11), J. Poole and W. Owen were the judges; Messrs. J. Seddon and P. Phythian, timekeepers, and Mr. F'. Eddelston, secretary. An athletic championship had been in pro- gress between the different departments of the glass- works and colliery for the past five weeks, the competi- tions being bowling, cyclists relay races, foot relay races, swimmingoquadron races and tug-of-war. The sheet works yard! team won witia five points, and the clerks and collieries tied for second place. Results:- Mile Bicycle Club Race.-1, .T. Bibby; 2,J. Dambell ; 3, H. Smith. Won by 3 yards. Time, 2 min. 33 2-5 sec. 100 yards, confined to Recreation Club.-1, M. Marron, (41) ; 2, R. Glover (2.1); 3, J, T. Manchester (scratch). close finish. Time 10-4-5 sec. Mile Novice Bicycle.-1, W. Eccleston, Wigan ;2, C. Hutton, Widnes; 3, J. Cowley, Preston. Won by a length. Mile Flat (Open).-1, H. Davies, West Cheshire (145) ; 2, F. Barber, Widnes (152) ; 3, IV. Begg, Liver- pool Cowley (125). Won by a foot. Time, 4min. 25sec. 100 Yards (Open).--1, C. J. Lewis, Liverpool H. (9); 2. R. Glover, Sutton (9.1) ; 3, J. Harrison. Waterloo (741. Won by inches. Time, lOsec. Quarter Mile Bicycle.-1, A. Bennion, Little Sutton (19)1; 2, P. Bailey, St. Helens (36) ; 3, R. Allen, Widnes (33). Won easily. Time, 31 2-ssec. ; 220 Yards Flat.-1, C. J. Lewis; 2, R. Glover; 3, F. Hesketb, Aughton. Clse finish. Time, 22 2-ssec. Bicycle.--1, N. ill. Stephens, Earlestown (40) ; 2, W. Wood, Chester (16); 3, A. Reunion (42). Time, 65sec. Two Mile Harrier Team Race.-1, S. Welding. Sutton Harriers won, 10 points; to Sankey. 13. Five Miles Club Championship.-1, J. Bibby ;2, J. Smith; 3, J. Huyton. Lap prize, H. Smith. Time 14min. 31sec. Relay Race.-1, Sheet Flatteners ;2, Sheet Works Yard. One Mile Walk.-1 G. Slocombe ;2, W. Parr. Two Miles Harriers' Championithip (confined).-1, C. Robertshaw ; 2, C. E. Shevlan ; 3, H. Lomax. At the conclusion o! the prize distribution Mr. F. Eddleston (secretary) on behalf of the Committee, p resented a silver-mounted umbrella to Mr. C. E. Pugh cretary of the Liverpool centre N.C.U.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS TRANSFER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Alderman Martin presided at the transfer sessions for St. Helens boroufh held on Monday.—The bench sanctioned alterations at the Grange Park Hotel for which Mr. Kelly . applied ; and alterations at the Coppersmith's Arms, Watery-lane,Sutton, on the applica- tion by Mr. E. W. Swift.—ln the case of the New Inn, Liverpool, Chief-Constable Ellerington said that in January last he had a very serioud complaint against John Flynn for giving credit at the New Church Tavern. He asked the bench to extract a promise from Flynn that he would not give credit in future. Flynn denied that he had given credit. Mr. E. W. Swift said tie represented Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., the owners, who would see that the regulations were carried out. The bench granted all the applications. NEGLECTING TO MAIN FAIN A FAMILY.</p>
        <p>John Leary, a man who bad been convicted of cruelty to his five children, was sent to prison for two months by the St. Helens magistrates on Monday for refusing to pay to the support of his children. In March, 1906, when the man was convicted, four of the children were sent to a home, and he was ordered to pay 4s. weekly in respect of one of the children that had been taken by the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. He had never paid a single penny, and defied the Society. Leary said he was willing to pay if the amount was reduced to 2s. a week.</p>
        <p>SMALL INVESTMENT. 5 Shop (Rents) £BB 2 All payments 40 13 Net Income</p>
        <p>0 4</p>
        <p>PRICE £220.-21k PER CENT. MR. CLAYTON F- -18, Albert Square, Manchester.</p>
