      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN FOOBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>_ The annual generitl meeting of this club was held last Thursday in the Co-operative Hall. There was a large attendance, and Mr. T. Siddeley presided. fie said they could congratulate themselves on getting into the "A " Division of the Lancashire Combination. They had a team of trie:s and the town ought to be proud of them. They must now make up their minds to stay in the " A" Division insteld of cl; .-.pping out every other year. With the team they had got he thought they would do so now.—(Applause.l—The accounts pre- sented by Mr. A. Perks (secretary), showed a balance in bank of £l7 18s. Bd., and cash in hand 4;1 10s.--The accounts were passed.- -Mr. T. Siddeley was unanimously re-elected presi- dent.—Mr. B. Ashcroft was re-elected hon. treasurer. Mr. Percy McHardy proposed the re-appointment of Mr. A. Perks as secretary. He had done his best under great difficulty and devoted himself to the work of the club in a way that was commendable to himself and everyone concerned.—The motion was carried with acclamation.—On the motion of the Chairman, Messrs. Alderson and Porter were re-electee auditors.-__jr. Perks presented a report. The club won the Lancashire Junior Cup without playing a match on their own ground. They also finished up a good second in Division " B " of the Combination, and it was only on the last Saturday the championship was snatched from their grasp. They scored 124 goals against 57, which was a record for goals in either combination. Of these Bartley scored 28 ; Boyer, 23; Ellis, 17 ; and Woods 12. The election of committee resulted as follows : Messrs. P. McHardy 170 votes, J. Sankey 156, J. Lilley 154, Wm. Jones 141, Robinson 139. E. Jones 136, Topping 134, Horridge 134, Haselden 130, W.H.Crowther* 121, Jolley 118, A. Warburlon 100. Not elected: E. Brammer, Harrison, .T. E. Clough, T. Carney, and H. Morris.—A variety of suggestions gave rise to a desultory discussion. The price of members' tickets was decided at Bs. 6d. for ground and stand and Be. for ground only.--Mr. Jones complained of the bad language used on the ground by some members towards visiting teams. They seemed to think the visitors only were I guilty of shady tactics and did not recognise unfair play by the home team. He pleaded for better language and a fairer treatment to the visiting clubs. He alleged- the sixpenny members were worse than the fourpenny ones, nor did he exclude the stand occupants.—The Chairman said it was proposed to rebuild the fences and asked for voluntary labour in this matter.—Mr. Macklin suggested the players should do it for exercise. The Chairman: Some of our players lost their form last year through too much work.</p>
        <p>WAIT A BIT, BONNIE. " Wait, sonnie," says the big man to the small boy straining after manliness, "you must eat some more pudding before you can do that," And don't you see that all the time the man is paying an indirect compliment to the Currant ? For the Currant is, after all, the most nutritive ingredient in cake, pudding or bread, one single pound of currants containing as much nutriment as three pounds of loan beef.</p>
        <p>At Worcester Assizes on Wednesday two publican' named King and Hope were each fined £.25, and a clerk named Hodnett £2O for haring committed bribery in the Couser,-ative interest at Worcester during the general election.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
