      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>Annual Concert.</p>
        <p>Dr. Arthur Somervell conducted the annual examina- tion in music at the Warrington Training College on Thursday. As usual the collective test took the form of a concert which was held in the evening, and at the invitation of the Principal (Canon Morley Stevenson) a rge number of prominent townspeople attended. The is ayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst) mere present. The works selected for interpretation g ere Romberg's " Lay of the Bell " and Jensen's "The Feast of Adonis," and from the point of view of the audience the choice was certainly a happy one. The concert gave the greatest pleasure and reflected credit upon all who took part in it. The principal vocalists were : Miss Margaret Machin, Miss Maggie Wardle, Miss Edith Harrison, Miss Emily Imison,Miss Margaret Sowert,y, Miss Miriam Gulland, Mr. Harry Evans (Liverpool Cathedral) and Mr. Arthur Weber (Liverpool Cathedral). "The Lay of the Bell " I occupied the major part of the programme and I this beautiful composition with its pictures of life's I changes and chances and its ine dramatic passages, was sung in a manner which delighted the audience. The solos were well sustained, Mr. Arthur Weber, as the master, being in particularly good voice and fully maintaining his high reputation. Mr. Harry Evans gave sympathetic) and artistic renderings of several fine tenor solos. Praise is also due to those students who figured as soloists. The chorus was strengthened by the inclusion of a number of local gentlemen and a quartette in "The Lay of the Bell" was sung by Mr. A. Greenough, Mr.J.Parkinson, Mr. P. C. Price and Mr. R.Harding. " The Feast of Adonis "—a very bright and tuneful work—brought the concert to a close. An orchestra, composed of the following, was in attend- ance :-Ist. violin, Mr. B. H. Pearson: 2nd violin, Mr. IE, H. Clare ; viola, Mr. G. Johnson; violoncello, Mr. Henry Bond; double bass, Mr. J. Sudworth ; piano- forte, Miss Hackett: harmonium, Mr. W. H. Payton, .Mus Bac .Mr W. S. Wood conducted with his customary i •• ; ability. Canon STEVENSON, in the course of a brief speech, said last year unfortunately Dr. Somervell was not well and they had to have their concert without any examiner. They examined themselves and thought they did pretty well. (Laughter.) This year they had to submit, though they were very glad indeed to do so to the comments of their examiner. They hoped he would take as favourable a view of them this year as they took of themselves last year. (Laughter.) They were particularly sorry that Dr. Somervell could not attend I the last concert because they bad a little musical : experiment and tried to work out something of the ; history and development of the English anthem. He • (Canon Stevenson)was a great believer in English music ' and especially in ecclesiastical music, and he thought the I English anthem was a composition of which English people might well be proud. (Applause.) Dr. SOMERVELL said that after the harmonious con- ditions which prevailed last year at the examination, he would be the last person to wish to strike a dis- cordant note this year—(laughter)—so he would not say very much about the performance except that he had listened to it with great pleasure, and one thing that interested him was that both the pieces were new. He was going to colleges nearly every day during four or five months of the year and he heard a great many of the same things over and over again. He had been constantly assured that spring was here—(laughter)— but he was bound to say that judging from the atmos- phere that night, he thought we had got to summer. It was rather refreshing in these days to hear two works which were st:, frankly melodious as the ones to which they had Just listened.ffe thought they were too much inclined nowadays to think that unless a piece of music thoroughly puzzled them the that time they heard it there was not much good in it. (Laughter.) He did not think that was true of the greatest musicians. They wrote music which as a rule one could take in,or at any rate understand to some extent,the first time. Speaking to the students, Dr. Somervell said he thought the sub- ject of music which they were going to teach in the , schools was about the most important subject in the I curriculum. (Laughter.) They had far too little music lin the schools. When a short time ago he expressed the opinion that the music in the schools was not good enough, he received a very angry letter asking him what he expected with only two half-hours a week devoted to the subject. He replied that that was one of the great reasons why the music was not better. He wanted the subject to be considered so important that half an hour a day should he set apart for it. When they obtained that land the children learnt singing thoroughly and were taught what was good music, they would feel what a benighted age the past one was. On visiting the schools, ,he was struck st Rh the extraordinary variety of music I I he heard—good,bad and indifferent music and trashy music Any kind of music seemed to do for the children, and he could not see why. In teaching poetry, for instance, I they were careful to see that something suitable was chosen. A short time ago a book was issued contain- ing 200 songs of the most beautiful kind that could , ever be taught to children—he meant the national songs of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales. They were really beautiful tunes which the children would learn extraordinarily easily, and which they ought to know because they were part of the national inheritance. Then again, the teachers would not got tired of teaching them. On the other hand, there ware things which were called school songs which not always but very often were written to ttashy words, and were not the work of musicians of any repute, so that they were purely ephemeral. Again alluding to the national songs, Dr. Somervell said they should be taught, because they were old and were valuable as links with the past. He believed that class of music was the proper approach all children should make towards modern music. As a concluding word of advice to the teachers, he would say: "Seek the old paths; sing the old songs; and tell the old stories." (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING. -- Rev. J. Manning's Farewell.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING. -- Rev. J. Manning's Farewell.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Wesleyan Methodist Circuit was held at Penketh on Thursday and wax largely attended. Tea was served in the school- room by ladies of the Penketh congrogation, and on the motion of Councillor W. Clegg seconded by Mr. E. J. Mitchell, they were cordially thanked for their hospitality. The Rev. E. J. Gawne and Mr. Johnson suitably replied. ln the interval between the tea and the business meeting the company adjourned to the grounds of Mrs. Garnett, Hall Nook, where a pleasant time was spent._</p>
        <p>The meeting in the church was presided over by the Rev. Sidney Pitt (superintendent) who was supported by the ministers of the circuit. Mr. John Upson, senior circuit steward, reported that the income for the quarter amounted to £241, which was £24 in excess of the expenditure. The circuit debt had now been reduced to £75.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. E. Richardson, F. A. Harris, J. G. Within- shaw, W. Silcock, and the circuit stewards were appointed as a committee to formulate a scheme for liquidating the debt.</p>
        <p>The meMbership returns were presented by the Rev. Sidney Pitt, and showed that there were 912 members and 215 meeting in junior society classes. The greater part of the evening was taken up with references to the departure from the Circuit of the Rev. Joseph Manning who leaves for Preston, after three years work here, at the end of August. The Rev. Sidney Pitt spoke on behalf of the ministers, and the other speakers included Mr.J. Upson, Mr. J.E. Richard- son, Mr. F. A. Harris, anu Mr. G. Weaver. Mr. -Manning's work in the circuit, and especially at Bold- street, was warmly_eulogised, and great regret was expressed that he was about to sever his connection with Warrington.—Mr. Manning briefly replied, and thanked the workers in the circuit for their kindness and hearty co-operation. The Rev. William Mellor reported that the income for foraien missions during the year was nearly .£4O in excess of the previous year's. The Rev. J. Manning, as educational secretary, pre- sented the day and Sunday school returns. There were, he said, 14 Sunday schools with a staff of 316 teachers and 2,828 scholars on the books, tilt, average morning attendance being 1,015 and the afternoon attendance 1981. At the Silver-street and Penketh day schools the scholars on the books totalled 1162, with an average attendance of 991. Sankey Bridges school had been transferred to the Education Committee. A discussion took place on the value of Catechetical teaching in the Sunday schools and a resolution was passed, urging the various schools to regularly use the Wesleyan Methodist Catechism. An invitation to hold the next meeting at Padgate on September 19th was accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Church.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at Cairo-street Church on Sunday on the occasion of the anniversary services in connection with the Sunday school. The preacher, morning and evening, was the Rev. C. J. Street, of Sheffield, who delivered interesting addresses. There was also a large attendance in the afternoon at the scholars service conducted by Mr. H. S. Knowles, of Sheffield. Special music was rendered by the choir at each service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>St. Barnabas' Church</head>
        <p>St. Barnabas' Church</p>
        <p>Tuesday being St. Barnabas' Day, the patronal festival of this church was celebrated. At evensong the preacher was the Rev. Canon Matthew, Rector of 'Wigan, whose homely but powerful discourse was listened to with great interest. He took for his text the words "He was a good man and full of the Holy Ghost "—the praise which is accorded to bt. Barnabas in the Acts of the Apostles. The chief lesson which the preacher emphasised was that the goodness of St. Barnabas' consisted largely of seeing the good in other people, and always looking at the best side of their characters. This also was the method of our Lord, and the preacher urged his hearers to follow it out in their own lives.—The Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth) conducted the service. The collection was on behalf of the day and Sunday school funds. Church of the Sacred Heart.</p>
        <p>The annual sermons in aid of the above Mission were preached on Sunday last by the Rev. Father George, S. J., of St. Francis Xavier's, Liverpool. There was a good congregation at both the morning and the evening service. The rev, gentleman urged upon Catholic parents the necessity of seeing that their children were brought up in the faith their forefathers had fought for and handed down to them. It was such a glorious gift to give to their children, and a happy consolation to them, when dying, to know that they had done spiritu- ally what their forefathers had done in years gone by. The choir, under the able conductorship of Mr. James Gibbins, sang a new Mass by Bordencl and gave a very fine interpretation of this beautiful work. The offer- tory piece "Ave Maria" (Apt), was also well sung. The evening music was choral service of the Sacred Heart and Benediction, sung by the male choir. Master W. Hardiman presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. and the Peace Conference.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. and the Peace Conference.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Wallington presided over the weekly meeting of the Wycliffe Society on Sunday, and sub- mitted the following resolution :—" That this meeting rejoices in ae prospect of the International Peace Conference, about to assemble at the Hague, and earnestly trusts that its deliberations may be fruitful in promoting right understanding and mutual contid- •ence amongst the nations therein represented. The meeting expresses the sincere desire that his Majesty's Government will do all in its power to promote the purposes of the Conference, and to bring them to a speedy and successful issue. It further desires that copies of this resolution may be forwarded to the Prime Minister, the Foreign Secretary, an the Member for War- -rington." Mr. Wallington said it was their duty to give the resolution their hearty support. There were many questions of a political character, which they refrained from discussing, as many of their P.S.A. brethren held different shades of politics, but that resolution commended itself to all Christian institu- tions.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. ATKINSON seconded the resolution, which was carried unanimously. Mr. T. G. TAYLOR, of Oldham, said the resolution had his most hearty support. He spoke upon the question of" Christianity." It was not played out, but, was proving its power day by day. He alluded to the awful results of ~var, and to the Gospel of Peace, as brought into the word by Christ. Miss A. 13F.swicK sang two solos in an effective manner—" Shepherd's cradle song" and " God Who madest earth and heaven, darkness and light." Mr. R. Lewis read the lesson, and the Secretary offered a special prayer, for the guidance of the Conference. Special hymns were sung upon peace. Mr. Payton presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Presentation of Memorial Chairs.</head>
        <p>Presentation of Memorial Chairs.</p>
        <p>The members and congregation at the Baptist Church 'Golborne-street, are just now rejoicing in the possession of a complete set of fine commcnion furniture, pre- sented to them by kind donors and subscribers, to per- petuate the memory of departed friends. The Recha- bites having presented on Thursday last a choice table in memory of Brother Thomas Hall, a similar event took place on Sunday evening when, through the kindness of Mrs. Alfred halmford, four beautiful oak chairs in memory of her husband, and late friend Mr. John Fieldhouse, were presented to the Church.—Towards the close of the service Mr. B. Few took charge of the meeting, and in a few remarks expressed his delight at being able to preside on such a notable occasion, and explained how Mrs.Balmford had so willingly come and proffered to give the beautiful gifts to the Church.— Magiorman Balmford, in presenting the furniture, on behalf of his aunt, spoke or the joy and pleasure it was to him to be allowed to hand over the chairs to the Church. He knew this was a long-cherished hope and desire she was now fulfilling, to give to the Church something lasting and useful in memory of her dear husband, who was so much attached to the place, and a willing friend to all who knew him. He never forgot the " assembling together of saints" and was always a regular and punctual worshipper in the House of God. Mr. John Fieldhonse, in whose memory the three smaller chairs were being given, was a devout and faithful Christian, of a genial and sympathetic disposi- tion. He was an esteemed friend of Mrs. Balmford. He served the Church faithfully and was an elder and earnest worker in connection with the place and always ready to serve and help on the work. Mr. E. Wynne Jones (deacon) accepted the gifts with deep gratitude, for the church, and expressed in a few chosen remarks how the church would cherish and prize the furniture because of the memory of those who had been called to a higher service and entered into a fuller and larger life. In no better way could the memory of these two friends be perpetuated than in the manner it was being done, and month by month as the church gathered</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>B</p>
        <p>THE SETTLER'S IDEAL HOME</p>
