      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June lb, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social 'workers by contributing themselves.</p>
        <p>- Correspondents must write on one side of the paper on/y, and endeavour to make their letter s and notes short arid to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Bottomley, Conservative agent for the Newton Division, has adopted a novel way of getting votes. It will be remembered that a demonstration of members of the Primrose League, under the auspices of the Newton Division and St. Helens , Habitation of the League, was to be held at the seat of Colonel R. Pilkington, Rainford Hall, and that a troop of Yeomanry was to be present to give them entertainment. Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., drew the attention of Mr. Haldane to the fact, and he very promptly issued instructions forbidding such a breach of the regulations. Mr. Bottomley, speaking at the 2emonstration, criticized Mr. Seddon's action, and said : " This is the worst day's work that Jimmy Seddon ever did, raising the questi,n and stopping the Yeomanry coining here to-day. If you Conserva- tives Liberal Unionists and Primrose Leaguers will go back to the villages from which you have come to-day and tell how he has robbed you of an entertainment, then r believe that ?nova votes will be secured to our eide."</p>
        <p>The Tory Party, not satisfied with claiming the Union Jack, wants the bloomin' Army as well. Go home you villagers, you Prinigoses, and tell all your friends and neighbours what a fine, clever, muddle somebody made of your gathering by attempting to secure for Tory purposes that which can only be used for fighting British battles!</p>
        <p>frT Cha—rles E. Parker, the President of the Warrington Liberal Association has been to Plymouth. Being fond of a row, he went to a Socialist gathering bravely passing the theatre doors en route. He heard first an earnest and eloquent address ; but that was not what he went for to hear. He went for a row ; and, judging by his subsequent remarks, he got more than he bargained for. _ _</p>
        <p>Instead " going for "the Tory Party,the speakers attacked the Liberal Party, and, cf course, the Presi- dent of a Liberal Association would, naturally, resent such a turn of events.</p>
        <p>If he wants to hear his party always praised why does he not stick to his own meetings? The Liberal party is continually storming against Socialism, and if some Socialists retaliate why not take the licking in good part ! But then, some people have no sense of humour!</p>
        <p>I wonder what, the members of the Women's Liberal Associations throughout the Kingdom will think now that the rules of the National Liberal Federation pro- hibit women from membership of the Executive Com- mittee. Women may work hard for the" good of the cause," but they must not ask to be allowed the privi- lege of acting on the Committee. "Dr. Massie," says the " Manchester Guardian," "did not want to shut the door against members of Parlia- ment; he tcantsd to shut it against women. Now why,we may aak,are women to be formally branded in this way as unworthy to sit on the Executive Committee of the Federation ? I also would like to know why women will continue to support a patty which flatters their vanity and egotism by promising them everything at Election times, but reduces theca to the rank of a clotheshorse when their services are not required ? Women are no longer superficially taught. They are beginning to understand the game of politics ; to take notice of other things besides the soft compli- ments of fine gentlemen. They will soon be in a position to use their party strength to .gain a little political freedom for themselves, and not to waste useful energy on any party which promises only to humiliate. Women will cease to be treated with degradation,as mere wages-receivers,when they refuse to be put off with flattery and bluff. A Jarrow-on-Tyne reader of The " Examiner" writes me an interesting letter, in which Ire says : "I have enjoyed your column the 'Voice of Labour' and am quite sure it, will be of much service to the real and true Cause of progress. You are doing a good work which will help many to see that they cannot conscientiously ignore the claims of Socialism ;—how can they if they are only honest to themselves? You will know that we are now right in the midst of a battle and what little time I have I shall not neglect to do ray little bit for a cause that stands 'most for peace amongst nations' . . . I hope you are well and at peace with the world—which is a vain hope if you are to do any good in it." I thank him most cordially for his letter. There are more kicks than ha'pence bestowed upon social reformers, now-a-days as in the bad old days. If one studied one's own comfort and peace of mind, one would resolve resolutely to go with the party which could offer the easiest personal comforts with the least possible labour. But then, fortunately, we are not all built that way. And so long as there is so much misery in the world, so many wrongs, so much ignor- ance and intolerance, so much selfishness, so much sin, it is the duty of every man to do what he can ; and by exposing the errors and evils of the present arrangement of society to help to transform man into a wise, virtuous and happy being. "Ring out the want, the care, the sin, The faithless coldness of the times; Ring out the feud of rich and poor, Ring in redress to all mankind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MACHINERY AND THE MAN</head>
        <p>MACHINERY AND THE MAN</p>
        <p>Sir, —The South African millionaires whose names are so distinctively all-British have again distinguished themselves. Cheap human labour, in the Asiatic form, having become unworkable, South African magnates now turn to the old and highly successful method of displacing the white man by the introduction of machinery and labour saving devices. They seek to dispense with the services of one white man out of every three formerly employed on the Rand, and, by casting him out to struggle among the other unemployed they hope to secure the labour of the two men retained at a cheaper rate, in addition to increasing their productive power. Hence one reason for the miners' strike in our latest possession. The old antagonism of interests receives a passing emphasis. Machinery in the hands of the employing class may lighten labour, but seldom does, for such machinery is easily turned into a means for displacing labour. It is useless to inveigh against invention of labout saving devices, and their subsequent introduction in the practical, soulless form of machinery. It is in the nature of things as they are, part and parcel of our industrial development, and one may just as well seek to sweep back the Atlantic with a broom as to try to stem the advance of machines designed to save labour 1 and increase production. But the evil is not in the tools, not in the things themselves, but in the increased power thereby given to the capitalist over the wage- earner. The capitalist calls to his aid the weak and casual workers, the women and the children, who take the places and reduce the wages of the men. A work- man in the Midlands says: " I have eleven children, but most of them are girls, thank God. The girls can get work, but the boys are difficult to place." Jo the American Steel Industry the tendency is to reduce by every possible means the number of highly skilled men employed, and to e,itablish the general wage on the basis of common unskilled labour.</p>
        <p>trom both sides of the Atlantic the lesson is persistent. It is high time the community as a whole recognised that its interests are not best served by class ownership and control for profit making. We workers want inventions and machinery to be used for the lessening of human toil, for the abolition of degrading manual work, for the provision of greater leisure. We seek an ordered,rehsoned liberty of mind, body and estate. It is not too much to ask that the race shall secure for the race the benefits of the know- ledge and wisdom and skill accumulated by the race. -Tours truly,</p>
        <p>WANG LO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MUNICIPALIZATION OF THE DRINK TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPALIZATION OF THE DRINK TRADE.</p>
        <p>The temperance fanatics who are so consistently intemperate in speech and action assume, in most Of their diatribes, that strong drink is an unmixed evil, alcohol an invention of the Devil and every public Athletic Successes. house an ante-room to Hades. Now we find that, almost without exception every At the Manchester A.C. sports at Fallowfied, on age and every clime has had its intoxicating beverages, Saturday, C. Berry, of Warrington, was second in the most of the world's greatest men have indulged to a , quarter-mile N.C.U. (Manchester Centre) Champion- greater ora lesser degree in stimulants, many of our ship to W. H. Webb. At the St.Helens Recreation C.C. sweetest singers have paid tribute to and sung the sports, N. Stevens, of Earlestown, won the half mile praises of Bacchus and without a doubt the great bicycle handicap from the 40 yards mark. At Rain- nations of the earth are all heavy drinkers. ; hill C.C. Sports, J. Stewart, the Warrington harrier, i Now n the face of all these facts is it not reasonable was second in both the half-mile and mile flat races, to conclude that there is something in a man which while J. Bramhall, Warrington A.C., was third in the demands a stimulant and is it not foolish to talk of half mile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ST. HELENS SENSATION.</head>
        <p>A ST. HELENS SENSATION.</p>
        <p>measures of a prohibitive character? To say that the ! moderate use of intoxicating beverages is wicked and : foolish is to say that the majority of the people on the earth are either wicked or foolish. Of course, this A Schoolboy's Head Covered with Ringworm. ' may be true. Perhaps the majority of people are both I Zam-Buk Banishes All Trace of Disease. i wicked and foolish and it is only the total abstaining minority wlio are moral and wise. As, however, it is Anxious parents who live in fear of their little ones catching that dread disease, ringworm, should read how I obviously foolish to attempt to legislate in advance of Zam-Buk effectually cured a schoolboy whose head was : public opinion, to talk of local veto or any other form one mass of sores. of prohibition, or to attempt to place on the Statute Mrs. Kay, of 79, Rigby Street, St. Helens, Lancs., Book any measure of a prohibitive character is unques- told a representative of the " St. Helens Free Press tionably the quintessence of folly. I that her grandson, Joseph Wetton, 12 years of age, We admit and deplore the evils of over-indulgence commenced to suffer from ringworm three months ago. but the only sensible remedy so far propounded is the "The disease broke out all over his head," she said. "I was so much alarmed that I took the boy to a Socialist one of Municipalization. 1 medical man, who tried several remedies, without Here are some facts to be considered. , effect. For one thing the doctor painted his head, and The public house is the workingman's club ;it is the said he could care him in a month. However, he got only place we have where he can (=rather with his no better, and just before the month was up I took him, fellows, blow his bacca, conjure up the spirit of con- at the suggestion of a friend, to see a lady doctor, as it vivialitv and enjoy himself. The average man likes was said that she would cure him much more quickly. a stimulant and does not feel happy in his moments The lady doctor gave me some white stuff which I was of relaxation if it is denied him. to use after rubbing the lad's head night and morning , with soft soap. I followed her instructions, but instead Largely owing to the greed for private profit and of my grandson getting better he became worse. the opposition of the Temperance party the evolution ! "In fact, his head was a mass of inflamed sores, and of the public house has been arrested and it remains really horrible to look at. It was at this stage that a squalid drinking den. Zam-Buk came under my notice. After I had used the Socialists and sensible men, the words are almost first box of Zam-Buk there were signs that the boy synonymous, agree that we must deal with men as would get better, and by the time we had used the third they are and cater for their tastes in a suitable manner. box he was really cured,though we used two more boxes oaftetrhtehatLotimuarllne surdeis.eaNseowittohubt bgmhaslniltvae since In Warrington to-day there are a number of squalid public houses and a few decent ones, in all the incen- recommended g Zaign-Buk to many people for skin tive to drink is great, the incentive to do anything diseases." else is nil. i</p>
        <p>Under municipalization very few establishments would be needed ; one in every district would be sufficient with a number of off-licence places for the supply of supper beer, etc. The public-houses proper could be handsome and commodious. Every kind of temperance beverage could be on sale ; whilst intoxi- cants would be of the purest description and conse- quently considerably less baneful in their effects than most of the present chemical beer and patent still spirit.</p>
        <p>Every public-house could be a miniature temple of art, decorated with pictures, sculpture and a wealth of flowers. Out of the profits music of a high character could be provided, and many forms of entertainment procured. This would be the sort of place to which a man could take his wife and children, a place where it would be impossible to get drunk ; whilst as there can be no doubt it would be profitable, the community would benefit.</p>
        <p>Next week t hope to enlarge upon this idea. Will the 1.0.G.T. join the fray!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>STANLEY LORD.</head>
        <p>STANLEY LORD.</p>
        <p>"PARTY CRITICISM."</p>
        <p>Sir,— The contents of last week's issue of the " Examiner" would be unusually interesting to all earnest, broad-minded political students and incidentally a little amusing. The President of the Warrington Liberal Associa- tion stated that being in Plymouth on the Wednesday previous he was "casting about for some place to spend the evening" and being somewhat fond of a row " he " selected the Socialist gathering" and 'found a place as near the platform as possible." Then follows an indignant protest against some keen, pointed criticisms of the Liberal party contained in a speech delivered at that meeting by the editor of "Justice" Mr. Harry Quelch,and says Mr. Parker "1 question if exaggeration and over-statement ever permanently advanceany cause and the belittling of the motives and efforts of those in all parties other than one's own is not a very worthy and wise method of political warfare." _ _</p>
        <p>Thus Mr. Parker is apparently, in his estimation, guilty of "unworthy and unwise political warfare," for in addition to his inference that Socialist assemblies were as a rule of a rowdy nature, he definitely asserted that this particular meeting had " been specially called to unmask the Liberal party." _ . _ _</p>
        <p>In another cOlumn I notice in a short article 'entitled "The Factory Acts" the writer, Mr. J. Howes, states that "the Tories are still claiming that they are the friends of the workers because they passed the Factory Acts, the Ten Hours' Bill, &amp;c. Like most other Tory claims they are utterly devoid of truth," says this ardent party politician. This quotation, Sir, is typical of the bitter, petty, spiteful partisanship underlying the whole .essay:</p>
        <p>Why do not these high-ininded public moralists cast out the beam that is in the political eye of their own party before attempting to cast out the mote that is in the eye of their political opponents, be they either Tory or Socialist? If Mr. Parker really believes that the" belittling of the motives and efforts of those in parties other than one's own, is an unworthy and unwise method of political warfare"- he should utilise his persuasive eloquence on the personality of his fellow Liberal Mr. Howes, to whom he might also present a largely printed copy of his (Mr. Parker's) self-made political axiom. "I question if exaggeration and over-statement ever permanently advance ANY cause."—Yours &amp;c., DEMOS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LAND SETTLEMENT IN RHODESIA.</head>
        <p>LAND SETTLEMENT IN RHODESIA.</p>
        <p>An exceptional opportunity now presents itself in Rhodesia for settlers. Land may be obtained from the British South African Company with option of purchase by instalments, subject to occupation. The agricultural and pastoral possibilities of Rhodesia are very great, and there are already over a thousand farmers earning a good living and increasing in wealth every year. One farmer who, ten years ago had only a span of cattle and his food supplied to liirn by the British South Africa Company, could to-day sell out for over £lO,OOO. Almost without exception the Rhodesian farmer succeeds, and there is every reason to believe that the right class of men from England would not only do well in this pros- perous colony, but would reap a rich reward as the result of their labour. The staple crop is maize, which gives the greatest yield of any cereal grown. The sell- ing price varies from 12s. to 20s. a 2001 b sack, and the cost of production is only about 4s. a sack. With cost of hauling to market added, it is evident that the profit is considerable. Several farmers annually sell from 7,000 to 10,000 bushels. Tobacco is another most remunerative crop, and tobacco growing is now an established local industry. Cattle-ranching will undoubtedly be the chief industry of the country in the future. The climate is good, and the country is wonder- fully free from disease. Men with energy and ability who have practical experience of farm work, are offered advantages in Rhodesia second to no other country in the world. Farms may be rented calculated at five per cent. on the purchase price, which is exceedingly low, but in order to assist settlers, the first year's rent is deferred, and is payable any time within the first five years of occupation. Central farms have been estab- lished where prospective settlers may acquire know- ledge and valuable experience for 12 months before entering upon their own farms. Board and lodging is given free in return for services. Reduced sea passages are available, and special redections have been made by the Railway Companies in the interests of farmers going to Rhodesia. The Secretary, British South Africa Company, 2, London Wall Buildings, London, E.C., will be pleased to give full particulars and advice to pros- pective settlers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PEEBLES HOTEL HYDROPATHIC.</head>
        <p>PEEBLES HOTEL HYDROPATHIC.</p>
        <p>The new building which was opened on March 22nd, has already been visited by a large number of visitors from all parts of the kingdom, and the seasoa which is opening up promises to be a most brilliant one. The new building has been erected on the site of the old, about half-a-mile east of the town, and set high on the , north bank of the river Tweed. Designed by Mr. J. I Miller, A.R.S.A., Glasgow, it is in the Georgian style, land is not less imposing and palatial in character than ; the one it has replaced. It has many features of interest. It has all the stateliness, solidity, and compactness of I design associated with this style of architecture ; its several parts have been admirably grouped, its details are picturesque, and with cream-coloured rough-cast walls, woodwork in pure white, and red orange tiles on its high-pitched roof, it presents a pleasing and effective contrast to the greens of the hills and woods amid which it is set. In the large and well-lighted basement are situated the baths which have always found their admirers at the Peebles Hydro. There are all the advantages of a Continental Bad, plus the comforts and luxury of a First-class Modern Hotel. The hydro is heated throughout by low-pressure steam pipes,a system which gives a continuous uniform temperature. The cost of the new building with furnishings and baths,etc. is nearly £lOO,OOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CROWNING THE ROSE QUEEN.</head>
        <p>CROWNING THE ROSE QUEEN.</p>
        <p>Pretty Spectacle at Padgate.</p>
        <p>' There was an exceedingly pretty spectacle at Padgate on Tuesday afternoon whoa the children at the Cottage Homes gave a Rose Qudlhn entertainment which afforded a great deal of pleasure to the large number of visitors who witnessed it. The event had been eagerly looked forward to by the youngsters, who had. been admirably trained to play their parts in the revels, and to crown all, brilliant sunshine—only too rare nowadays—favoured the rejoicings. The large playing field attached to the schools was the scene of the festival which opened with a procession presenting many features of interest. It was headed by the school band discoursing lively music, and next came a troup of Morris dancers who tripped gaily along and looked thoroughly happy at their work—or should it be play? A bewildering variety of characters in all manner of dainty costumes passed before the spectators in quick succession. There were garland girls carrying choice flowers, a merry troupe of pierrots, pleasing representations of the seasons and of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales. •A stately Britannia, a robust John Bull with any amount of self confidence, and characters familiar in nursery rhymes, made up a charming picture. Nor was the humorous side lacking, for a couple of clowns indulged in laughable antics, in which they were rivalled by diminutive niggers. Mary Ellen Thompson—the Lancashire lass—was there with "her box, and the old woman in a shoe was busy keeping her large family in order. Towards the end of the pageant came the Rose Queen (Teresa Longshaw, who had been selected by the votes of the children as the most popular girl in the school), beautifully dressed and mounted on horse-back. She was attended by train bearers, maids of honour, and guard of honour. The Lord Chamberlain (William Losh, the most popular boy) and his attendants brought up the rear. The Queen was escorted to the throne and crowned with due pomp and ceremony by the Lord Chamberlain, her youthful subjects cheering lustily. Then the revels began. Twenty-four boys and girls danced the May Pole dance, without which none of these festivals would be complete. It was a little unfortun- ate that two of the ribbons went sailing high into the air, with the result that the plaiting soon became complicated. However, this was only a minor hitch and the dance was very pretty and delighted the onlookers. Then the whole of the scholars, grouped on a platform,sang a May song and a"sweet step dance" was gracefully executed by girls. The national songs "The Ash Grove," "Auld Lang Syne" "The Shamrock" "Red, White and blue" and God bless the Prince of Wales "proved a popular feature and were given in a highly creditable manner. There were several charming dances—the morris danc3 by sixteen boys, the lancers by eight little boys and girls, the veleta dance, skating dance, and garland dance by girls, and a pierrot dance. Perhaps the most pleasing item was the lancers, splendidly gone through by tiny children. The entertainment throughout was most enjoyable and too much praise cannot be given to Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Wright (superintendent and matron) and the members of the staff for the excellent train- ing of the children. All the dresses had been made by the officials and scholars. The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs.Smethurst) were interested spectators of the revels, and many mem- bers and ex-members of the Board of Guardians and other invited guests attended. At the close of the enter- tainment, the presentation of prizes for good conduct and efficiency was maee by the Mayoress. Before doing this the Mayoress presented the Rose Queen with an Empire Day brooch, while the Mayor presented her with his portrait. William Losh was given an Empire Day medal and a portrait of his Worship. The other recipients of prizes (which should have ' been presented at Christmas) were :</p>
        <p>GIRLS :—Teresa Longshaw, most popular girl, workbox ; Lizzie Morrell, proficiency in readings Standards V. and VI., umbrella; Marie Matthews, pro- ficiency in writing, Standards V. and VI., workbox; Kate Shaw, proficiency in arithmetic, Standards V. aiad VI., umbrella; Ethel Appleton, proficiency in reading, Standard IV., book, "Little Folk" ; Emily Morrell, proficiency in writing, Standard IV., book, " Nister's Holiday Annual" ; Jane Jeffreys, proficiency in reading, Standard MI book, "Cosy, Corner Pictures" ; Clara Savage, proficiency in arith- metic, Standard 111.. "My Own Story Book " ; Jane Weston, proficiency in arithmetic, Standard II.," Arm Chair Stories " ; Elsie Phillips, proficiency in writing, ' Standard I.' toy tea service •, Sarah Taylor, proficiency in reading, Standard 1., book, "Nursery Stories" ; Lily Losh, school monitress, work box; Norah Jackson, best darner in No. 3 Home, umbrella; Emma Jasker, best kitchen girl in No. 3 Home, umbrella ; Betsy Taylor, neatest girl in No. 3 Home' umbrella ; Annie Bibbey, best darner in No. 4 Home, book, " Leading strings " ; Alice M'Mahon, best kitchen girl in No. 4 Home' book, " Sunday "; Maggie Morrell. neatest girl in No. 4Home, book, "My very best book" ; Alice Poole, Matron's girl, workbox ; Ellen Chappell, Matron's girl, umbrella. Boys.—William Losh, most popular boy, hook, "Little Folk" ; John Thynne, proficiency in arith- metic, Standard IV., book, "Favourite Stories " ; William Kelly, proficiency in writing, Standard TEL, book," Violet Douglas " ; James Gresty, proficiency in reading, Standard IT., book, "My very own picture book"; Charles Scruton, proficiency in writing, Standard 11., book, " Fatliftil Friends " ; Edward Hitchinson, proficiency in arithmetic, Standard 1., . book, " My book of true stories" ; James Kelly, school monitor, writing desk ; George Lawton, best worker in No. 1 Home, writing desk ; Ernest Bentley, best tailor boy. writing desk ; Thomas Ward, best kitchen boy No. 1 home, book, " Chatterbox " ; Arthur Western, best worker in No. 2 Home, book, "Road, rail, and sea " ; James Taylor, best shoemaker, book, "Chatterbox " ; John Norton, best kitchen boy, No. 2 Home, book, "The Dog Crusoe " ; Alfred Mills best painter. book, " Sunday " • Simon Glynn, best farm boy, book, "Martin Rattler."</p>
        <p>The Mayoress was presented by Marie Matthews, one of the school children, with a very fine artificial bouquet of roses, which had been made by herself.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. George Mather (chairman of the Cottage Homes Committee) a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Mayor and Mayoress. In responding the Mayor said he wished those fathers and mothers could have been present who objected to their children being sent to such an institution as the one at Padgate, as life here was better than tramping about the streets at night selling newspapers and matches, when children should be in bed. (Hear, hear.) Here also the children were trained in the fear of God and were taught to love each other as brothers and sisters. Very great praise was due to Mr. and Mrs. Wright for the great trouble they had been put to in training the children in such an admirable manner ; their work that afternoon was characterised by much cleverness. He wished the children every success in their future life. (Applause.) The proceedings concluded by the band playing "God save the King." Tea was afterwards served to a large number of ladies and gentlemen in the schoolroom.</p>
        <p>'CERT. I hear a splendid concert in my garden every day, When the breezes find by grove and lawn some instrument to play; • They shake the shiny laurel with the clatter of the "bones," And from the lofty sycamore draw deeper 'cello tones; And giving thus the signal that the concert should begin, The brook beside the pebbled path strikes up its mandoline. Then all the garden wakes to sound, for not a bird is mute: The robin pipes the piccolo, the blackbird plays the flute; While high upon a cedar-top, a thrush with bubbling throat, Lifts up to this accompaniment her clear</p>
        <p>soprano note. Then by-and-by there softly sounds, beside some flowering tree, The oboe of the dancing gnat, the cornet of the bee; Such tiny notes—,and yet with ease their cadence I can trace, While overhead some passing rook puts in his noisy bass, Or from a green and shady copse, a daisied field away, I hear the jarring discords of a magpie and -</p>
        <p>a jay. The wind conduots the orchestra, and .as 'e beats the time, The flood of music sinks and swells in melody sublime; Till, when the darkness deepens and the sun sets in the west, They all put up their instruments, and settle down to rest; And when I seek my slumber, like the daisy or the bird. My rest is all the better for the concert I have heard. EMILY STOCKS.</p>
        <p>THE PDX AND THE CROW. (i)</p>
        <p>A crow, having taken a piece of cheese out of a cottage window, flew up with it into a high tree in order to eat it. A young fox, who saw this, determined to get the cheese for him- self, and this is how he set about it. Though young, he was a sly rogue, and knew many pore tricks than he ought to have. done. "My dear, sweet miss," said he; "what a hame it is of people to say such lies about you. They say you are as black as coal; but now I see your feathers are as white as snow. They must be blind! Then, what a beautiful shape you have! I have never seen a bird more beautiful. I am sure that you must have a musical voice. Do sing to me." The foolish crow heerd all this nonsense with delight, and though she knew that the flattery was false, she tried to believe it true; at least, she thought that the fox was charmed with her, and believed all he said. So, to please him, and to hear more of his delicious flattery, the foolish bird opened her mouth to- sing. Before she could utter even "Caw,- the fox had gained his purpose, for as scion as she opened her mouth, out dropped the cheese, which the fox at once snapped up and ran away with, laughing heartily at the silly crow, who had been so easily gulled by his flattery. Moral.—Beware of flattery.</p>
        <p>IN THE ABSENCE OF A TIP</p>
        <p>"Excuse me, madam, would you mind walk- ing the other way, and not passing the horse?" said an English cabman, with exaggerated politeness to the fat lady who had just paid a minimum fare, with no fee. "Why?" she inquired.</p>
        <p>" Becituse if 'e sees wot 'e's been carrying for a shilling 'e'll 'ave a fit," was the freezing answer. FRED W.</p>
        <p>...--.-........e..................</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—/ have not sent up for two or three weeks, but I have found a nice story, called, THE YOUNG KNIGHT ERRANT. .</p>
        <p>On a cold morning in early spring a little man stood at the corner of a London street 'sell- ing newspapers. He was thinly cladNand trot- ting up and down trying to keep warm. But his voice was so hoarse from cold that passers-by could hardly hear him. Some boys jeered and laughed at him, but one, about thirteen years old, after looking at him for a few moments, walked up, and said, "I will shout for you." The man thought the boy was making fun of him, but the boy began to call out. " ' Tele- graph 1" Chronicle!" News " in a clear voice, which attracted so many customers, that in a little while the man had sold nearly all his stock. He offered to pay his young partner, but the boy would take nothing, and went off with a smiling face, happy at having done a good deed to one who needed it. Hoping to see this in print, Enainai Hays.</p>
        <p>PROOF POSITIVE.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Superintendent: "Who led the children of Israel into Canaan? Will one of the smaller boys answer?" No reply. Superintendent (somewhat sternly): "Can no one tell? Little boy on that seat next to the aisle, Who led the children of Israel into Canaan ?"</p>
        <p>Little Boy (badly frightened): "It wasn't me. I-I just moved yere last week fm Ipswich."</p>
        <p>THE NAUGHTY KITTENS. "Look at old Puss," the Kittens said; " He's fast asleep; he nods his head. How dull and stupid it must be To be as slow and old as he! He lies and sleeps there in the sun, And does not try to play or run. Creep up and give him just a pat— He ought to run, he gets so fat-1" But Puss awoke. " Hullo!" said he; "You think to play your tricks on me? I/know I'm old, I'm glad I'm fat— My dear, kind mistress sees to that. I scare the birds while lying here; They dare not come when I am fleas To steal my mistress's nice fruit— My time to some good use is put.</p>
