      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>IPo My</head>
        <p>IPo My</p>
        <p>This week, my fair nieces and gentle nephews, I have succeeded in tearing myself away from the noise and bustle of the Printing Office, and am resting in a nice little sand- hollow, dug out by chubby hands, on an East Coast beach.oWith the calm blue sea in front, and the deep blue sky above, I feel that life is really worth living, after all, and after I have finished writing this letter I have a jolly good mind to enjoy a yes, you're all right— paddle is the word. If I do, I will tell you how I enjoyed it when I write again. I expect soon I shall get a lot of little missives, telling cme of happy hours spent on the sea-shore. Lots more pigs have come to Uncle's Piggery —such a funny lot again, real puzzlets some of them—square pigst round pigs, tall pigs, short pigs; and the prize pig is sent in by Alice Ebury, and the usual six Consolation Mirrors have been awarded to six unsuccessful contri- butors. Next week the two prizes will be given for the best two cards sent in with the funniest drawn Donkeys, and I hope the same interest will be shown as in the Pig Competition.— Yours, until next week„</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>Emmie Emms, Ruth Barnes, Johnny Slade, Dick Rudd, Alfred Watson, Dorothy Mills, Bartol Schofield.</p>
        <p>REPLIES TO CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>Tom E. J.—You must excuse my carelessness in overlooking your application for a bottle of Mason's. It has been sent off now, and I hope you are enjoying it. BOBBY SMITH.—GIad you like the little Tor- toiseshell Mirror you received. Trust you _ will be more successful next time.</p>
        <p>JACE SCOTT.—No, Jack ;,not having the pleasure of a personal acquaintance, I cannot say whether little Prince Eddie has played marbles or not. I should suggest you write, asking him.</p>
        <p>WHEN THIEVES FALL OUT. That tempting painb-box, there it lay; And when the puppies went to play, Of course they saw it. With a shout, They set to work to turn it out. The mischief that they might have done! But, as they gambolled in their fun, And threw the things about pell-mell, To quarrelling the puppies fell. Then two a certain brush espied; They wanted nothing else beside. They tugged and pulled with might and main. But not an inch could either gain. But then a third joined in the fight, He had at least an equal right; But such a noise their snarling made, Their mischief they themselves betrayed. ARTHUR FEARN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PRIDE COMES BEFORE A FALL</head>
        <p>PRIDE COMES BEFORE A FALL</p>
        <p>Going along a lonely road, at dusk, were two men, mounted on horses. One man was poor, ragged, and humble; the other, worth thousands of pounds. The latter said, "Look Amy horse, how stately it is. Your horse is a shaggy old thing, and is nothing in comparison with mine; and look at my suit, made of the finest cloth, and has not a single patch in it, while yours is ragged and torn." He then laughed, and so the other man hung his head, ashamed Hiding in the bushes by the road was a band of robbers, preparing to attack the two lonely travellers. When they saw the fine, stately horse of the rich man, and the old, shaggy horse of the other, they resolved only to rob the rich man, and let the other travel on, be- cause they knew they could get nothing from him. The chief of the robbers sprang out of his hiding-place, and ran straight for the wealthy man. He pulled him off his horse, and ordered him to put up his hands. This he would not do, so the robber struck at him with his fist, but the other traveller, who had dismounted, warded off the blow. The rich man then said, "Get away, I don't want help from tramps." So the other traveller rode away, leaving the man to the mercy of the robbers, who killed him. Moral.—Do not de- spise others lower than yourself. _ _</p>
        <p>TO-M EDMUND JESSOP.</p>
        <p>• 1 • HER PRIVILEGE PRIVILEGE TO CHOOSE. I "I would like something in oil for my dining- room," said the richly-dressed owner of a new house. ,</p>
        <p>"Yee, madam," replied the obliging salesman, "our variety is unlimited—will you have a painting, or a box of sardines?"</p>
        <p>Berlin, Ontario, Canada, May 14th, 1907.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l think you will be sur- prised at receiving a letter from Canada; but I would like to become a member of your "Corner." I like to read the other children's letters. Father has been a reader of the paper ever since it was first issued. I was born in England, but came out here when I was three years old, with my father, mother, and brother. I have now two sisters. If you will accept me as a member, will you please let me know, the rules? I will now close, remaining, yours sincereV, BARTOL SCHOFIELD. THE MOULD OF FORM.</p>
        <p>An old Englishwoman of exceeding stoutness was making efforts; not immediately successful, to enter the rear door of an omnibus. The boy on the box leaned down and cried, genially: "Try sideways, mother, try sideways !" To which the old woman responded: "Lord bless ye, John, I ain't got no sideways!"</p>
        <p>There is an echo at Killarney which, when you call out, "How are you, Paddy Blake I" replies "Very well, thank you, sir." -</p>
