      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor aa a guarantee of good faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M. P. The Work of the Government —An Optimistic Estimate.</head>
        <p>Letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M. P. The Work of the Government —An Optimistic Estimate.</p>
        <p>Sir.—One hears it said, and sees it statsd not infrequently by critics of the Government, that we are "doing nothing," and so perhaps with your kind permission it will not be entirely out of place if I take this opportunity to reply to the allegation. And first of all, it is not for a Liberal Government to grumble when reforms, improvements and national progress are demanded. Apathy would be fatal. Energetic pressure on the contrary, helps us on and reminds us how great is the task that is .vet to be accomplished. And I am sure that your readers who wish for reform and substan- tial progress will recognise the absolute necessity for keeping in power for many a year to come a strong Government to continue the good work we have in 'land. I say continue, because, in spite of the House of Lords, it is a very great mistake to imagine, as some people seem to do, that we have been "doing nothing." I need not remind your readers of the work of last Session, because it was generally admitted that our harvest of legislation was the richest on record, even after deducting the Education Bill and the Plural Voting Bill which were wrecked by the Lords. I will confine my remarks in this letter therefore, to the work of the present year, which alone has been made the subject of criticism. What then may we expect to accomplish in 9O7?1 IMPERIAL MATTERS.</p>
        <p>I will begin with Imperial matters. To the Transvaal and the Orange River Colony we have granted free constitutions; and in this connection one cannot but reflect that our Colonies owe their political autonomy to the efforts of Liberalism in the 19th century. This year, therefore, we have completed the great work of Colonial enfranchisement, which was begun under a Liberal Government when Lord Durharn,in the early forties, obtained the grant of a constitution for Canada. Liberals may perhaps be forgiven if they feel a deep satisfaction in the completioa of this work—a record of statesmanship which has no previous parallel in history, and which is in such happy contrast to the old bureaucratic spirit of ascendancy, for which some people in high places still plainl^anifest a craving, which, fortunately for the welfare of onr Empire %vat never be satisfied. These grants of self-government to the Transvaal and the Orange River Colony are surely amongst the most inspiring events in recent history. and, to Liberals at any rate, it is also satisfactory to know that the white workmen of the Rand agree with the workmen of Lancashire and of Britain in detesting the Rand system of Chinese labour. Within the next week or two coolie repatriation will commence ; not as an arbitrary act imposed by the Colonial Office, but by the full desire of the Colonists themselves. lam glad to say that we are guaranteeing a loan of five millions to General Botha's Government, which will thus be placed in a position of independence. And I must say, after all the experience this country has had of the machinations of the Rand-lords and their con- sequences—matters which I have fully dealt with in other letters which you were good enough to print—l do think it important that this new Colony should not be at the mercy of men, many of whom are neither of English nationality or sentiment, and who are men who have used their influence and power without scruple against the interests of both the Transvaal and the Empire. With these facts fresh in our memory we may well be on our guard. The loan will cost our own tax- payers nothing, but it will obviate any risk of powerful people on the Rand driving our new Colony into bank- ruptcy, as a means of forcing upon the White popula- tion the continued evil of the Yellow Labour Colony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>INDIAN AFFAIRS.</head>
        <p>INDIAN AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>Next let me say a-Wo-r'cr or two about India. In this direction also, I submit, we have reason to be proud of our work. During the last ten years our splendid dependency has suffered from the black cloud.of plague, which has swept away in that period no fewer than five and a quarter millions of people. Even this figure is dwarfed by the awful ravages of plague in that vast penin- sula in the days before the country secured the blessing of firm, orderly British Government. Still, the loss of five and a quarter million lives by plague, amounting to a death-rate of 3 per 1,000 of the whole population of the country (but of course to a far higher death-rate in certain localities) is a sad and serious fact, and one cannot wonder there has been some discontent, in a measure caused by this. Mr. Morley has wisely sanctioned the strong measures taken in India against certain enthusiasts who have allowed themselves to spread what is undoubtedly sedition among the troops, especially among the Sikhs. But, as one would expect from him, he is doing more than this. In order that we may know what is in the mind, and as farm possible at the back of the mind of the people of India them- selves, he is endeavouring to bring us into close contact with them. Two responsible native statesmen folarSfetiEggatcti mAxto,,,bn 1typ...0 int ed .to t.bewSecre.t sly be a larger representation of Indians on the Viceroy's Council at Calcutta, and on the Provincial Councils throughout the peninsula. Moreover, a Council of native Indian notables is to be summoned for the pur- pose of advising the Government of India on all matters of legislation. And further a Royal Commission is to examine into the work of the Government. To pick holes? Far from it. The work done by our fellow I countrymen in India is assuredly an objebt for legiti- mate admiration and pride to us all, and we can but. hope to strengthen and further improve it. Well, these changes will be another landmark in the history of our Eastern possessions. They forward the work of Lord Ripon, who was Viceroy many years ago, and we may hope they will counter any discontent that may have arisen amongst the Eastern people, owing perhaps to their desire as Asiastics to emulate the successes of Japan. I have a good deal to try and compress into this necessarily lengthy letter, and must not further extend it by even trying to summarise the extremely important speech which Mr. Morley delivered the other day in the House. I will only further allude to two points in it of great importance. While probably your readers are pretty well agreed that when all is said and done, and all deductions made, democratic government is beyond question the best form of government for educated populations of white people in the West, it is utterly unsuited to that great population, that aggrega- tion of races whom we rule in India. Mr. Morley in his great speech laid strong emphasis on the fact that strong personal government is the only possible form of government there now, and in the future too; and so in local government, while natives will be associated in the work, a strong official majority is of course abso- lutely necessary. The second point to which I am referring is the fact that while the great mass of the people in India are uneducated, there is still a small educated minority, and the administrative acts I have just quoted show that i as in fact Mr. Morley told us,he s far from forgetful of this minority and its legitimate claims to consideration. It is very satisfactory to note that the Secretary of State for India has the entire support of Lord Minto, the Viceroy, who is not a Liberal in politics, but who was appointed by the late Conservative Government in succession to Lord Curzon. In Imperial matters then 1907 may be regarded as one of the most important and encouraging years in the history of our race.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>PEACE AND THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>PEACE AND THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>Now let us look a little nearer home, and may I say a word or two about the much discussed Hague Confer- ence. Though we do not expect it to bring us the Millenium, Liberals do hope, yes, and we have a firm belief that these Conferences will in the long run, as surely as day follows night help us towards the goal of better things. Meantime, though we decline to let the military spirit, and what has been called the" beggar- mv-neighbour " game of armaments, carry us too far, we are no more unmindful of the defence of our country and Empire than other people,and Mr. Haldane as all the world knows, is making a great effort in order that our Army may be managed on sound efficient lines During the long time our political opponents were in office they certainly failed to reform the War Office, in. spite of attempts of three successive Secretaries of State. Mr. Haldane's Army Bill may be modified in some particulars before it becomes law, but it is certainly the greatest step in advance since the days of Mr. Cardwell in the seventies. And by the way it. is an interesting co-incidence—or some may think it a signi- ficant fact—,that Mr. Cardwell, the last great military reformer, was also a Liberal. I just mention that fact in passing for the benefit of friends of mine, but politicsl opponents, who are a little inclined at times to represent oar party as careless of both Army and Navy. They mean well lam sure, but are mistaken. Mr. Haldane hopes to simplify our Army organisa- tion, and to make each part of the Army complete in itself—thoroughly furnished with the necessary horses and artillery. He is first of all, giving us a highly trained professional Army, able to undertake service in any part of the world ; and secondly, a Territorial Army for home defence, which will as far as possible consolidate our auxiliary forces. The details of this great scheme are beyond the scope of my letter, but the work now nearly done surely stands out as a good example of the loss theatrical side of democratic effort. REFORMS IN THE COMMONS. Turning to the House of Commons, its reform has sndeed been only less important than that of the House of Lords itself—'-or rather I should say, of the relation between the two Houses. The latter is a question on which I hope to have the pleasure of writing you at no distant date. But as regards the House of 'Commons one has heard, up and down the country, plenty of talk for many a year past about the waste of time here at Westminster. Well, this year we have taken strong measures to lessen the evil. We have increased the slumber of Standing Committees, and a great deal of the immense mass of detailed work that has to be tackled by the Commons has been referred to them. Then we are parcelling out the programme of the year: deciding how many days should be spent on this subject and upon that subject, and I hope we shall find it Possible to carry forward legislation from one year to another, so that the time spent on.inFornpleted Bills -Will not be lost. Outside the House it is not easy to</p>
        <p>appreciate the enormous importance of these changes, which will have a great effect on the whole course of our political development.</p>
        <p>THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>I‘lay I in conclusion refer for a moment to the Budget. It is really one of a series, and marks the beginning of far-reaching financial developments. Let us consider briefly what Mr. Asquith has accomplished in his eighteen months of office: First, he has resolutely stopped borrowing ; which was a vicious habit into which the Tories tell.</p>
        <p>Second, he has already paid off debt at an unprece- dented rate pir year. liven so it will be the work of a generation (as I heard a member on our side of the House ruefully explaining during Mr. Asquith's speech) to pay off the war debt which the Conservative Party left on our hands. It will alas be many years before we are again back in the same strong financial position we occupied when the Nvar broke out. If we had avoided that we should have been able to spend all these years in still further reducing the national debt. But NTr. Asquith has faced tha situation, and made a right godcl start. He has also abolished the coal duty, reduced the tea duty, and differentiated the income tax—reducing the income tax on earned incomes by 3d. in the t— further graduated the death duties, and completely re-organised the Tory system of doles to local authori- ties. That, I think, is something to be going on with. Is it what our Conservative friends call "doing nothing ?"—Yours etc.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Examinee, Saturday June 15, 1907,</head>
        <p>The Examinee, Saturday June 15, 1907,</p>
        <p>bodily wants while engaged in this great and momentous undertaking, but sent them forth without purse or scrip, as He Himself had gone before. And filled with the same spirit of love far perishing men which animated their beloved Master they made no levies for their services' but laboured with their own hands for daily bread. But if they had no endowments ,no salaries,nor even collections, they were richly gifted with super- natural powers as testimonials that they were indeed the ordained servants of Christ.</p>
        <p>And in the third place, the New Testament, which embraces almost the whole of the first century of the Christian era, mentions only one collection! But it was not for Church building, preachers' wages, nor even for missions, but was raised for the relief of poor brethren in Jerusalem in a time of famine.</p>
        <p>In the face of all these facts, which cannot be gain- said, one feels ashamed of the methods that are adopted by the people called Christians for the maintenance of the various sects which pose as churches of Christ in this country. The cry for money is perpetual and universal, and develops itself in numerous ways. The land is tithed for the support of "the clergy " ; waits in churches and chapels are farmed out ; bazaars,raffies, and rummage sales are every-day occurrences; and collections even at prayer meetings are universal and without end.ol course, it will be said that all these things are necessary for the maintenance of the Christian Church. I should be extremely sorry to think that the Church founded by the life, sufferings, and cruel death of Jesus of Nazareth should be, in any way, dependent upon filthy lucre. It is the Church of Mammon which requires these earthly crutches, and that the institutions which now pose as churches of Christ are really developments of that grasping power is evident from some striking facts to which f would respectfully call the reader's attention. In the first place, the ministry of the present day is igiot a divine but a human institution. This is painfully evident from the babel of tongues which prevails among the ministers. Men who can preach various doctrines and formulate different practices in the name of Christianity cannot hold their commission from God, who is not the author of confusion but of unity. Then the modern preacher is absolutely destitute of any testimonials from God, establishing his claim to be his appointed messenger. His qualifications are a little book learning, and the title " Reverend " (one of God's attributes), acquired at the school or college of his sect. And it is a fact well known to everybody that he is a mercenary, and follows the occupation of preaching and ministering for his hire, just as the lawyer and the accountant do for their fees. So long as salaries and honours are attached to our pulpits, so long will hire- lings be found competing for them. But take away the honours and emoluments, and the modern apostles would soon become extinct.—Yours truly, June, 1907.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
        <p>wo..th dressing. They are too oily to have any value, and Mr. Seal therefore roams the waters unmolested and swallows many fish which would be of more use to man. There is a rock uncovered at low water, which is a favourite haunt of the seal tribe. You may find a score of them there at any time when the tide is Out and only when you get within about 80 yards will they proceed to shuffle down to the water. Then for a few minutes the waves will be dotted with retriever-like heads, all presenting a very fair target for the man with the gun. Next time we shall take a mitrailleuse or a pom-pom to do deadly execution among the seals. Sam's little boat is a capital sailer and the ran out to the lighthouse on the bar, or to Green island, is one we can recommend. From the water you get the best view possible of the mountain panorama. Altogether, the visitor will find Greenore and its environs not only healthful but charming, if he is content for the nonce to dispense with the accompaniments of so many English seaside resorts —the cork-tanned nigger and the giddy pierrot. There is a freshness about Greenore which visitors from busy Lancashire will appreciate. The air is whisper it humbly—equal to that of Blackpool,though there could not be imagined a greater contrast than between the two places. If you are a golfer, you will find in Greenore the place you have been long looking for. If you are not, ydu may possibly become one all at once, but in any case you will enjoy y our visit to Carlingford Lough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FIRST GARDEN CITY.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST GARDEN CITY.</p>
        <p>A Year's Progress at Letchworth.</p>
        <p>[BY CHARLES E. PARKER.]</p>
        <p>It is a year since I visited Letchworth. Great strides have been made. Three thousand people have been added to the population. Full success, which a year ago appeared still doubtful, is now assured. Even from the first there was little doubt that the City would draw to it a considerable number of those possessed of a modest competency who desired to live amid conditions of health, beauty and progress, but there was a doubt if the First Garden City would attract manufacturers with their work-people. This doubt is now removed, and at least six fair sized factories are already erected. There is now no fear that Letch- worth will be a backwater for the gentlemen and gentlewomen to find homes out of the main current of the nation's activity, and it will now take its place amongst the manufacturing cities of England and become a working model of what our industrial towns ought to be. The building of these factories has naturally given a great stimulus to the Co-operative Tenants Company in the building of houses. As we hope to form a similar company in c ionection with the Garden Suburbs Company in Warrington I spent some time in examining the houses. This Company is now erecting about a hundred. I was glad to notice that some mistakes in those first built are not being repeated. We are fortunate in Warring- ton in being able to profit by the experience gained by the Companies that have been at work for some years. ,One mistake in the first houses is the sacrifice of comfort by the long sloping roofs necessitating dormer-windows, and bedrooms completely spoilt by these sloping roofs. One kind woman showed me over her house. In, the principal bedroom after the bed had been fixed in the only possible place, there was only a narrow space by the door in which a small wardrobe could stand against the wall ' • the wash-stand about three feet six high had to stand out six inches from the wall because of the aloing.roof. J suppose our friends the architects will codimnu mat • style of building, bat I am disposea to question whether any style is really beautiful which is not constrained and governed by utility. In the smaller houses with the parlour and kitchen in one, a mistake has been made by putting ranges in, which while good for cooking do not warm the room or give to it that homeliness which is so essential in the principal living room. A fire grate or a range which can be made, if need be, to radiate its heat into the room, is specially ' necessary in these smaller houses in many of which the stairway leads out of the kitchen and carries off the heat. These are details, but they are all important, and should be taken note of in any collective house building we attempt at Warrington. nave plans of what appeared t me the best style of houses which are now being erected. Some of the features of these ought to be useful in Warrington. What we ought to aim at is to get the co-operation of those who are going to live in the houses. It will be folly ..to say This is our architect's idea of a beautiful and com- modious cottage, and if it is not what you like, it is what you ought to like." Doubtless there is a great , need for education in regard to what our homes ought to be, but any violent deviation from style and arrangement of the house, things that have grown out of the habits of life and convenience of ; working, should be viewed with distrust. I do not mean to convey that the monotony of the interiors and exteriors of our cottage houses ought to be repeated in our Warrington garden suburbs, but the way in which the interior convenience has been sacrificed to exterior effect in the first houses in the first Garden City conveys a warning to .which we would do well to give heed. The social activity of the dwellers in Letchworth malls for notice. There are already ever forty distinct Societies or Associations. They touch almost every phase of thought and conduct. No doubt many would consider that some of the adherents of these organizations are extremists and cranks. Doubtless some of them are, particularly those who forsake the beaten track and cut a new way for themselves and. their associates. What is called an Open Air School is in course of erection. It will cost when complete between twenty and thirty thousand potinds, and is built by a benevolent lady who believes that much physical deterioration (apart from lung diseases), is duo to breathing impure air in confined spaces. The successful treatment of tuberculosis by the open air cure has removed the question out of the region generally occupied by facts —that is as far as this disease is concerned. It is reasonable to believe that what is curative is also preventive, and so the open air cult is in great evidence at Letchworth. Every bedroom window appeared to be open, most of them wide open through the night. In summer time many of the residents sleep on the balconies under the verandahs in camp beds or hammocks. Without doubt it would greatly conduce to the health and vitality of those who live in Warrington if more fresh air were allowed to circulate through the houses, especially in the bedrooms. But with us there is the fatal disadvantage that the air is often laden with smoke which would deposit dirt on everything in the room as ()Very household in the town too well knows.</p>
        <p>If we are in secure the full vitality which comes with the freest circulation of air, we must find and adopt measures to do away with the smoke' or failing that to do what we can to find homes for the workers away from the smoke in garden suburbs where sun and air can do their health giving and healing work. June 11th, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>WHITE CITY.</head>
        <p>THE WHITE CITY.</p>
        <p>In the New Testament, which is regarded as the Christian's "Guide Book" to eternal life, there is , neither precept no precedent for such calls for money. / Mirth and merriment reign supreme at the White Salvation is the free gift of God, and can neither be ; City at Old Trafford. Everything has been brought bought nor sold, and the men and the methods by which I well up to time with all the latest amusements and it is to be proclaimed are clearly set forth and illus- I attractions, in fact it is "Fun and Refinement " with trated in the teaching and example of the Lord and His all the roughness left out. The entrance fee is placed immediate followers, at a nominal sum, and for this the visitors get more In the first place, Jesus Christ, the author and founder i than the right to promenade amongst lovely lights and of Christianity, was so poor that he could say to a i flowery walks. Many of the Side Shows, such as Hale's would-be disciple, "The foxes have holes, and the birds I Tours of the World, Fighting the Flames, Mystic of the air have nests, but the Son of Man bath not, where Rivers, Gravity Railway, Dragon Slide, Skating Rink, to lay His head." He went about preaching the "Gospel 1 Water Chute, Fun Factory, and others too numerous to of the Kingdom" (a very different Gospel from the one mention, furnish amusement for all at a slight extra now heard in the pulpits), healing the sick, and feeding , cost. All the members of a family can find much to the hungry, without money and without price. No ; amuse them at" The City of Pleasure." collection was ever made by Him, or upon His account. Hale's Tours of the World have aroused great And His simple,sel [-denying life of poverty was recorded enthusiasm not alone in the White City, Manchester, for the example and guidance of all who should stand ' London, Blackpool, Dublin, but everywhere where cars up to minister in His name. have been located. The Press and public have .Again, His chosen apostles were poor men in the encouraged them. They are distinctive and dignified. working ranks of life, and His commission to them was, It is another step toward realism which is bound to be "Go ye into all the world and preach the Gospel to pre-eminent, and promises to be one triumphal march ever 3 creature." But he made no provision for their along the pathway of sincerity and success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>GREENORE.</head>
        <p>GREENORE.</p>
        <p>A TRIP TO ONE OF IRELAND'S BEAUTY SPOTS.</p>
        <p>The. general public must be familiar with the name " Greenore " even if they are somewhat hazy in their notions as to where Greenore is. So assiduous has the London and North-Western Railway Company been in its advertising that the handsome coloured pictorial posters of Greenore and district must have caught the eye of most people at one time or another. Now Greenore is in Ireland, on the shores of the beautiful Carlingford Lough, and to be still more precise only a short run across from Holyhead. The tourist can leave Warrington or any other part of Lancashire in the evening and breakfast comfortably at Greenore the following morning. The railway company does not guarantee a smooth passage, but it provides all the comforts of a first-class steamboat service and the voyageur can have nothing to grumble at in crossing the Irish sea but the elements (perchance) and himself. 1.30 a.m. is the somewhat unearthly hour at which the steamer for Greenore usually leaves Holyhead, but as passengers can go aboard at 930 they need not worry about the "small hours." Their convenience is carefully studied, for on arrival they are not obliged to turn out at 6 a m., but can either breakfast on board or enter the hotel at their own time. The steamer, the railway station, aud the Hotel form a sort of homogeneous whole at Greenore. Leaving the steamer, one passes up the gangway on to the quay, through the station, and into the fine hotel which is under the management of the L. and N.-W. Railway Company. Here is no strange city, with bewildering streets and a battery of hotels. Like the station and the quay, the hotel and G-reenore are one. Arrived at Greenore y3u are in the Hotel, and vice versa. You could not well stay anywhere else if you wanted, unless you took one of the bunga- lows which adjoin the golf links and harboured your family and yourself there. Even then you would still be the guests of the Railway Company, which, if it does not own Ireland, owns a good deal of what you can see here. We ought to add that the Greenore Hotel is worthy of Dublin itself. It is commodious, up-to-date, well-managed and reasonable.</p>
        <p>Greenore is magnificently situated on a point of land whieh juts out into the blue waters of the Lough. In fact you have water on three sides of you, and whichever way the wind blows yau get the bracing i sea breeze. The view in all directions s entrancing, and to Carlingford Lough, Thackeray's words might well have been applied : Were such a bay lying on English shores it would be a world's wonder, or if on the Mediterranean or Baltic, English travellers would flock to it in hundreds." "We are glad to say that they are already *inning to do so. The bay reminds us very much--looking inland—of the liavvddach estuary at Barmouth and is hardly surpassed by it in beauty. For nine or ten miles the Lough runs between Ireland's emerald shores, and is backed in all direcions by mountains of varying sizes, contour and colour. Right across from Greenore are the Mourne m mntains, which present a picture the eye is npver tired of feast- ing upon. All the various shades between green, gold, and purple seem to be based in clothing these massive peaks with beauty as the sun pursues his glittering path through the heavens. Right at the foot of the mountains is the small village of Green- castle stretching down to the water's edge, and dominated by the ruins of a fine old Norman Castle or Keep, the dugeons of which to-day provide snug stabling for a neighbouring farmer's cattle. On the Greenore side and boldly facing the mountains of Mourne is. Carlingford mountain, a rugged mass nearly 2,000 feet high which keeps watch and ward over the old and interesting little town of Cartingford. To digress a little,the visitor here quickly realises that that which is now merely a fishing hamlet of some six or seven hundred inhabitants, having a small, coin- pact and well-built harbour, was once a place of very considerable importance. Here are fragments of walls, centuries old, and ruins of castles and churches, galore. And, as a matter of historical fact, Carling- ford was the chief town on the north-western frontier of the Pale. It was accordingly a strrngly fortified place, and as recently as two hundred years ago there are said to have been as many as thirty-two castellated buildings and churches in the town. But much farther back legend carries the history of Carlingford. Hero St. Patrick landet when he came to Ireland s in to convert the natives 'L() ottriat;ity,— aA ,hu curious such matters may be interestett to know that, cis the craact;4i)ot where the saint stepped ashore on his glorious mission is now marked by the respectable quay which the modern fishermen use in the most matter,of fact fashion, and with no regard whatever to the momentous incident just recorded. Overlooking the quay is Carlingford Castle, the edifice which about seven centuries ago gave distinction to the town. It is known as the castle of King Job.-a,and in its prime, must have been a place of very consider- able dimensions. It is irregular in form, .by reason of the fact that it is built upon a mass of rock that juts out from the shore. In places the walls are ten or eleven feet thick, while the dungeons are of the god old romantic order, hewn in the solid rock. Carlingford and its Castle were often in the wars in the old days, and the town was burned time and again. Just previous to the battle of the Boyne, followers of the Duke of Berwick attempted its destruction by fire, but failed, and subsequently the Duke of Schomberg was glad of the failure, for it was to Carlingford that he despatched his sick and wounded after the famous victory. From Greenore to Carlingford is a very pleasant walk of three miles by way of the golf links. At the head of the Lough is Warrenpoint, a charming little town with a fine square, a promenade, and sea-water open- air baths. A. mile or two to the right is Rostrevor, the most beautiful , spot on the shores of the Lough. So sheltered is the place that nearly anything will grow there, and in the winter time various kinds of shrubs and plants can be seen in bloom. The mountain behind is richly-wooded, and a contrast to the more exposed and bracing places nearer the entrance to the Lough. Greenore itself is somewhat scant of vegetation, with the single exception of the plantations of pine-trees which shelter the golf-links. Those links are the Mecca nf nine out of ten arrivals at Graenore. They are beautifully situated, and the first tee or the well-appointed Club-house can be reached in two minutes from the hotel. The course possesses the best qualities of seaside links. It is all sandy,and the greens are decidedly good. Hazards are natural, and consist of trees, saad-pits, whine, ponds, a disused railway line, and an old stone house, which in former years was dignified by the name of a powder-magazine. In fact the whole course of eighteen holes is altogether charming, as, from first to last, it possesses interest, difficulty, and variety. Greenore is also within easy reach of the celebrated seaside golf links at Newcastle which can be reached either by rail, or boat to Green- castle and thence by coach along the beautiful County Down coast. Golfers at Greenore Hotel have free use ' of the links, and also ci thv Lough steamer which pliee daily to .Warrenpoint hnd Greencastle.</p>
        <p>Even it golf does not claim all the time of the visitor there is plenty to do at Greenore. Jaunting cars can easily be obtained by telephone from Carling- ford and some very enjoyable excursions arranged. Mr. J. A. Rarnsden, the London and North-Western District Manager at Greenore, is the soul of courtesy, and always pleased to furnish useful information to the 'tourist who is anxious to got about and not well acquainted with the "lie of the land." Carlingford, Omeath, Warrenpoint, Newry, and Dundalk are all within easy access by rail, and the company's steamer brings a number of other places conveniently near. Among out-door amusements at Greenore itself, there are tennis and croquet in addition to the golf, and likewise sea-bathing, sailing and fishing. The last two can best be taken together—like cod-liver oil and emulsion. There is one sailing-boat, one fisherman, and one fish (which we caught a month ago). But that was before the season had opened, and there are doubt- less others by now. If you hunt up Sam McKinley the proud 'possessor of Greenore's one sailing-boat. You will soon learn all about the fishing, and the run of the tide, which is in itself an interesting study. Sam is an honest, genial fellow and makes no pretence that the bay is full of fish. You may catch a couple of dozen, or you may only catch one as we did and think you have got the lot. There seem to be more seals than fish of the ordinary variety. Sara has no love for the seals. Their dog-like heads come bobbing round his boat, and they qaze at him with a sort of contempt born of familiarity. You see, he does not often have his gun! But this is not the whole of their offence. The seals eat the fish and spoil not only the sport but Sam's livelihood to some extent. And as he says, they are "good-for-nothing creatures." The visitor naturally feels at first some- what excited at the thought of seal-hunting. Visions of a seal-skin coat or waist-coat rise before him, only to be dispelled on consulting McKinley. The Carlingford Lough seals possess coats which are not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Purse --White.</p>
        <p>A very pretty wedding was solemnized at St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, on Tueeday, the con- tracting parties being Miss Rosa White, elder daughter of Mr. and Mrs. H.P.C. White, Halton View, Penketh, and Mr. Fredmick Walter Purse, son of Mr. aud Mrs. W. C. Purse, Redland, Bristol, and chief assistant engineer in the Corpora- tion electricity and tramways department. The wedding aroused considerable interest and there was s large attendance at the church to witness the cere- mony which was performed by the Rev. J. R. Jones. The bride, who was given away by her father, looked charming in a dress of white silk, trimmed with embr Adored mousselaine de soie, point lace and orange blossoms, and a veil surmounted by a wreath of myrtle and orange blossom. Her ornaments con- sisted of a gold chain and pendant, the gift of the bridegroom,and she carried a beautiful shower bouquet of lilies of the valley,petunias and gardenias. The brides- maids were Miss Alabel C. White, sister of the bride and Miss Purse,sister of the bridegroom,and they wore dresses of pale blue crystalline, trimmed with embroidered gauzes and lovers' knots of ribbon, and white lace hats trimmed with tuscan, forget-me-nots and DlOB3 roses. Their ornaments were gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom, and they carried bouquets of pink carnations with smilax streamers. The mother of the bride carried a shower bouquet of cream roses and ferns, Mr. J. Teller, tramways manager, was the best man, whilst Mr. J. Gray,assistant water engineer, was the groomsman. Miss Mary Standish was at the organ and contributed a number of suitable selections. The wedding breakfast was held at" Halton View," after which the happy couple left for North Wales for the honeymoon, the bride travelling in a costume of blue cloth and a white hat with crown of white roses and foliage. The following is a list of the presents : Bridegroom to bride, gold neck chain and pendant Bride to bridegroom, gold and ruby scarf pin Bridegroom to bridesmaids, gold brooches Father of bride, upright grand piano Mother of bride, household and table linen and point lace Father and Mother of bridegroom, cheque Miss Mabel White, silver hot water jug and bedspread Miss Purse, silver and ruby glass epergne Mr. W. H. S. Purse, cheque and point lace handkerchief Mr. and Mrs. H. E. Purse (Birmingham), drawing LOOM rug Mr. and Mrs. J. Purse (Houlton), silver jam dish and spade. Mrs Rudge, walnut drawing-room table Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Rudge, satin eider down quilt Masters A. and H. Rudge, copper kettle Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Singleton, walnut coal cabinet Miss Dora Singleton, butter cooler Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Crosby, dinner service Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Walker, tea service Mr. and Mrs. E. Budge, silver and glass combination jam dish Miss Crosby, set of jugs Master Sydnev Ornahr, +.,,ps,c, And hot water jug Tir;vaviai, siiktable cover Miss L. White, silver-backed brush Mr. and Ars. G. F. F. White, silver bread trencher</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. E. A. Wtaite,,case of silver tea spoons Mrs. White (Exeter), embroidered nightdress case Cousins at Benkenham copper afternoon tea kettle Mr. and Mrs..J. Bunch, silver tea-pot Miss Bunch, silver mustard pot Mr. and Mrs. Henderson (Nottingham) silver sugar basin Mr. A. C. R. Wilby (West Africa) silver egg stand Gray, case of silver afternoon tea knives Mrs. I).sarrvhokise (Frodsham), case of silver after- noen tea spoons aacl sugar tbc Miss Wilson, case of fish knives and tsrks Miss Darlington, cut glass and silver flower vasos Mr. and Mrs. T. E. Shoobridge, case of silver pepperettes e Rev. J. R. Jones, Bible Miss Jones, Browning's Poems Mr. J. Telfer, silver cake basket Mrs. Harrison, linen sheet shams Mrs. Bennion, table centre Mr. and Mrs. T. Dunabin, quilted counterpane Miss Payne, fern pot Mr. J. L. Davenport, chinpendale clock Mr. and Mrs. Hyde, tapestry table cloth Mrs. F. V. L. Mathias, silver sugar basin and cream jug Mr. and Mrs. Laine (Ceylon), Indian lace collar Mr. Green, chair Misses Standish, silver backed mirror Madame Ellaine, photo frame Mr. and Ara. A. Ashton, silver photo frame r. Mrs. and Miss A. Owen, silver basket Miss D. and A. Owen, brass pan Mr. S. P. Siiconic, barometer Misses Ditohaeld, silver vases Miss M. B. Joynson, hand-painted velvetlable centre Mr. Chibley, silver and cut glass butter cooler Miss Falconer (Liverpool) embroidered pin cushion and tidy Miss _Forster (Widnes), afternoon ten knives Mr. and Mrs. W: Owen, lobster picks Mr. and Mrs. Treacy, copper plant pot Miss Mottram (Birkenhead), silver butter dish and knife Mrs. Soulsby (Whitby), silver butter knife Miss 'Brown (Whitby), silver jam spoon Miss Pownall, oopper plant pot Mr. A. Bear, table cutlery Miss Houghton copper hot water can Mr. and Mrs. Bancroft (Plumbley), silver shoo horn and button hook Mr. and Mrs. Piroolstencroft, silver photo frame Miss Woolstencroft,silver and cut glass smelling salts bottle Miss E. L. Foster, silver butter knife and jam spoon Rev. and Mrs. C. Harvey Cook, oxidized plant pot Hr. and Mrs. France (Liverpool), cut glass and silver rose bowl Mr 9. Branwood, pair of vases _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>A. H. CROSFIELD. Warrington Infirmary Bazaar and Fete.</head>
        <p>A. H. CROSFIELD. Warrington Infirmary Bazaar and Fete.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Might I be allowed through the medium of your paper to appeal for further donations or articles of any description in aid of the above which takes place on June 19th, 20th, 21st, 22nd. Gifts, however small, would be gladly received on _behalf of the Warrington stall at the Town Hall, or 8 Bewsey rd., not Lt eith Toesday next —Yours truly, JANE SAIETHURST, Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Lion of the Council.</head>
        <p>The Lion of the Council.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l observe from your report of the last monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council that the Councillor who was so neatly silenced by the Local Government Board Inspector recently,after he had been christened the "Lion of the Council" by Alderman Bennett, was again attempting to frighten the Council with his roar. But wh,o can detect anything leonine about his roar ? Does it not remind one more forcibly of the noise which is made by an animal with long ears? Since we have now got a lion in the Council is it not time to have a lion tamer? But joking apart, cannot the ratepayers see from the masquerading which took place at the last meeting what a serious thing it is to have men in the council who are obviously influenced in everything they say and do by personal animosity and prejudice?—men who either cannot or wont discuss 'my- thing on its merits,and who appear to be quite indifferent about public duty and responsibility to ratepayers. Is it a fact, by the way, that the "lion of the council" was never elected by the ratepayers and that he repre- sents no one but himself? If so is it not gross imperti- nence for him to attack men who were duly elected in a straightforward manner on a clear issue? When we remember how the Council has been humbugged and ignored from the commencement of the Lawson Con- tract, can any unbiased ratepayer assert that Councillor Pemberton was not justified in everything he said? If he submits to every mouth-shutting device and expedi- ent which is adopted by the" Keep it dark" party he might as well be a dummy. He was elected for a clear and definite purpose.—Yours &amp;c., INVESTIGATOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Warrington Children's Summer Camp.</head>
        <p>The Warrington Children's Summer Camp.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Manchester Press with a kindliness of spirit which I feel assured you will emulate, ask the public not to forget " the cry of the little ones." You know the natural longing which all youthful creatures, and, especially young children, have at this season for the green fields and the flowers. Will you allow a small space in year valuable columns in order to urge the claims of the Warrington Children's Summer Camp? You, and your readers, will remember with pleasure the happy days of childhood, of budding manhood and womanhood, when you sought the sylvan retreats of woodland glade and flowery lane. Those for whom we plead have no cricket ground, no golf links, no archery, croquet, or lawn-tennis grounds, to bring them welcome relief. The world to them has a very sombre outlook. There are dismal and sordid homes even in "good old Warrington." There are people too poor to give the little ones a fair chance. Five hundred and thirty-six children (boys and girls) were given a week's holiday at the seaside last season. Every effort is now being made to make the Camp of increased usefulness. A Warrington cook and caretaker have been engaged, whom there is every reason to believe will enter upon the work with a desire and determination to make the season a greater success than any proceeding one. Will you help us with your donations to attain this object? Increased effort means increased 07(p431M. but iLLItU Wt, are exceedingly anxious to wipe off last season's debit balance, necessarily incurred to complete the structural improvements and additions, an expense not likely to arise again for some years. The undersigned will gladly acknowledge sub- scriptions and donatlms.</p>
        <p>JOHN A. RANSOME, Hon. Treasurer, Shirley, Appleton. W. H. J. JENKINS, Hon. Secretary, Bank Chambers, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>House of Lords. How it was Got Rid of in 1648.</head>
        <p>The House of Lords. How it was Got Rid of in 1648.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As the Prime Minister is about to bring in a Bill designed to disable the House of Lords, if not to abolish it, it is well to recall the circumstances under which this was done by the Free Churchmen of a former age. It was in the end of the year 1648 that we had in this country a House of Commons that took upon itself to declare that the consent of the House of Lords to one of its measures was unnecessary. It was a curious House of Commons, consisting of 53 members all told, all puritans of the Independent type. They had got the House of Commons all to themselves three weeks before by getting the Army to exclude from the House all members who did not agree with them. The famous Colonel ift,jde was brought down with two regiments of dragoons to guard the door of the Honse and prevent all members from entering who were not in sympathy with the 53. So it appears that the people who took upon themselves to abolish the House of Lords, had really abolished the House of Commons a short time before; for the 53 were only about one-eighth of the proper number of members. Well the work that these nice gentlemen had in hand, and which they considered themselves justified in forcing through in this way was nothing less than that of the trial and execution of the King. The could not get a majority of their own House, Long Parliament though it was, to vote for it, and so they turned, or rather got the army to turn, the majority out of the house. They could not get the House of Lords to assent to tl4e vote, and so they voted that house down as unnecessary. Their next difficulty was a suitable Court for the King's trial; and as they could not rely upon the existing Courts, they formed a Court of Justice of their own—the High Court of Justice known as the New Slaughter House. Of course the trial was a foregone conclusion ; the 53 fanatics took care that the Court should be constituted to give effect to their wishes, the accusers to a large extent being also judges, 100 out of the 150 belonging to the army. _</p>
        <p>How the country was governed in those days may be further gathered from the fact that all these proceed- ings, the " purging" of the House of Lords, the trial and exaction of the King were got through within the brief period of seven weeks. The House was cleared on December 6th, 1648; in less than a month, that is on January 4th following the ordinance for the trial Charles Stuart King of England was read in the House and unanimously agreed to,and within 26 days from the vote, viz., on January 30th the King was executed. The summary methods of these gentlemen, I think, are instructive . and when the safeguards which the Con- stitution provides against all such proceedings are again threatened, it is well to recur to this, the only precedent which our history furnisbes for our guidance in the matter.—l am, &amp;c., _</p>
        <p>E. C. E. CARLETON.</p>
        <p>12th June, 1907.</p>
        <p>Christianity and Money. Sir,—ln the " Examiner " of the Ist instant you reported that the Bishop of Liverpool had made a personal appeal to the people of this town to aid him in raising £lOO,OOO for the spiritual needs of his diocese. As an humble believer in the Free Gospel of the Son of God I desire, with your kind permission, to record my solemn protest against that appeal and every other of a similar import for the following simple and sufficient</p>
        <p>reasons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>wore very pretty dresses of pale blue silk muslin trimmed with cream lace, edged with narrow kiltings of the muslin, pale blue hats and tulle and pale pink roses, and each carried a shower bouquet of la France roses, and wore blue enamel pendent watches (the gift of the bridegroom). The bridegroom was attended by Captain Crosfield, who acted as best man.</p>
        <p>The wedding ceremony was performed by the Rev. H. G. Barnes, M.A., curate of Wraicall and Failand, Somerset (cousin of the bride), assisted by the Rev. F. E. Coryton, vicar of the parish. During the service, which was fully choral, the hymn "0 Perfect Love" was sung kneeling, and the organist, Mr. W. Stone, beautifully rendered the following bridal music: - Processional March by Bird; "Spring Song" (Efollands) ; Bridal March from Lehengrin ; " Benedictione .14u.ptiale " (D'Evry) ; Mendelssohn's Wedding March; and introduction to Act 111. from Lohengrin. The chancel was beautifully decorated with palms and white flowering plants. The bride's mother wore silk striped black voile over white silk, with strappings of silk on skirt and bodice, which was also trimmed with white lace, sequin bonnet, with white tulle and pink roses, and carried a bouquet of pink carnations. Mrs. Kingdon (bridegroom's mother) wore a hand- embroidered black net robe over white silk—bodice trimmed with cream, gold lace and chiffon velvet; white crinoline bonnet with black tulle and ostrich tips. . _ _</p>
        <p>-After the wedding, a reception was held at St. Elmo, the residence of the bride's father. ,</p>
        <p>The happy pair left for London, from whence they will start for a motor tour through Devon and Corn- wall. The bride's travelling dress was of pastel blue taffetas—bodice in Kimono style, sleeves and vest of cream lace, picture hat of pale blue, trimmed with tucked chiffon, and long feather of blue shading to brown, and white ostrich feather boa. The handsome presents amounted to nearly three hundred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>INFIRMARY BAZAAR, Latest Arrangements.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY BAZAAR, Latest Arrangements.</p>
        <p>A great elort on behalf of tlie Infirmary Extension Fund will be made next week, and the liberal support of the townspeople is appealed for. A bazaar and fete by which it is hoped to raise most of the £3,000 still required for this Nvcrthy object, will be held is the picturesque grounds of Hill Cliffs" (by per- mission of Mr. Henry Greenall)oa Wednesday, Thurs- day, Friday and Saturday next. The arrangements are now complete, and the event promises to be a splendid success. The opening ceremonies at 2.30 each afternoon will be performed as follows : Wednesday: The Hon. Ellinor and the Hon. Elizabeth Wilson-Patten ; chairman, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, chairman of the Extension Committee.</p>
        <p>- Thursday: The Hon. Mrs. Willis, of Halsnead •, Chairman, the Rev. El. V. Pigot, HA., chairman of the Infirmary Board of Management. Friday: Mr. William Long, J.P. ; Chairman, Mr. George Dakin, hon. treasurer of the Infirmary. Saturday : Mrs. Langford-Brooke, of Mere ; Chair- man, Mr. Henry Greenall. Presentation of money collected by the pupils of the Secondary school. The following are the presidents of the various stalls :</p>
        <p>Warrington Stall: The Mayoress of Warrington. Stockton Heath Stall: Mrs. Lyon and Mrs. Vere Davies. Latchford Stall: Mrs. Broadbent. Thelwall and Grappenhall Stall: Mrs. Frost and 1 Mrs. Rvlands.</p>
        <p>Lynain Stall: Mrs. Coon. Daresbnry and Moore Stall: White.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Blair and Mrs</p>
        <p>Refreshment Stall and" Hill Cliffe" Stall : Lady Greenall, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mrs. W. P. Rylands, and Miss Litton.</p>
        <p>There will be an exceptionally attractive series of entertainments and side shows. Mrs. Ralph Johnson has arranged coon and variety entertainments which will be held at 3.15 and 7.0 each day and will include selections from "The Blue Mlon " and" Miss Hook of Holland." A comedy in one act " Time is money" will be performed by Captain A. Willoughby-Osborne, Miss J. A. Edelsten, and Miss Sylvia Broadbent at 4.30 and 8.15 daily. Putting competitions and croquet and tennis tournaments for prizes will be held under the superintendence of Mr. W. Peter Rylands. Outdoor amusements galore will be prgyided__nn.dor the_managenit tha. Cyciii.ig Club 'and they include steam roundabout, Alpine railway, and other attractions of the fair-ground.. There will be palmist delineations by Madame Zisva, gipsy 'palmist, Mrs. Kennedy and Senora Gloriana. The following bands will be in attendance ;—Wednesday, Walkers Brewery Band; Thursday, Borough Band; Friday, Messrs. Crosfield's Band; Saturday, Ist. V.B. South Lancashire Regiment Band. There will be dancing each evening on a specially-prepared floor. We cordially recomunend all who wish to spend a pleasant time =aid delightful surroundings and to help a noble cause, to make a point of visiting the bazaar which will certainly be one of the most attractive ever held iz Warrington and district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>The passing of time has worked many changes ILA th. children's festival at Stockton Heath, and not the least marked is the increase in the number of scholars taking part. Walking Day was first instituted in the district some sixty years ago, at the time when there was a mere handful of children in the village, whilst yesterday when the festival was repeated, over one thousand youngsters had to be catered for. The day's programme has not undergone any very substantial alteration in half a century. In the good old days the children were entertained in the vicarage garden, and in addition to being well supplied with toothsome edibles were each given a glass of wine, and the only difference now-a- days is that more commodious quarters are sought, and the wine has given place to tea, which is distributed in copious quantities. The route for the march round the village is the same, the same order is observed,and the same unbounded enthusiasm is displayed. Fortunately for all concerned,the weather yesterday was fine and if it was a little dull it mattered not, for nothing short of a thunderstorm would have produced undue depression of spirits. The village wore its holiday attire. From the business premises and private houses flags and bunting were hung to wave gaily in the breeze, and everyone did honour to the occasion by turning out in the lightest summer attire. The youth of the district was early astir and in the morning there was much dressing of hair and self-adorn- ment with the result that all were looking their best when the time came for them to assemble. The curl papers on Mary Jane's head had given place to a new hat which she wore with conscious pride—pride which was increased as she looked down upon her nice new frock and well shod feet, whilst John was resplendent in all the glory of a spick and span sailor suit and his face shone again as the result of the vigorous appli- cation of soap and water. It had cost something in time and trouble, aye and in the more material cash to bedeck the children for the occasion but the sacrifice counted for nought for was it not the day of the year in the lives of the children ,• and was not every additional effort which would lend to their enjoyment a labour of love; and did not the hard working mother reap her reward when she was able to point to her offspring marching with head erect and joining in the day's revels with the children of those perhaps a little better circumstanced ? Shortly before two o'clock a brief service was held in the Church. There was no advertised time for the pro- cession to start and therefore it could not be late,but a little more promptitude was perhaps to be desired in getting the children en route. As in the past the band of the Blue Coat School took the lead and enlivened the walk round the village by playing spirited marches. The clergy and officers of St. Thomas' Church followed, succeeded by a detachment of the local Company of the , Church Lad s Brigade who looked smart and marched well. The procession itself presented no unusual features. There was the usual die- spoons. play of flags carried by the children whilst Mr. and Mrs. Boon, (Belfast) box of embroidered some also carried baskets of flowers, but the attractive- cambric handerchiefs ness of the proceedings lay chiefly in the smiling, Kindlon—Risk. 0 healthy faces of the youngsters, who entered with whole-hearted enteusiasm into the joys of the day. The A marriage of considerable interest to Warring- infants in particular appeared to enjoy themselves, and toniang was s gemnized at St. John's Church minded not the discomforts of being packed a little Weymouth, on Saturday, the contracting parties being . tightly on gaily-bedecked lurries and traps which had , Miss Katherine Lucy Risk, daughter of William Bond been placed at their disposal. At the end of a some- ! , wat tiring walk the children were rewarded with a ; Risk, Paymaster-in-Chief, R.N., St. Elmo, and Mr. nsubs tantial tea, served for the seniors in the schools. I George Holman Kent Kingdon. MA, of Taddyforde, and for the infants in the Church Institute. With this I Exeter, and Stockton Heath, Warrington. Thethe official programme ended, but not so the pleasures weather was beautifully fine and a large and fashion- of the day. In twos and threes the youngsters later j able congregation assembled to witness the ceremony. in the day proceeded to the Wilderspool Brewery Park, At two o'clock, the bride, leaning on the arm of her where they romped about until dark, and it was not father, entered the east porch, and was preceded up until a late hour that the day's revels were concluded. the aisle by the white-robed clergy and choir, singing "The voice that breathed o'er Edon." The aide Visrr To MANCH EsTER, MONDAY NEXT.--11r. Thomas looked very pretty in a handsome dress of rich ivory Ison Ocularist and Aurist of Ison's Eve and Ear Dis e satin, trimmed with pleated chiffon, pearl embroidery D P nsary Leds, established 1871), will visit the j eansgate hotel,e Thansgate, Afanchester, on Monday, and Honiton lade (the gift of Mrs. Kingdon). I June 17. Hours 11 to 8, and the Old Bull Hotel, She wore a coronet of orange blossom and !Blackburn, on Wednesday, June M. Hours 3.30 to 7, a Brussels lace veil (kindly lent by Mrs. W. and may be consulted on all cases of failing sight and' Johnston), and carried a bouquet of white roses ; hearing. New treatment without operation. Sufferers lilies, orange blossom and myrtle (the gift of should avail themselves of the present opportunity of , I consultation. Spectacles adapted for weak or defective I the bridegroom). She was followed by a !sight. Artificial eyes supplied. Those unable to avail charming bevy of young ladies, who acted themselves of a personal consultation should write to I as bridesmaids—The Misses May, Nellie, Nora ILeeds for printed form of questions to answer ; Muriel and Constance Risk, and Miss Kingdon (sister forwarded free. Letters