      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>LAND SETTLEMENT IN RHODESIA.</head>
        <p>LAND SETTLEMENT IN RHODESIA.</p>
        <p>An exceptional opportunity now presents itself its Rhodesia for settlers. Land may be obtained from the British South African Company with option of purchase by instalments, subject to occupation. The agricultural and pastoral possibilities of Rhodesia are very greats and thGre are already over a thousand farmers earning a good living and increasing in wealth every year. one farmer who, ten years ago' had only a span of cattle and his food supplied to him by the Britith south Africa Company, could to-day sell out for over £lO,OOO. Almost without exception the Rhodesian farmer succeeds, and there is every reason to believe that the right class of men from England would not only do well in this pros- perous colony, but would reap a rich reward as the result of their labour. The staple crop is maize which gives the greatest yield of any cereal grown. The sell- ing price varies from 12s. to 20s. a 2001 b sack, and the cost of production is only about 4s. a sack. With cost of hauling to market added, it is evident that the profit is considerable. Several farmers annually sell frolu 7,000 to 10,000 bushels. Tobacco is another most remunerative crop, and tobacco growing is now an established local industry. Cattle-ranching will undoubtedly he the chief industry of the country in the future. The climate is good, and the country is wonder- fully free from disease. Men with energy and ability who have practical experience of farm work, are offered advantages in Rhodesia second to no other country in the world. Farms may be rented calculated at five per cent. on the purchase price, which is exceedingly low, but in order to assist settlers, the first year's rent is deferred, and is payable any time within the first five years of occupation. Central farms have been estab- lished where prospective settlers may acquire know- ledge and valuable experience for 12 months before entering upon their own farms. Board and lodging ia given free in return for services. Reduced sea passages are available, and special reductions have been made by the Railway Companies in the interests of farmers going to Rhodesia. The Secretary, British South Africa Company, 2, London Wall Buildings, London, E.C., will be pleased to give full particulars and advice to pros- pective settlers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PEEBLES HOTEL HYDROPATHIC.</head>
        <p>PEEBLES HOTEL HYDROPATHIC.</p>
        <p>The new building which was opened on March 22nd, has already been visited by a large number of visitori from all parts of the kingdom, and the season, which is opening up promises to be a most brilliant one. The new building has been erected on the site of the old,, about half-a-mile east of the town, and set high on the north bank of the river Tweed. Designed by Mr. J. A.R.S.A., Glasgow, it is in the Georgian style, and is not less imposing and palatial in character than the one it has replaced, It has many features of interest. It has all the stateliness, solidity, and compactness of design associated with this style of architecture ; its several parts have been admirably grouped, its details are picturesque, and with cream-coloured rough-oast walls, woodwork in pure white, and red orange tiles on its high-pitched roof, it presents a pleasing and effective contrast to the greens of the hills and woods amid which it set. In the large and well-lighted basement are situated the baths which have always found their admirers at the Peebles Hydro. There are all the advantages of a Continental Bad, plus the comforts and luxury of a First-class Modern Hotel. The hydro is heated throughout by low-pressure steam pipes,a system which gives a continuous uniform temperature. The cost of the new building with furnishings and baths,etc. is nearly .£lOO,OOO.</p>
        <p>Marriages, PURSE—WHITE—June 11th, at St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, by the Rev. J. R. Jones, M.A., Frederick Walter Purse to Rosa White. DAN-me—MILLI:R.—June 13th, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. W. Barber, Thomas Winder Davies to Annie Miller.</p>
