        <p>to be brought to the front so that Warrington politicians would know that the workers meant business. Governments would do nothing except by extraordinary pressure. There were millions of workers who could not afford to pay to a trade union ' • they , were almost in a state of starvation. It would be an ' honour to trade unions if they could lead the way in securing pensions. The resolution was carried and Messrs. Smith, Hicken- bottom, Green, Owen and Plinston were appointed delegates. Mr. WAINWRIGHT expressed indignation at the remarks of some of the speakers and left the room. At the request of the Labour Party the Council endorsed a resolution expressing extreme disappoint- ment that old age pensions were not provided for in the Budget and protesting against the relief estimated at £1,250,000 given to people whose incomes ranged up to £4O a week at a time when the Chancellor of the Exchequer stated that he could take none of the taxes off tea and sugar and that he was unable in any other way to assist the working classes. JARROW ELECTION. The Council sent a resolution conveying good wishes to Mr. Curran, the Labour candidate in this election. THE UNEMPLOYED QUESTION. At the request of the Chatham Trades Council the following resolution was passed :—"Seeing that the Un- employed Workmen Act of 1905 has proved so inefficient and unworkablb, consequent on the degrading and insulting inquiries into their private affairs preventing all but a small proportion of genuine unemployed registering themselves[under the Act,and thus preventing an accurate estimate of the amount of unemployment being arrived at,this Council calls on the Government to deal immediately with this most pressing mattsr by placing a workable Act on the Statute Book providing ; funds from the National Exchequer to adequately cope with the distress created by unemployment." It was decided to send copies of the resolution to the Right Hon. John Burns and to the Borough Member. Mr. PODMOREI said Mr. Burns would perhaps be influenced more by resolutions from the branches as he did not appear to care much for Trades Councils and thought they were meddlesome bodies. It was decided that copies of the resolution be sent to the affiliated branches with a request that they would endorse it and forward it to Mr. Burns. THE AMALGAMATION QUESTION. The SECRETARY of the Labour Representation Com- mittee wrote asking if they were to consider that the question of amalgamation of the two bodies was now closed. The CHAIRMAN said only one Society that was affiliated to the Labour Representation Committee was not affiliated to the Trades Council. It was decided to reply that the question of amalgama- tion considered at the recent special conference was now at an end. MR. CROSFIELD,M.P. AND RAILWAY MEASURES. The SECRETARY said he had received the following letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. :—I thank you for bringing under my notice the draft of a Bill "to regu- late the employment of persons as drivers of or assist- an,s on locomotive engines" and so far as I can see,its provisions as at present drafted would certainly receive my support in the House of Commons. Mr. PLINSTON added that he knew nothing about the , matter and if anyone could give him information to enable him to forward the letter to the proper quarter he would be glad. The general opinion was that the letter was intended for the Union of Enginemen and Firemen of which another Mr. Plinstou is secretary and it was agreed to forward it to him. NEW RULES. The new rules prepared by a special committee were submitted and consideration of them was adjourned to , a special meeting to be held next Tuesday evening.</p>
