        <p>MOTORISTS FINED. _ Speed Limit Exceeded on Cheshire Roads. Several motorists made their appearance at the Daresbury Petty Sessions at Stockton Heath on Tues- day, to answer charges of driving cars at excessive speeds. Charles Simpson,Walton Lea,vvas charged with driving a motor car at a speed dangerous to tbe public at Stretton on May Ist. The defendant pleaded not guilty. Acting-Sergeant S. Kennerley said he was stationed at Stretton on the day in question, when he saw the defendant drive na,st ;^ -- DLit:3U OI oo Lailt3S all uuur. toe Car passed the four road ends without slackening and no horn was sounded. John William Harmer. of Warrington said the motor was going at a terrific speed. No horn was sounded. W. H. Purse'', carter, Warrington, estimated the speed at 30 miles. He had never seen a car go so fast before. Defendant said he had never yet driven to the danger of the public, and he was not doing so on the day in question. He was within the legal speed. A fine of 20s. and costs was inflicted. George Bankes, Whelpriggs, Kirby Lonsdale, West- ! morland, was summoned for driving a motor car at the rate of 39 miles an hour between Walton and Preston Brook, on May Bth. On the date in question two con- stables were placed one et Preston Brook and one at ' Walton, with instructions to time all motorists and enter the particulars on a card. Defendant covered the distance at a rate of 39 miles an hour. Mr. Ellis for the defendant said they did not suggest that what the police had said was untrue, and they i must accept their evidence. The defendant was not I driving dangerously or recklessly. A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed. Thomas Louther, 3, Birdcage, Halifax, was charged ! with a similar offence, the speed in this case being 26 miles an hour. Defendant pleaded not guilty, and said it was impossible for the car to go at the rate named. The highest possible speed was 23 miles. The case was dismissed. Holbrook Gaskell, Hill Croft, Woolton, near Liver- pool, was charged with travelling at the rate of 27 miles an hour from Walton to Preston Brook on May 18th. He was represented by Mr. T. W. Grace, Man- chester. In this case also the speed of tbe car was ascertained by the aid of watches and cards. In answer to Mr. Grace, Preston said br§ watch was a good timekeeper. The watch was tested in court and was found to be accurate. Further questioned by Mr.Grace,witness said when the defendant came up in his car it struck him that he was driving in a reasonable manner. lie did not know at the time that the speed was excessive. That was found out afterwards, Superintendent Ennion,expleined that motorists must know when they were being timed by the police as the watches were to be plainly seen in the bends of the eonstables. I was possible to stop the motorists and tell them, but some of them would tell the police to go somewhere. (Laughter.) Mr. Grace for the defence, contended that the police had made a perfectly pardonable but real mistake as to the time. The car which defendant vas driving could not exceed 18 miles an hour. It was an old-fashioned car, four years old and 12-horse power and do what be , liked, the chauffeur could not make the car exceed 18 I miles an hour. Defendant said he bad tested the car over ten miles of road on the Chester highway, and found that it could not go more than 18 miles an hour on the third gear, which was the one he was using at the time of the alleged offence. It was a 12-horse power car, of heavy make, and at the time carried a good deal of luggage. I The Beneh considered the ease proved and fined I defendant 20s. and costs.</p>
